window.VIVA_GEN={"1.1": {"title": "Peripheral nerve injury, groin pain after hernia repair", "vignette": "A 38-year-old male warehouse worker presents with right groin pain that began soon after an open mesh repair of a right inguinal hernia 9 months ago. He describes burning and shooting pain along the scar that spreads into the upper inner thigh and the right side of the scrotum. It is worse when he walks fast, lifts boxes, bends at the hip or wears a tight belt, and it eases when he lies with his hip flexed. Light touch of the clothing on the skin is unpleasant. He had moderate groin pain before the operation. Paracetamol, tramadol and amitriptyline 25 mg (stopped because of sleepiness) have not helped. The surgeon found no recurrence on examination and has suggested \"removing the nerves\" if nothing else works. He is now on light duties.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> pain after inguinal surgery lasting more than 3 months, burning and shooting quality, distribution (scar, upper inner thigh, scrotum), allodynia, worse with hip extension and activity, eased by hip flexion, pre-operative pain (a risk factor). <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> pain on walking and bending (think hip pathology or recurrence), scrotal pain (think genitofemoral nerve or testicular causes), the surgeon's offer of neurectomy (skips the diagnostic block).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> chronic post-herniorrhaphy pain, neuropathic type, from ilioinguinal nerve entrapment or injury (with possible iliohypogastric involvement).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genitofemoral nerve neuralgia (genital branch: scrotum; femoral branch: femoral triangle)</li>\n<li>Iliohypogastric neuralgia (suprapubic and lateral gluteal skin)</li>\n<li>Nociceptive pain: mesh-related inflammation (\"meshoma\"), recurrent hernia, granuloma</li>\n<li>Hip pathology: hip OA, femoroacetabular impingement, labral tear</li>\n<li>Adductor tendinopathy, osteitis pubis (athletic groin pain)</li>\n<li>Upper lumbar radiculopathy (L1, L2)</li>\n<li>Testicular or urological causes (epididymitis, varicocele)</li>\n<li>Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve injury (lateral thigh)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (look for a hernia bulge, cough impulse), then supine with the hip extended, then with the hip flexed.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensory mapping:</strong> Neurotip and light touch (cotton wool) over the T12–L2 territory, mark areas of loss, allodynia and hyperalgesia on the skin with a pen and compare with the left. Map the ilioinguinal (upper inner thigh, root of the penis, anterior scrotum), iliohypogastric (suprapubic, lateral gluteal) and genitofemoral (scrotum, femoral triangle) territories.</li>\n<li>Brush (dynamic allodynia), cold (cold roller or tuning fork), and a 128 Hz tuning fork.</li>\n<li>Tinel's sign: tapping medial and inferior to the ASIS and along the scar.</li>\n<li>Reflexes with a hammer (knee, cremasteric reflex); MRC power of hip flexion and adduction.</li>\n<li>Lumbar spine: range and femoral stretch test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scar inspection and palpation; a hernia check at the deep and superficial rings with a cough impulse.</li>\n<li>Hip: range of motion, FADIR, FABER; adductor squeeze test and palpation of the pubic symphysis.</li>\n<li>Pain with hip extension (stretching the nerve) and relief with flexion.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>map the sensory changes on the skin with a marker. A sensory map is the key physical sign in post-surgical neuropathic pain.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical diagnosis</strong> using neuropathic pain criteria (pain in a neuroanatomical distribution, a relevant lesion, sensory signs in the same territory). Screening questionnaires: DN4 or painDETECT.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> recurrence, mesh complications, seroma; the II and IH nerves near the ASIS; a neuroma near the scar; dynamic scan with Valsalva for a hernia. Compare with the left side.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI pelvis (or CT):</strong> if recurrence, meshoma or other pathology is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Hip X-ray or MRI</strong> if the hip examination is positive; <strong>lumbar MRI</strong> if upper lumbar radiculopathy is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided nerve blocks:</strong> II and IH block near the ASIS first; a separate genitofemoral block if the scrotal pain persists. The block pattern helps plan any neurectomy.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz</strong> probe, small-parts or MSK preset, depth 3–4 cm. Patient supine.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> the inguinal region and scar, deep inguinal ring and the femoral vessels (the inferior epigastric vessels with colour Doppler); the inguinal canal with Valsalva (dynamic hernia check); the mesh (a bright, wavy, shadowing layer).</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the II and IH nerves:</strong> place the probe medial and superior to the ASIS, perpendicular to the inguinal ligament, with its lateral end on the iliac crest. Identify the three muscle layers: external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis. The II and IH nerves lie in the split fascia between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, usually within 1.5 cm of the iliac crest, the II closer to the crest.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> nerve cross-sectional area or diameter, compared with the left side; look for a neuroma (a fusiform swelling) near the scar.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> the deep circumflex iliac artery runs in the same plane as the nerves; use it as a landmark and to avoid it.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> Valsalva or a cough for recurrence; sonopalpation (pressing on the nerve with the probe) reproduces his pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Also beware:</strong> a nerve in the same plane further cephalad and medial is the subcostal nerve; do not block it by mistake.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> ASIS, iliac crest, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves, deep circumflex iliac artery, peritoneum, bowel, inferior epigastric vessels, inguinal canal, mesh.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Explanation and reassurance;</strong> many patients improve over the first 1–2 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuropathic medication (NeuPSIG first line):</strong> a gabapentinoid (pregabalin or gabapentin), duloxetine, or a tricyclic at night (nortriptyline is better tolerated than amitriptyline). Second line: lidocaine 5% plasters over the scar, capsaicin 8% patch. Tramadol as second or third line. Avoid strong opioids long term.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> desensitisation, graded activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Work adaptation</strong> and avoiding tight belts.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological support</strong> (pain coping, sleep).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerve block, between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, near the ASIS:</strong> first with local anaesthetic alone (diagnostic), then with steroid if the diagnosis is confirmed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Landmark techniques place the needle anterior to the ASIS, where the nerve course is highly variable; the classic anatomy is found in only about 42% of people, and blind blocks fail in about 10–40% of cases.</li>\n<li>US-guided blocks reached the correct plane in about 95% of cadaver injections (Eichenberger 2006).</li>\n<li>Later options: pulsed RF of the nerves; cryoneuroablation; peripheral nerve stimulation; surgical triple neurectomy (II, IH and genital branch of the GF nerve) for refractory neuropathic pain, after a positive diagnostic block (HerniaSurge guideline).</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies, anticoagulation.</li>\n<li>Supine; machine on the opposite side; mark the painful area and baseline pain.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear probe medial and superior to the ASIS, lateral edge on the iliac crest, perpendicular to the inguinal ligament; identify the three muscle layers and the nerves between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis; colour Doppler for the deep circumflex iliac artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25G 38 mm (out of plane, Peng's preferred approach) or 22–25G 90 mm in plane from medial to lateral.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the split fascia between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, next to the nerves.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrolocation:</strong> inject 0.5–1 mL saline once the tip passes the internal oblique; the fluid should split the plane and outline the nerves.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 5 mL 0.25% bupivacaine (diagnostic), with methylprednisolone 40 mg added for the therapeutic block.</li>\n<li>Do not go deeper than the transversus abdominis (peritoneum and bowel lie underneath).</li>\n<li>Re-test allodynia, pain on hip extension and the sensory map after 15–20 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Femoral nerve block</strong> from spread along the transversus abdominis plane to the iliacus fascia: temporary quadriceps weakness and fall risk (warn him, check before walking).</li>\n<li><strong>Peritoneal or bowel puncture</strong> if the needle goes too deep; <strong>vascular</strong> puncture (deep circumflex iliac artery), haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrong target:</strong> subcostal nerve block, or failure because the genitofemoral nerve is the main source.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, local anaesthetic toxicity, steroid effects (glucose, flushing, skin atrophy), vasovagal reaction.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> percentage pain relief, change in allodynia and the sensory map (numbness in the II territory confirms the block); quadriceps power before walking.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain diary</strong> for 24 hours (duration of relief compared with the local anaesthetic).</li>\n<li><strong>At 2–6 weeks:</strong> pain scores, neuropathic symptoms, activity and work, sleep, medication use.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> positive block with short relief: pulsed RF or cryoablation, or referral for triple neurectomy; negative block: genitofemoral block, re-imaging for recurrence or meshoma, review the hip and spine.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>No sensory map, or not comparing with the left side.</li>\n<li>Not distinguishing II, IH and GF territories (the scrotal pain may need a GF block).</li>\n<li>Not mentioning the femoral nerve block risk and fall advice.</li>\n<li>Sending the patient to neurectomy without a diagnostic block.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Chan C-W, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided blocks for pelvic pain. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 17, ilioinguinal, iliohypogastric and genitofemoral nerves pp. 167–171.", "Kumar P, Peng P. Ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 6, pp. 75–83.", "Peng PWH, Narouze S. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part I: nonaxial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:458–474.", "Eichenberger U, et al. Ultrasound-guided blocks of the ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerve: accuracy of a selective new technique confirmed by anatomical dissection. <em>Br J Anaesth</em> 2006;97:238–243.", "Aasvang E, Kehlet H. Chronic postoperative pain: the case of inguinal herniorrhaphy. <em>Br J Anaesth</em> 2005;95:69–76.", "HerniaSurge Group. International guidelines for groin hernia management. <em>Hernia</em> 2018;22:1–165.", "Finnerup NB, et al. Pharmacotherapy for neuropathic pain in adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Lancet Neurol</em> 2015;14:162–173."]}, "1.2": {"title": "Peripheral nerve injury, pudendal neuralgia", "vignette": "A 46-year-old man who rides a road bike about 250 km a week presents with an 18-month history of burning pain in the right side of the perineum, spreading to the scrotum and the base of the penis and towards the anus. The pain is worst when he sits, especially on a hard chair or the bike saddle, and it builds through the day. It is much better when he stands, lies down, or sits on a toilet seat. It does not wake him at night. He sometimes feels as if there is a foreign body in the rectum. His urologist treated him for prostatitis with two courses of antibiotics without benefit; urine tests and prostate examination were normal. Pregabalin helped a little but made him dizzy. He has stopped cycling and is struggling at his desk job.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> cyclist (repetitive perineal compression), perineal burning in the pudendal territory (penis, scrotum, perianal), worse sitting, relieved by standing or sitting on a toilet seat, no night waking, foreign body sensation, normal urological work-up. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> the prostatitis label (chronic prostatitis or chronic pelvic pain syndrome), rectal fullness (anorectal causes), no mention of sensory loss (the Nantes criteria expect normal sensory examination).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> pudendal neuralgia from pudendal nerve entrapment (cyclist's syndrome), meeting the essential Nantes criteria: pain in the pudendal territory, mainly when sitting, not waking him at night, no objective sensory loss, and (to be confirmed) relief with a diagnostic pudendal block.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic prostatitis or chronic pelvic pain syndrome (urological)</li>\n<li>Inferior cluneal nerve neuralgia (lower buttock, posterior thigh, lateral perineum)</li>\n<li>Piriformis or obturator internus myofascial pain; levator ani syndrome</li>\n<li>Sacral radiculopathy (S2–S4) or a sacral (Tarlov) cyst; cauda equina lesion</li>\n<li>Coccydynia; ischial bursitis or hamstring origin tendinopathy</li>\n<li>Anorectal causes: fissure, haemorrhoids, abscess, proctalgia fugax</li>\n<li>Pelvic or sacral tumour (red flag if pain wakes him at night or there is weight loss)</li>\n<li>Post-herpetic neuralgia (sacral dermatomes)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (posture, sitting tolerance), then lateral decubitus (Sims position) or prone for the perineal and gluteal examination; supine for the lumbar and hip screen. Explain each step and offer a chaperone.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurotip and cotton wool in the S2–S4 dermatomes and the perineum, comparing right with left; mark allodynia or hyperalgesia. (Objective sensory loss is an exclusion criterion and should raise suspicion of a sacral root or cauda equina lesion.)</li>\n<li>Reflex hammer: knee, ankle; bulbocavernosus and anal wink reflexes (S2–S4).</li>\n<li>128 Hz tuning fork at the feet; MRC power of the legs; straight leg raise.</li>\n<li>Digital rectal examination: tenderness when pressing on the ischial spine (Tinel's sign of the pudendal nerve) and in Alcock's canal; anal tone; prostate; pelvic floor muscle tenderness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar spine and SIJ screen; hip range and FADIR.</li>\n<li>Palpation: coccyx, ischial tuberosity, piriformis and obturator internus (FAIR test), hamstring origin.</li>\n<li>Sitting test: reproduction of the pain when sitting on a firm surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>say \"Nantes criteria\" and list the five essential criteria. Say you would offer a chaperone before the rectal examination.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>No imaging or neurophysiology test confirms pudendal neuralgia;</strong> the diagnosis is clinical, supported by a diagnostic block.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI pelvis and sacrum (and lumbosacral spine):</strong> to exclude tumours, Tarlov cysts, cauda equina lesions and other causes; MR neurography if available.</li>\n<li><strong>Urology review results</strong> (urine culture, PSA, flow studies), already normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Neurophysiology:</strong> pudendal nerve terminal motor latency has poor sensitivity and specificity and is not recommended alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> the pudendal neurovascular bundle at the ischial spine; piriformis and obturator internus; the ischial region.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided pudendal nerve block</strong> with local anaesthetic: one of the essential Nantes criteria.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Curvilinear 2–5 MHz</strong> probe, abdominal or MSK deep preset, depth 6–8 cm. Patient prone with a pillow under the pelvis.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview (three scans, cranial to caudal):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Iliac crest and ilium:</strong> probe transverse over the posterior superior iliac spine region, then moved caudally along the ilium.</li>\n<li><strong>Greater sciatic notch:</strong> the ischium as a curved bright line, gluteus maximus and piriformis above it, the sciatic nerve deep to the piriformis with a branch of the inferior gluteal artery (colour Doppler).</li>\n<li><strong>Ischial spine:</strong> moving caudally, the ischium becomes straighter and longer, the piriformis disappears, the sacrospinous ligament appears as a bright line medial to the ischial spine (unlike bone, it casts no shadow), and the internal pudendal artery appears.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> the internal pudendal artery and the pudendal nerve (usually medial to the artery) in the interligamentous plane between the sacrospinous ligament (deep) and the sacrotuberous ligament (superficial, under the gluteus maximus).</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth from skin to the sacrospinous ligament for needle planning; compare the anatomy with the left side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour Doppler identifies the internal pudendal artery, the landmark for the nerve, and the inferior gluteal artery near the sciatic nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> internal and external hip rotation (knee flexed) to see the piriformis and obturator internus move; gentle probe pressure (sonopalpation) over the ischial spine to reproduce his pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> ilium, ischium and ischial spine, sacrospinous ligament, sacrotuberous ligament, gluteus maximus, piriformis, sciatic nerve, inferior gluteal artery, internal pudendal artery, pudendal nerve, obturator internus, ischial tuberosity.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Stop or modify cycling:</strong> a cut-out or noseless saddle, correct saddle height and tilt, padded shorts; a cushion with a perineal cut-out (\"donut\" or U-shaped) for sitting at work; a standing desk.</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic floor physiotherapy:</strong> down-training and relaxation of an overactive pelvic floor, myofascial release, neural mobilisation.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuropathic medication:</strong> amitriptyline or nortriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin or pregabalin (start low, titrate slowly given his dizziness); topical lidocaine.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological support:</strong> pain education, CBT, sleep and sexual health.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided pudendal nerve block at the ischial spine</strong> in the interligamentous plane: local anaesthetic alone (diagnostic), then local anaesthetic with corticosteroid (therapeutic), up to three times.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>US and fluoroscopic pudendal blocks gave similar pain relief in an RCT, and US shows the artery and nerve and avoids radiation (Bellingham 2012).</li>\n<li>Blocks at the ischial spine act on the most common entrapment site; an Alcock's canal (transgluteal or CT-guided) block may be added if the first block does not help.</li>\n<li>Later options: pulsed radiofrequency of the pudendal nerve; botulinum toxin to the obturator internus or pelvic floor if muscle spasm contributes; surgical pudendal nerve decompression for patients with good but short relief from blocks; neuromodulation (sacral or pudendal).</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies and anticoagulation; explain the expected numbness of the perineum and the pain diary.</li>\n<li>Prone with a pillow under the pelvis; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Curvilinear probe transverse over the ischial spine; identify the sacrospinous ligament, the sacrotuberous ligament and the internal pudendal artery with colour Doppler.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 90 mm (3.5 inch) spinal needle, in plane from medial to lateral, entering about 2 cm from the probe edge.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the interligamentous plane, next to the internal pudendal artery (the nerve is usually just medial to it), deep to the sacrotuberous ligament and superficial to the sacrospinous ligament.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrolocation:</strong> once the tip passes the sacrotuberous ligament, inject 0.5–1 mL saline and watch it spread between the two ligaments. Aspirate before injection (the artery is close).</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 4 mL 0.25% bupivacaine for the diagnostic block; with 1 mL methylprednisolone 40 mg for the therapeutic block.</li>\n<li>Stay medial to the tip of the ischial spine: the sciatic nerve lies on its lateral side.</li>\n<li>Check perineal numbness 15–20 minutes later and ask him to sit on a firm chair.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Sciatic nerve block</strong> from lateral spread: temporary leg numbness and weakness (fall risk; he should not drive or ride home).</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> internal pudendal artery puncture, intravascular injection, haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Temporary loss of function:</strong> perineal numbness; transient urinary or faecal incontinence or difficulty voiding; reduced sexual sensation.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, local anaesthetic toxicity, steroid effects, vasovagal reaction, increased pain; rectal puncture is very unlikely with the posterior approach.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> perineal numbness (confirms the block reached the nerve), relief when sitting on a firm chair, leg strength and sensation (sciatic spread), ability to void.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain diary</strong> for 24 hours, with sitting tolerance in minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic value:</strong> at least 50% relief of sitting pain during the local anaesthetic effect supports the diagnosis (Nantes essential criterion).</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain, sitting tolerance, return to work and cycling, sexual and bladder function, medication use.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> repeat therapeutic blocks up to about three times; if relief is good but short, pulsed RF or referral for decompression surgery; if negative, reconsider inferior cluneal neuralgia, pelvic floor myofascial pain and other causes.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not naming the Nantes criteria, especially \"no objective sensory loss\" and \"relief with a diagnostic block\".</li>\n<li>Not using colour Doppler to find the internal pudendal artery as the landmark.</li>\n<li>Going lateral to the ischial spine (sciatic nerve).</li>\n<li>Forgetting the chaperone and the rectal examination over the ischial spine.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Chan C-W, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided blocks for pelvic pain. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 17, pudendal neuralgia pp. 174–176.", "Bellingham GA, Peng P. Pudendal and inferior cluneal nerve. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 9, pp. 109–119.", "Peng PWH, Narouze S. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part I: nonaxial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:458–474.", "Labat JJ, et al. Diagnostic criteria for pudendal neuralgia by pudendal nerve entrapment (Nantes criteria). <em>Neurourol Urodyn</em> 2008;27:306–310.", "Bellingham GA, Bhatia A, Chan CW, Peng PW. Randomized controlled trial comparing pudendal nerve block under ultrasound and fluoroscopic guidance. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2012;37:262–266."]}, "1.3": {"title": "Peripheral nerve injury, CRPS of the hand", "vignette": "A 58-year-old female hairdresser fell on her outstretched left hand 10 weeks ago and fractured her distal radius, which was treated in a cast for 6 weeks. Since the cast came off, the hand has been swollen, red and warm, and she has a constant burning pain in the whole hand that is \"out of proportion\" to the injury. Even the touch of a sleeve or a breeze on the skin hurts. She says the hand sweats more than the other and the nails seem to grow faster. The fingers are stiff, and she cannot make a fist or hold her scissors. She is frightened to move the hand and keeps it close to her body. She has also noticed tingling in the thumb and index finger at night. Paracetamol, codeine and ibuprofen have not helped.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> trauma and immobilisation (cast), pain disproportionate to the injury, allodynia, oedema, colour and temperature change, sweating change, trophic change (nails), motor dysfunction and stiffness, fear-avoidance and guarding. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> thumb and index tingling at night (carpal tunnel syndrome after the wrist fracture, which can trigger or coexist with CRPS; a nerve lesion would make it CRPS type II), the swelling and warmth (infection, deep vein thrombosis, or a fracture complication).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> CRPS type I of the left hand (no major nerve lesion yet proven), early warm phase, after a distal radius fracture and cast immobilisation. She meets the <strong>Budapest criteria</strong>: continuing pain disproportionate to the event, symptoms in all four categories (sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor/oedema, motor/trophic), signs in at least two, and no other diagnosis that explains it better.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>CRPS type II, if a nerve injury is confirmed (median nerve, carpal tunnel syndrome after the fracture)</li>\n<li>Acute carpal tunnel syndrome after distal radius fracture</li>\n<li>Infection (cellulitis, septic arthritis, osteomyelitis); deep vein thrombosis of the arm</li>\n<li>Fracture complications: malunion, non-union, ulnar-sided wrist injury (TFCC), tendon rupture (EPL)</li>\n<li>Inflammatory or crystal arthritis</li>\n<li>Disuse with stiffness and oedema without CRPS</li>\n<li>Thoracic outlet syndrome, cervical radiculopathy</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, both arms exposed and supported, with the unaffected hand examined first for comparison. Explain each step; she may be frightened of touch.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> swelling, colour (red or blue), skin shine, sweating, hair and nail growth, posture of the hand (guarding).</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Allodynia:</strong> brush or cotton wool stroke (dynamic mechanical allodynia), compared with the right.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperalgesia:</strong> Neurotip (pinprick).</li>\n<li><strong>Thermal:</strong> cold and warm test tubes or a cold roller.</li>\n<li><strong>Vibration:</strong> 128 Hz tuning fork; <strong>deep pressure</strong> over the joints.</li>\n<li><strong>Median nerve:</strong> Tinel's sign at the carpal tunnel, Phalen's test, two-point discrimination (for CRPS II or carpal tunnel syndrome).</li>\n<li><strong>Motor:</strong> MRC power (often limited by pain), tremor, dystonia, neglect-like symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temperature difference</strong> with an infrared thermometer: more than 1°C between the hands supports CRPS.</li>\n<li><strong>Oedema:</strong> hand volumetry or circumference of the fingers and wrist, compared with the right.</li>\n<li><strong>Range of motion</strong> of wrist and fingers with a goniometer; grip with a dynamometer (if tolerated).</li>\n<li>Elbow and shoulder range (shoulder–hand syndrome), cervical spine screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>name the four Budapest categories and show signs in each with tools (brush, pin, thermometer, tape measure). The examiners want the criteria named and applied.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>CRPS is a clinical diagnosis</strong> (Budapest criteria); tests exclude other causes or support the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray of the wrist:</strong> fracture healing, alignment; patchy osteopenia may appear after weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Three-phase bone scintigraphy:</strong> increased uptake in the delayed phase in periarticular areas (supportive, mainly in the first year).</li>\n<li><strong>Nerve conduction studies:</strong> to detect median nerve injury or carpal tunnel syndrome (CRPS II).</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> FBC, CRP, ESR (infection, inflammatory arthritis).</li>\n<li><strong>Doppler ultrasound</strong> of arm veins if DVT is possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Skin temperature</strong> and sweat testing (thermography, QSART) where available.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome measures:</strong> CRPS Severity Score, pain NRS, QuickDASH, fear of movement (Tampa Scale), mood.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong>, small-parts preset, depth 3–4 cm. Patient supine, head slightly extended and turned a little away from the side being scanned.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> transverse scan of the anterior neck: trachea, thyroid, carotid artery and internal jugular vein; move down to the level of C6 and C7.</p>\n<p><strong>Level identification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C6:</strong> prominent anterior tubercle (Chassaignac's tubercle) and posterior tubercle; C6 root between them.</li>\n<li><strong>C7:</strong> only a posterior tubercle; the vertebral artery lies anterior to the transverse process before it enters the C6 foramen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Focus at C6:</strong> the <strong>longus colli</strong> muscle on the anterior surface of the transverse process, covered by the <strong>prevertebral fascia</strong>; the cervical sympathetic trunk lies on or just inside the fascia over the longus colli, behind the carotid sheath.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> longus colli thickness and the depth to the prevertebral fascia; compare the anatomy with the right side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler (essential):</strong> colour Doppler for the carotid artery, internal jugular vein, <strong>inferior thyroid artery</strong> (often crossing the needle path at C6), <strong>vertebral artery</strong> and the ascending cervical artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> ask her to swallow: the <strong>oesophagus</strong> (usually on the left, medial, at C6–C7) moves and shows its concentric layers. This is the structure to avoid on the left side.</p>\n<p><strong>Also scan the wrist</strong> (carpal tunnel): median nerve cross-sectional area compared with the right, for the tingling.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> trachea, thyroid, oesophagus, carotid artery, internal jugular vein, vagus nerve, anterior and posterior tubercles of C6, C6 root, longus colli and longus capitis, prevertebral fascia, inferior thyroid artery, vertebral artery, sternocleidomastoid.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation is the core of treatment:</strong> early hand therapy with physiotherapy and occupational therapy; graded motor imagery (laterality training, imagined movements, mirror therapy); desensitisation; oedema control; graded exposure to movement and function.</li>\n<li><strong>Education:</strong> explain CRPS, the good prognosis with early treatment, and the importance of using the hand.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> NSAIDs; a short course of oral prednisolone in the early inflammatory phase; bisphosphonates (for example intravenous neridronate or pamidronate, or oral alendronate) in early CRPS; neuropathic agents (gabapentinoid, amitriptyline or nortriptyline); avoid long-term opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychology:</strong> fear-avoidance, anxiety and mood support.</li>\n<li><strong>Treat carpal tunnel syndrome</strong> if confirmed (splint, decompression if needed).</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention:</strong> vitamin C after wrist fractures has been suggested to reduce CRPS risk (mixed evidence).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided left cervical sympathetic (stellate ganglion) block at C6</strong> (local anaesthetic deposited beneath the prevertebral fascia over the longus colli), as a series alongside intensive hand therapy, to allow her to rehabilitate.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The fluoroscopic \"blind\" anterior paratracheal approach risks vertebral artery, inferior thyroid artery and oesophageal puncture; US shows these structures and lets a small volume reach the target (Gofeld 2009; Narouze 2014 review).</li>\n<li>Evidence for sympathetic blocks in CRPS is limited: a Cochrane review found little high-quality evidence of benefit (O'Connell 2016). They are used to separate sympathetically maintained from independent pain and to give a window for rehabilitation.</li>\n<li>Later options: dorsal root ganglion or spinal cord stimulation for persistent CRPS (evidence for SCS in CRPS I); sympathetic RF or neurolysis rarely.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent (Horner syndrome, hoarseness, arm numbness, rare serious complications); IV access, monitoring; resuscitation equipment.</li>\n<li>Supine, neck slightly extended, head turned slightly away; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Baseline skin temperature of both hands (thermometer) and pain score.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear probe transverse at C6; identify the anterior tubercle, longus colli, prevertebral fascia, carotid artery, internal jugular vein; colour Doppler for the inferior thyroid and vertebral arteries; swallow to show the oesophagus.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22–25G 50 mm, in plane from lateral to medial (behind the carotid sheath, avoiding the C6 root and the oesophagus), or a short-axis approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> beneath the prevertebral fascia on the surface of the longus colli (subfascial), not into the muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrodissection</strong> with saline first to confirm the plane and avoid intravascular injection; aspirate.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 5 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine or 0.5% ropivacaine (US allows a low volume), injected slowly in 1 mL aliquots.</li>\n<li>Only one side per session.</li>\n<li>Monitor for 30 minutes: Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, enophthalmos, conjunctival injection, nasal congestion), rise in hand temperature, pain relief.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Expected effects:</strong> Horner syndrome, nasal congestion, facial flushing.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural spread:</strong> recurrent laryngeal nerve block (hoarseness, choking), phrenic nerve block (avoid bilateral), brachial plexus block (arm numbness and weakness).</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> vertebral or carotid artery injection with seizure (tiny doses can cause seizures), haematoma (retropharyngeal haematoma can compromise the airway).</li>\n<li><strong>Oesophageal puncture</strong> (mediastinitis), especially on the left.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuraxial:</strong> epidural or intrathecal spread (high block), very rare.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> pneumothorax (at lower levels), infection, local anaesthetic toxicity, vasovagal reaction.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Confirm a successful sympathetic block:</strong> Horner syndrome, rise in hand skin temperature (at least 1–2°C, close to the other hand), venous engorgement; a block without these signs cannot be interpreted.</li>\n<li><strong>Response:</strong> pain relief, allodynia, range of motion during the block; <strong>hand therapy immediately afterwards</strong> while pain is lower.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety:</strong> voice, swallowing and breathing before discharge; no eating or drinking until swallowing is normal; no driving.</li>\n<li><strong>Series:</strong> if there is a good response, repeat the block (for example weekly for a few sessions) alongside therapy; if no response, stop sympathetic blocks (sympathetically independent pain).</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up:</strong> CRPS Severity Score, function, oedema, temperature, mood; refer early to a specialist CRPS service if not improving; consider neuromodulation for refractory CRPS.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not naming and applying the Budapest criteria.</li>\n<li>Not using colour Doppler for the inferior thyroid and vertebral arteries, or not identifying the oesophagus by swallowing.</li>\n<li>Not checking that the block worked (Horner syndrome and temperature rise).</li>\n<li>Presenting the block as the treatment instead of a window for rehabilitation.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided cervical sympathetic block. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 24, pp. 237–242.", "Alshuraim FMM, Flamer D. Cervical sympathetic trunk. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 3, pp. 43–52.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Harden RN, et al. Validation of proposed diagnostic criteria (the \"Budapest Criteria\") for complex regional pain syndrome. <em>Pain</em> 2010;150:268–274.", "Gofeld M, Bhatia A, Abbas S, Ganapathy S, Johnson M. Development and validation of a new technique for ultrasound-guided stellate ganglion block. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:475–479.", "Narouze S. Ultrasound-guided stellate ganglion block: safety and efficacy. <em>Curr Pain Headache Rep</em> 2014;18:424.", "O'Connell NE, et al. Local anaesthetic sympathetic blockade for complex regional pain syndrome. <em>Cochrane Database Syst Rev</em> 2016;(7):CD004598."]}, "10.1": {"title": "Sacroiliac joint pain after pregnancy", "vignette": "A 32-year-old female schoolteacher presents 9 months after the vaginal delivery of her second baby, which was long and needed forceps. Since late pregnancy she has had pain in the right lower back and buttock, just below the belt line, with occasional spread into the back of the right thigh and the groin. She can put one finger on the spot. The pain is worst when she stands on her right leg, climbs stairs, gets in and out of the car, turns over in bed and lifts her baby from the cot. She is breastfeeding. She has no leg numbness or weakness. A pelvic belt helped during pregnancy. Physiotherapy and paracetamol have helped a little; she avoids NSAIDs because of breastfeeding. Lumbar MRI was normal.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> peripartum onset, unilateral pain below L5 over the sacral sulcus (Fortin finger sign: one-finger pointing to just inferomedial to the PSIS), referral to buttock, posterior thigh and groin, worse with single-leg loading, stairs, turning in bed, transfers, response to a pelvic belt, normal lumbar MRI. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> posterior thigh pain (radiculopathy), groin pain (hip pathology), breastfeeding (medication and steroid choices), forceps delivery (pelvic floor, pubic symphysis, coccyx injury).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> right SIJ pain (pregnancy-related pelvic girdle pain persisting post-partum).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pubic symphysis dysfunction (anterior pelvic pain)</li>\n<li>Lumbar facet pain or discogenic pain (MRI normal, but these do not show on MRI)</li>\n<li>Lumbar radiculopathy (no neurological signs)</li>\n<li>Piriformis syndrome; gluteal tendinopathy</li>\n<li>Hip pathology: labral tear, femoroacetabular impingement (groin pain)</li>\n<li>Coccydynia; pelvic floor dysfunction after forceps delivery</li>\n<li>Sacroiliitis (spondyloarthritis: inflammatory back pain, morning stiffness, HLA-B27); post-partum septic sacroiliitis (fever, raised CRP)</li>\n<li>Sacral insufficiency fracture (rare, post-partum with breastfeeding-related bone loss)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (posture, gait, single-leg stance, Trendelenburg), supine, side-lying, prone.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (knee, ankle); Neurotip and light touch L2–S1; MRC power of the lower limbs; straight leg raise and slump (negative).</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fortin finger test:</strong> patient points to an area within 1 cm inferomedial to the PSIS.</li>\n<li>Palpation: PSIS, sacral sulcus, long dorsal sacroiliac ligament, pubic symphysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Five SIJ provocation tests (Laslett):</strong> distraction, compression, thigh thrust, Gaenslen, sacral thrust. Three or more positive tests (including thigh thrust or distraction) give a sensitivity of about 90% and specificity of about 80% for SIJ pain; if none of the tests reproduce the pain, SIJ pain is unlikely.</li>\n<li><strong>Active straight leg raise test</strong> (pregnancy-related pelvic girdle pain); <strong>posterior pelvic pain provocation test (P4).</strong></li>\n<li>Lumbar range and extension–rotation; hip range, FABER, FADIR.</li>\n<li>Pubic symphysis palpation, modified Trendelenburg.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>name all five Laslett tests and the \"three or more of five\" rule. It is the single most useful thing to say in an SIJ case.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>No imaging confirms mechanical SIJ pain;</strong> imaging excludes other causes.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI of the sacroiliac joints (STIR sequences):</strong> bone marrow oedema of sacroiliitis, insufficiency fracture, infection; lumbar MRI already normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic X-ray:</strong> symphysis widening, sacroiliitis (in later disease).</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods</strong> if inflammatory or infective features: CRP, ESR, HLA-B27, FBC.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic SIJ injection</strong> with local anaesthetic (image-guided): the reference standard; relief of at least 50% (75% with dual blocks for stricter selection) supports the SIJ as the pain source.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Curvilinear 2–6 MHz probe</strong> (linear in a slim patient), depth 4–6 cm. Patient prone with a pillow under the abdomen.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> transverse scan at the sacral hiatus (sacral cornua, median sacral crest), then moving laterally and cephalad.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus (Klauser's two levels):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lower level (S2 foramen):</strong> transverse view at the level of the S2 posterior foramen; the lateral edge of the sacrum and the medial edge of the ilium form a hypoechoic cleft (the inferior SIJ). The joint gap is lateral to the S2 foramen; do not mistake the foramen (medial, round) for the joint (lateral, a cleft).</li>\n<li><strong>Upper level (S1 foramen):</strong> the posterior superior iliac spine and the upper joint, less accessible because of the overlying ilium and interosseous ligaments.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth to the joint for needle planning; joint gap compared with the left side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> small vessels near the joint and the posterior sacral foramina; during injection, colour Doppler shows fluid spreading in the joint cleft.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> gentle hip rotation or a sacral spring test while scanning to see joint movement; sonopalpation over the joint reproduces her pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> median sacral crest, lateral sacral crest, posterior sacral foramina (S1–S4), sacral cornua, sacral hiatus, ilium, PSIS, SIJ cleft, posterior sacroiliac ligaments, gluteus maximus, multifidus.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and reassurance:</strong> most pregnancy-related pelvic girdle pain resolves, but some persists beyond a year.</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic belt</strong> (sacroiliac belt) for stability during activities.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> specific stabilising exercises (transversus abdominis, gluteal and pelvic floor strengthening), manual therapy; pelvic floor physiotherapy after a forceps delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Activity advice:</strong> ergonomics for lifting the baby (raised cot, changing table), sitting rather than standing to dress.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication compatible with breastfeeding:</strong> paracetamol, ibuprofen (short courses, generally compatible with breastfeeding), topical NSAIDs.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided intra-articular SIJ injection</strong> into the lower third of the joint at the S2 level, with local anaesthetic and steroid (diagnostic and therapeutic).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Landmark-guided SIJ injections are intra-articular in only about 22% of cases; image guidance is required.</li>\n<li>US-guided injection at the lower level was intra-articular in about 76% of patients (Klauser 2008) and in about 88% in a cadaver study (Narouze review); US with fluoroscopic confirmation gave similar accuracy and efficacy to fluoroscopy alone (Soneji 2016). Fluoroscopy with contrast remains the reference for confirming intra-articular spread.</li>\n<li>Single-dose steroid is considered compatible with breastfeeding.</li>\n<li>Later options: sacral lateral branch blocks (L5 dorsal ramus and S1–S3 lateral branches) and, if positive, cooled or conventional RF of the lateral branches; SIJ fusion for selected refractory cases.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies and anticoagulation; discuss breastfeeding (steroid passes into milk in small amounts; usually no need to interrupt).</li>\n<li>Prone with a pillow under the abdomen; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Curvilinear probe transverse at the sacral hiatus, then moved cephalad and lateral to the S2 level; identify the S2 posterior foramen and the lateral SIJ cleft.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 90 mm (3.5 inch), in plane from medial to lateral, or out of plane, aiming into the hypoechoic cleft between the sacrum and the ilium.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the lower third of the joint (the synovial part), at S2.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> watch for fluid entering the cleft; colour Doppler shows spread in the joint. If fluid spreads superficially in the ligaments or muscle, re-direct.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 2–4 mL total, for example 1–2 mL 0.5% bupivacaine with 40 mg triamcinolone or methylprednisolone.</li>\n<li>Re-test the provocation tests and her painful activities after 15–30 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> spread to the sciatic nerve or sacral roots (temporary leg numbness or weakness, fall risk); injection into a sacral foramen (S1 or S2 root block) if the foramen is mistaken for the joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> intravascular injection (gluteal vessels), haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Joint:</strong> septic sacroiliitis (rare).</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> flare, flushing, menstrual irregularity, raised glucose; small amounts in breast milk.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, increased pain.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> leg strength and sensation before walking; percentage relief during provocation tests and painful activities (diagnostic value).</li>\n<li><strong>Pain diary</strong> for 6–8 hours, matching the local anaesthetic.</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain, function (ODI or Pelvic Girdle Questionnaire), ability to care for her baby and return to work.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> continue stabilisation exercises and belt; repeat injection if helpful (not more than about 3–4 a year); if short relief, sacral lateral branch blocks then RF; if no relief, reconsider facet, discogenic, hip or symphysis pain.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not naming Laslett's five tests and the \"three or more\" rule; not doing the Fortin finger test.</li>\n<li>Mistaking the S2 posterior foramen for the joint cleft.</li>\n<li>Claiming that landmark SIJ injections are reliable (they are intra-articular in only about a fifth).</li>\n<li>Forgetting breastfeeding in medication and steroid decisions.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Vydyanathan A, Narouze SN. Ultrasound-guided sacroiliac joint injection. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 14, pp. 151–154.", "Finlayson R, Le-Beuffe MFE. Sacroiliac joint and sacral lateral branch blocks. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 15, pp. 185–190.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Laslett M, Aprill CN, McDonald B, Young SB. Diagnosis of sacroiliac joint pain: validity of individual provocation tests and composites of tests. <em>Man Ther</em> 2005;10:207–218.", "Klauser A, et al. Feasibility of ultrasound-guided sacroiliac joint injection considering sonoanatomic landmarks at two different levels in cadavers and patients. <em>Arthritis Rheum</em> 2008;59:1618–1624.", "Soneji N, et al. Comparison of fluoroscopy and ultrasound guidance for sacroiliac joint injection in patients with chronic low back pain. <em>Pain Pract</em> 2016;16:537–544."]}, "11.1": {"title": "Knee pain, osteoarthritis", "vignette": "A 67-year-old retired cook presents with a 4-year history of pain in both knees, the right much worse. The pain is felt across the front and inner side of the knee and is worst going down stairs, getting up from a low chair and after walking for 20 minutes. She is stiff for about 15 minutes after getting up in the morning or after sitting. The right knee swells after a busy day and sometimes feels as if it will give way. She also has a sore spot just below the inner side of the right knee, and an occasional ache in the right groin. Her BMI is 34; she has hypertension and chronic kidney disease stage 3. Paracetamol helps a little. She is on a 14-month waiting list for knee replacement and asks if anything can help in the meantime.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> age over 50, use-related pain, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, swelling after activity, obesity, both knees. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> sore spot below the medial joint line (pes anserine bursitis), groin ache (hip OA referring to the knee), giving way (meniscus or quadriceps weakness), CKD (limits NSAIDs), the planned knee replacement (affects timing of steroid).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> primary knee osteoarthritis, medial compartment and patellofemoral, with a symptomatic effusion. She meets the ACR clinical criteria: age over 50, stiffness under 30 minutes, with crepitus, bony tenderness or bony enlargement.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pes anserine bursitis or tendinopathy</li>\n<li>Degenerative medial meniscal tear</li>\n<li>Hip osteoarthritis with referred knee pain</li>\n<li>Crystal arthritis: CPPD (pseudogout) or gout; inflammatory arthritis</li>\n<li>Spontaneous osteonecrosis of the knee (sudden severe medial pain, night pain)</li>\n<li>Baker's (popliteal) cyst</li>\n<li>Saphenous neuropathy (infrapatellar branch); L3 or L4 radiculopathy</li>\n<li>Septic arthritis (hot, swollen knee: always aspirate)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (alignment, varus or valgus, gait, single-leg stance), then supine with the knee supported; prone for the popliteal fossa.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> varus deformity, quadriceps wasting (measure thigh girth 10 cm above the patella, both sides), swelling, skin changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (knee and ankle jerks); Neurotip and light touch in L3–S1 and over the infrapatellar saphenous territory; MRC power of hip and knee muscles.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Effusion: bulge (sweep) test and patellar tap.</li>\n<li>Range of motion with a goniometer: fixed flexion deformity, flexion range; crepitus.</li>\n<li>Palpation: medial joint line tenderness, osteophytes, warmth; <strong>pes anserine tenderness</strong> about 5 cm below the medial joint line.</li>\n<li>Stability: varus and valgus stress, Lachman and anterior drawer.</li>\n<li>Meniscus: McMurray and Thessaly tests (often positive in OA, low specificity).</li>\n<li>Patellofemoral: patellar grind (Clarke) test.</li>\n<li><strong>Hip:</strong> internal rotation, FADIR and FABER; reproduction of \"knee\" pain with hip internal rotation points to the hip.</li>\n<li>Function: timed up-and-go or 30-second chair stand.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>always examine the hip in knee pain, and say why.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Weight-bearing X-rays:</strong> standing AP, Rosenberg view (posteroanterior at 45° flexion, more sensitive for joint space narrowing), lateral and skyline. Grade with <strong>Kellgren–Lawrence (0–4)</strong>: osteophytes, joint space narrowing, sclerosis, deformity. X-ray severity does not match pain closely.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration of the effusion</strong> (done under US at the injection): appearance, cell count, crystals (CPPD rhomboid, positively birefringent; urate needles, negatively birefringent), Gram stain and culture if any suspicion of infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> effusion and synovitis, osteophytes, meniscal extrusion, Baker's cyst, cartilage thickness, pes anserine bursa.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> not needed for OA; only if osteonecrosis, a mechanical lesion or another diagnosis is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> renal function (CKD, NSAIDs), HbA1c, uric acid if gout is suspected; ESR and CRP if inflammatory arthritis is possible.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. Linear 6–13 MHz probe (curvilinear for deep structures in a large knee), MSK knee preset, depth about 4 cm. Patient supine with the knee flexed 20–30° on a pillow.</p>\n<p><strong>Systematic overview by quadrant:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Anterior:</strong> quadriceps tendon and suprapatellar recess in long and short axis; measure effusion depth (above about 4 mm is significant) and look for synovial thickening; patellar tendon. Then, in full knee flexion, the femoral trochlear cartilage in a transverse view; measure its thickness and compare with the left.</li>\n<li><strong>Medial:</strong> medial femorotibial joint in the coronal plane: osteophytes, MCL, <strong>medial meniscal extrusion</strong> (more than about 3 mm is abnormal), then the pes anserine tendons and bursa below the joint line.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral:</strong> lateral joint, iliotibial band, lateral meniscus.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior (prone):</strong> popliteal fossa; Baker's cyst between the medial gastrocnemius and semimembranosus (the \"comma\" neck); popliteal vessels.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> power Doppler of the synovium in the suprapatellar recess (synovitis); popliteal vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> standing (weight-bearing) view of the medial joint to show meniscal extrusion increasing; quadriceps contraction moves fluid into the suprapatellar recess; gentle compression of the parapatellar recesses (\"milking\") shows small effusions.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> quadriceps tendon, suprapatellar fat pad, prefemoral fat pad, suprapatellar recess, femur, patella, trochlear cartilage, MCL, medial meniscus, pes anserine tendons (sartorius, gracilis, semitendinosus), semimembranosus, medial head of gastrocnemius.</p>", "<p>Following the OARSI 2019 and ACR 2019 guidelines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and self-management.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss:</strong> 5–10% body weight improves pain and function.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> quadriceps and hip strengthening, low-impact aerobic exercise, aquatic exercise, tai chi.</li>\n<li><strong>Topical NSAIDs</strong> first-line for knee OA, and preferred with her CKD (oral NSAIDs should be avoided or used very cautiously).</li>\n<li>Paracetamol (small benefit); duloxetine as an option.</li>\n<li><strong>Aids:</strong> walking stick in the opposite hand, cushioned footwear; medial unloading brace or taping.</li>\n<li>Avoid opioids where possible. Glucosamine and chondroitin are not recommended.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided intra-articular knee corticosteroid injection via the suprapatellar recess, with aspiration of the effusion first.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intra-articular corticosteroid is strongly recommended for knee OA flares; benefit usually lasts 2–8 weeks. It works best with an effusion.</li>\n<li>US guidance improves accuracy (about 91–96% guided against 40–84% blind, Spinner 2014 review) and reduces procedural pain. The superolateral approach has the highest accuracy (about 100%), against about 95% midlateral and 75% medial.</li>\n<li><strong>Timing with surgery:</strong> avoid steroid within about 3 months before knee replacement (higher risk of prosthetic infection).</li>\n<li><strong>Other options:</strong> hyaluronic acid (conditional recommendation, best in milder OA), PRP (growing evidence), <strong>genicular nerve block and radiofrequency ablation</strong> for refractory pain or if unfit for surgery (case 11.2); pes anserine injection if the bursa is the main source.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies, anticoagulation, renal function and glucose; note the date of planned surgery.</li>\n<li>Supine, knee flexed 20–30° on a pillow; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear probe in the transverse plane just above the patella (or rotated about 45° towards the lateral side) to show the suprapatellar recess under the quadriceps tendon, between the suprapatellar and prefemoral fat pads.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 21–22G 38–50 mm (a 22G 90 mm spinal needle in a large thigh), in plane from lateral to medial, passing through the vastus lateralis under the quadriceps tendon, at a flat angle.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspirate first:</strong> remove as much effusion as possible (compress the parapatellar recesses to push fluid up); send fluid for cell count, crystals and culture.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> triamcinolone acetonide 40 mg (or methylprednisolone 40–80 mg) with 3–5 mL of 1% lidocaine; total about 5 mL. Extended-release triamcinolone (32 mg) causes a smaller glucose rise, if available.</li>\n<li>Watch the fluid fill the recess, not the fat pads or the tendon.</li>\n<li>Bend and straighten the knee a few times to spread the drug.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Common:</strong> post-injection flare (including crystal-induced synovitis), facial flushing, transient raised blood glucose.</li>\n<li><strong>Joint:</strong> septic arthritis (rare); haemarthrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Local:</strong> subcutaneous fat atrophy and skin depigmentation (lateral approach, if steroid tracks back).</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated injections:</strong> faster cartilage loss; McAlindon 2017 found that triamcinolone every 3 months for 2 years caused more cartilage volume loss with no pain benefit over saline.</li>\n<li><strong>Before arthroplasty:</strong> higher periprosthetic infection risk if given within about 3 months of surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, allergy.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> pain relief after the local anaesthetic, walking, no signs of bleeding; fluid results (crystals or infection change management).</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-net advice:</strong> a hot, swollen, very painful knee or fever in the next days means urgent review (septic arthritis).</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain scores, function (WOMAC or KOOS), walking distance, effusion, range of motion.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeat:</strong> no more often than every 3 months, and no more than about 3–4 per year; keep at least 3 months clear before her knee replacement.</li>\n<li><strong>Escalate:</strong> genicular nerve block and RF ablation if injections stop helping; keep up weight loss and exercise.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not aspirating and not sending fluid for crystals and culture.</li>\n<li>Forgetting her CKD when listing NSAIDs.</li>\n<li>Not mentioning the 3-month gap before knee replacement.</li>\n<li>Not examining the hip, or not checking the pes anserine bursa.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Hurdle M-FB. Ultrasound-guided knee injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 29, pp. 283–285.", "Nouer Frederico T, Peng P. Ultrasound-guided knee intervention. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 23, knee joint pp. 283–286.", "Spinner DA, Danesh H, Baksh WS. Knee. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 6, pp. 57–58.", "Peng PW, Shankar H. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part V: knee joint. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2014;39:368–380.", "Bannuru RR, et al. OARSI guidelines for the non-surgical management of knee, hip, and polyarticular osteoarthritis. <em>Osteoarthritis Cartilage</em> 2019;27:1578–1589.", "Kolasinski SL, et al. 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation guideline for the management of osteoarthritis of the hand, hip, and knee. <em>Arthritis Care Res</em> 2020;72:149–162.", "McAlindon TE, et al. Effect of intra-articular triamcinolone vs saline on knee cartilage volume and pain in patients with knee osteoarthritis: a randomized clinical trial. <em>JAMA</em> 2017;317:1967–1975.", "Jackson DW, Evans NA, Thomas BM. Accuracy of needle placement into the intra-articular space of the knee. <em>J Bone Joint Surg Am</em> 2002;84:1522–1527."]}, "12.1": {"title": "Hip pain, osteoarthritis", "vignette": "A 71-year-old retired farmer presents with an 18-month history of right groin pain. The pain is deep in the groin and spreads to the front of the thigh and sometimes to the inner knee. It is worst when he gets out of the car, puts on his right sock and shoe, and walks more than 400 metres; he now uses a stick. Morning stiffness lasts about 30 minutes. He also has long-standing low back pain and an ache over the outer hip when he lies on that side at night. He has atrial fibrillation and takes apixaban. His BMI is 30. Paracetamol and topical diclofenac help a little; his orthopaedic surgeon has suggested a hip replacement, but he wants to delay surgery until after the harvest.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> age over 50, groin pain with referral to the thigh and knee, difficulty with shoes and socks (loss of internal rotation and flexion), car transfers, morning stiffness under 60 minutes, reduced walking distance. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> low back pain (lumbar referral), lateral hip pain lying on the side (trochanteric pain), knee pain (knee pathology), apixaban (anticoagulation decision), planned surgery (steroid timing).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> right hip osteoarthritis.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar spine: L2–L3 radiculopathy, facet or SIJ referral</li>\n<li>Greater trochanteric pain syndrome (lateral hip pain lying on the side)</li>\n<li>Iliopsoas tendinopathy or bursitis</li>\n<li>Avascular necrosis of the femoral head</li>\n<li>Femoral neck stress or insufficiency fracture</li>\n<li>Inflammatory arthritis; septic arthritis</li>\n<li>Meralgia paresthetica (anterolateral thigh, numbness)</li>\n<li>Inguinal or femoral hernia; intra-abdominal or pelvic causes</li>\n<li>Knee OA (knee pain is the referral, not the source)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (gait with and without the stick, Trendelenburg sign, leg length), then supine, then side-lying.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (knee, ankle); Neurotip and light touch in L2–S1 and the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve territory; MRC power (hip flexion L2, knee extension L3); femoral nerve stretch test and straight leg raise.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inspection: muscle wasting, fixed deformity, scars.</li>\n<li>Range with a goniometer: flexion, abduction, adduction, <strong>internal rotation (lost first)</strong>, external rotation; Thomas test for fixed flexion deformity.</li>\n<li>Provocation: FADIR (flexion, adduction, internal rotation), FABER (Patrick), log roll (very specific for intra-articular pain), resisted straight leg raise (Stinchfield).</li>\n<li>Palpation: greater trochanter, groin, adductor origin, inguinal canal.</li>\n<li>Lumbar spine and SIJ screen; knee examination.</li>\n<li>Leg length (ASIS to medial malleolus).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>say \"reduced internal rotation is the earliest and most useful sign\" and do the log roll.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> AP pelvis and lateral hip. Joint space narrowing (superior, axial or medial), osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts. Grade with Kellgren–Lawrence. Look for AVN (crescent sign, collapse) and fracture.</li>\n<li><strong>ACR clinical criteria:</strong> hip pain with internal rotation under 15° and morning stiffness under 60 minutes, age over 50, or radiographic features.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> if X-ray is normal and symptoms are significant (AVN, stress fracture, labral pathology); MRI lumbar spine if radicular signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> anterior recess effusion or synovitis, capsule distension (difference of more than about 2 mm against the other side), osteophytes; also checks the iliopsoas, trochanteric region and femoral vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic intra-articular local anaesthetic:</strong> separates hip pain from spine pain before surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> renal function, FBC; ESR and CRP if inflammatory or septic arthritis is possible; HbA1c.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side of the patient, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Curvilinear 2–6 MHz probe</strong> for this deep joint (linear 5–16 MHz in a slim patient), MSK or abdominal preset, depth 6–8 cm, focus at the femoral neck. Patient supine, leg in neutral or slight internal rotation.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Transverse scan in the groin: femoral nerve, artery and vein (lateral to medial \"NAV\"), iliopsoas, then the femoral head under the iliopsoas.</li>\n<li>Femoral shaft in short axis in the upper thigh, then up to the intertrochanteric region.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Focus on the anterior recess:</strong> rotate the probe to lie parallel to the femoral neck (oblique sagittal). See the femoral head, head–neck junction and neck, the anterior capsule (iliofemoral ligament) and the anterior synovial recess between capsule and neck. Measure the capsule-to-neck distance and compare with the left (more than about 7 mm, or 1–2 mm more than the other side, suggests effusion or synovitis).</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour Doppler to show the femoral vessels and the lateral circumflex femoral artery branches crossing the neck, before planning the needle path.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> gentle internal and external rotation of the hip while watching the head and recess; iliopsoas over the femoral head during flexion–extension (snapping hip).</p>\n<p><strong>Also look at:</strong> the greater trochanter facets and bursae (his lateral pain), iliopsoas tendon and bursa.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> femoral head, head–neck junction, femoral neck, acetabular rim, labrum, anterior capsule (iliofemoral ligament), anterior synovial recess, iliopsoas, sartorius, rectus femoris, femoral nerve, artery and vein, lateral circumflex femoral artery.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and exercise:</strong> strengthening and range-of-motion exercises, aquatic therapy, tai chi.</li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss</strong> if overweight.</li>\n<li><strong>Walking aid</strong> in the opposite hand (already using one); raised seat, long-handled shoe horn and sock aid (occupational therapy).</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> paracetamol; topical NSAIDs are less effective for a deep joint like the hip; oral NSAIDs with caution (age, anticoagulation increases bleeding risk); duloxetine as an option; avoid long-term opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Work adaptation</strong> for the harvest.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided intra-articular hip injection of corticosteroid and local anaesthetic, anterior approach.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diagnostic and therapeutic: relief after local anaesthetic confirms the hip as the main pain source, which helps the surgeon.</li>\n<li>Landmark accuracy is only about 50–80%, and an anterior landmark needle can pass within about 4.5 mm of the femoral nerve (Leopold 2001). US-guided hip injection has an accuracy of about 97% (Smith 2009) and lets you see and avoid the femoral neurovascular bundle.</li>\n<li>Steroid relief typically lasts weeks to about 3 months; hyaluronic acid has a weaker evidence base in the hip; PRP is under study.</li>\n<li><strong>Timing:</strong> avoid steroid within 3 months before hip replacement (infection risk).</li>\n<li>Later options: articular branch (femoral and obturator) nerve block and radiofrequency ablation for patients unfit for or delaying surgery; total hip arthroplasty.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; discuss apixaban. A hip joint injection is a low-to-intermediate bleeding-risk procedure; many clinicians proceed after holding one or two doses in liaison with his prescriber, balancing stroke risk. Check renal function, allergies and glucose.</li>\n<li>Supine, leg neutral; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Pre-scan: mark the femoral vessels and nerve with colour Doppler.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Probe parallel to the femoral neck (oblique sagittal), head–neck junction centred.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 90–120 mm spinal needle (Peng: 22G 3.5–5 inch), in plane from caudal-lateral to cranial-medial, entering about 2 cm from the probe edge for a shallower path; well lateral to the femoral nerve and vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the anterior synovial recess at the femoral head–neck junction, touching bone under the capsule.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> fluid should spread under the capsule along the neck (capsule lifts); fluid spreading in the iliopsoas or the fat means extra-capsular.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> triamcinolone 40 mg or methylprednisolone 40 mg with 4–8 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine or 1% lidocaine.</li>\n<li>Record pain on provocative movements (FADIR, walking) 15–30 minutes later.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Neurovascular:</strong> femoral nerve block from local anaesthetic spread (temporary quadriceps weakness: risk of falls; warn him); femoral or circumflex artery puncture; haematoma (higher on anticoagulants).</li>\n<li><strong>Joint:</strong> septic arthritis (rare); post-injection flare.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> raised glucose, flushing; possible faster joint deterioration with repeated injections; prosthetic infection if surgery follows within 3 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, allergy, local anaesthetic toxicity.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> percentage relief of groin pain during FADIR and walking (diagnostic value); quadriceps power and sensation before walking; fall risk (stick, supervision); no driving for the rest of the day.</li>\n<li><strong>Anticoagulation:</strong> restart apixaban as agreed; watch for a groin haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-net:</strong> fever, a hot or very painful hip means urgent review.</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain, walking distance, function (WOMAC or Oxford Hip Score), sleep.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> repeat no sooner than 3 months; record the injection date for the surgeon (at least 3 months before arthroplasty); refer to surgery when pain and function justify it.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not scanning and marking the femoral neurovascular bundle with Doppler before needling.</li>\n<li>Using a linear probe without considering depth in a patient with a BMI of 30.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the anticoagulant and the surgery date.</li>\n<li>Forgetting to separate hip pain from lumbar pain and trochanteric pain.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Shankar H, Saeed K. Ultrasound-guided intra-articular hip injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 28, pp. 279–281.", "Stogicza A. Hip. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 22, hip joint pp. 270–274.", "Ibrahim MM, Scott Y, Spinner DA, Herrera JE. Hip. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 5, pp. 43–45.", "Peng PW. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part IV: hip. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2013;38:264–273.", "Leopold SS, Battista V, Oliverio JA. Safety and efficacy of intraarticular hip injection using anatomic landmarks. <em>Clin Orthop Relat Res</em> 2001;(391):192–197.", "Smith J, Hurdle MF, Weingarten TN. Accuracy of sonographically guided intra-articular injections in the native adult hip. <em>J Ultrasound Med</em> 2009;28:329–335.", "Bannuru RR, et al. OARSI guidelines for the non-surgical management of knee, hip, and polyarticular osteoarthritis. <em>Osteoarthritis Cartilage</em> 2019;27:1578–1589."]}, "12.2": {"title": "Hip pain, greater trochanteric pain syndrome", "vignette": "A 56-year-old female accountant presents with a 7-month history of pain over the outer side of the right hip. The pain started after she began brisk hill walking to lose weight. It is worst at night when she lies on either side, when she climbs stairs, and when she stands on the right leg to put on trousers. She sits with her legs crossed at work, which aches. The pain sometimes spreads down the outer thigh towards the knee, and she has occasional tingling over the outer thigh. She is menopausal and her BMI is 31. Paracetamol, ibuprofen and two massage sessions gave little relief, and she has stopped walking. A friend told her she needs \"a cortisone shot for bursitis\".", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> female, 40–60, menopausal, recent increase in load (hill walking), lateral hip pain, night pain lying on the side, pain on stairs and single-leg stance, crossed legs (tendon compression). <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> pain down the outer thigh (L5 radiculopathy, ITB), outer-thigh tingling (meralgia paresthetica), the patient's own request for \"bursitis\" treatment (the main problem is usually tendon, not bursa).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> greater trochanteric pain syndrome, mainly gluteus medius and minimus tendinopathy (the \"rotator cuff of the hip\"), with possible secondary trochanteric bursitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gluteus medius or minimus tendon tear</li>\n<li>Hip osteoarthritis or femoroacetabular impingement (groin pain, reduced internal rotation)</li>\n<li>L4–L5 radiculopathy or lumbar facet or SIJ referral</li>\n<li>Meralgia paresthetica (lateral femoral cutaneous nerve)</li>\n<li>Iliotibial band syndrome</li>\n<li>Piriformis or deep gluteal syndrome</li>\n<li>Femoral neck stress fracture (in a new walker; pain on weight-bearing)</li>\n<li>Rarely: AVN, tumour, infection</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (gait, Trendelenburg sign, single-leg stance), supine, then side-lying with the painful side up.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (knee, ankle); Neurotip and light touch over L4–S1 and the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve territory; MRC power of hip abductors (L5), dorsiflexion and great toe extension; straight leg raise and slump; Tinel's sign medial to the ASIS.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpation: point tenderness over the greater trochanter (posterior-superior and lateral facets); jump sign.</li>\n<li><strong>Single-leg stance for 30 seconds:</strong> pain within 30 seconds is one of the most useful tests for gluteal tendinopathy.</li>\n<li><strong>FADER with resisted external de-rotation test,</strong> resisted hip abduction, FABER (lateral pain).</li>\n<li>Hip range and FADIR (intra-articular pathology), Ober test (ITB tightness).</li>\n<li>Hip abductor strength (MRC) and Trendelenburg; lumbar spine and SIJ screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>name the single-leg stance test and the de-rotation test. Lateral tenderness alone is very sensitive but not specific.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical diagnosis</strong> in most cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> first-line imaging. Gluteus minimus (anterior facet) and medius (lateral and posterosuperior facets) for thickening, hypoechogenicity, calcification, cortical irregularity of the trochanter, partial or full-thickness tears; bursal fluid; power Doppler. Compare with the other side.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> AP pelvis to exclude hip OA, enthesophytes, calcification.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> if a tear is suspected (failed treatment, weakness), or to exclude stress fracture or AVN.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI lumbar spine</strong> only with radicular signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided local anaesthetic injection:</strong> relief supports a trochanteric source.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine in front of you, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 5–12 MHz probe</strong> (curvilinear in a larger patient), MSK preset, depth 4–6 cm. Patient lying on the left side, painful side up, hips slightly flexed with a pillow between the knees.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> transverse scan over the greater trochanter to find the bony \"facets\"; then a scan anterior to the trochanter for the hip joint recess.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the trochanteric region (transverse and longitudinal):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Anterior facet:</strong> gluteus minimus tendon.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral facet:</strong> gluteus medius (lateral part); the sub-gluteus medius bursa between it and the bone.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterosuperior facet:</strong> main gluteus medius insertion.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior facet:</strong> bare bone covered by the trochanteric (sub-gluteus maximus) bursa, deep to the gluteus maximus and iliotibial band.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> tendon thickness and any bursal fluid; compare with the left.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> power Doppler for neovascularity in the tendons or a hyperaemic bursa.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> passive hip internal and external rotation shows tendon and ITB movement over the trochanter; snapping of the ITB over the trochanter during flexion–extension.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> greater trochanter and its anterior, lateral, posterosuperior and posterior facets; gluteus minimus, gluteus medius and gluteus maximus; tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band; trochanteric (sub-gluteus maximus), sub-gluteus medius and sub-gluteus minimus bursae.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and load management</strong> (the most effective element): avoid crossing the legs, standing \"hanging\" on one hip, low chairs, and lying on the painful side (or put a pillow between the knees when lying on the other side); reduce hills and stairs temporarily.</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive exercise programme:</strong> isometric then isotonic gluteal strengthening. In the LEAP trial (Mellor 2018), education plus exercise gave better results than a steroid injection at 8 and 52 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> paracetamol, topical or short-course oral NSAIDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Shockwave therapy (ESWT):</strong> reasonable evidence for GTPS when exercise fails.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided trochanteric bursa injection of corticosteroid and local anaesthetic,</strong> placed in the sub-gluteus maximus bursa and, if the gluteus medius is the main source, the sub-gluteus medius bursa (half of the dose in each), <strong>as an adjunct to the exercise programme.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A steroid injection gives good short-term relief (better than usual care at 3 months, Brinks 2011), but by 1 year, education and exercise alone do better.</li>\n<li>Fluoroscopic guidance did not improve outcomes over blind injection in an RCT (Cohen 2009), but US shows tendon tears and avoids intratendinous injection.</li>\n<li>Do not inject steroid into the tendon.</li>\n<li>Later options: PRP into the tendinopathy (some RCT evidence of benefit over steroid at longer follow-up), needle tenotomy, ESWT; surgical repair for a full-thickness gluteal tendon tear.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies, anticoagulation and glucose.</li>\n<li>Left side-lying, painful side up, pillow between the knees; machine in front of you.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear (or curvilinear) probe in the transverse plane over the greater trochanter; identify the facets, tendons and bursae; colour Doppler for vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 50–90 mm (depending on body size), in plane from posterior to anterior (or anterior to posterior).</li>\n<li><strong>Targets:</strong> the trochanteric (sub-gluteus maximus) bursa, between the gluteus maximus/ITB and the gluteus medius tendon over the lateral and posterior facets; and the sub-gluteus medius bursa, between the tendon and the bone of the lateral facet.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> fluid should spread in a thin layer along the bursal plane; resistance or fluid inside the tendon means withdraw.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> triamcinolone or methylprednisolone 40 mg with 4–5 mL of 1% lidocaine or 0.25% bupivacaine; split between the two bursae if both are targeted.</li>\n<li>Re-test single-leg stance and palpation 10 minutes later.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Common:</strong> post-injection flare; transient raised glucose; facial flushing.</li>\n<li><strong>Local:</strong> skin depigmentation and fat atrophy (especially in thin patients if injected superficially); intratendinous injection with tendon weakening or rupture (avoid repeated injections).</li>\n<li><strong>Rare:</strong> infection, haematoma, vasovagal reaction, allergy; temporary numbness of the thigh if local anaesthetic spreads to cutaneous nerves.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> relief of point tenderness and single-leg stance pain (diagnostic); walking safely.</li>\n<li><strong>Essential:</strong> start or continue the progressive exercise and load-management programme within days; this decides the 1-year outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>At 6–8 weeks:</strong> night pain, VISA-G score (Victorian Institute of Sport Assessment, gluteal), pain on stairs, abductor strength.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeat steroid</strong> no more than 2–3 times a year; if pain returns quickly or weakness develops, image for a gluteal tendon tear and consider PRP or surgical referral.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Calling it \"bursitis\" and ignoring the tendon: say \"gluteal tendinopathy\".</li>\n<li>Not offering education and exercise as the main treatment, with the injection as an adjunct.</li>\n<li>Not checking for a tendon tear before repeated injections.</li>\n<li>Not addressing the tingling (meralgia paresthetica) distractor.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Stogicza A. Hip. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 22, greater trochanteric complex and bursae pp. 275–278.", "Ibrahim MM, Scott Y, Spinner DA, Herrera JE. Hip. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 5, greater trochanteric pain syndrome pp. 45–46.", "Peng PW. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part IV: hip. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2013;38:264–273.", "Mellor R, et al. Education plus exercise versus corticosteroid injection use versus a wait and see approach on global outcome and pain from gluteal tendinopathy: prospective, single blinded, randomised clinical trial. <em>BMJ</em> 2018;361:k1662.", "Brinks A, et al. Corticosteroid injections for greater trochanteric pain syndrome: a randomized controlled trial in primary care. <em>Ann Fam Med</em> 2011;9:226–234.", "Cohen SP, et al. Comparison of fluoroscopically guided and blind corticosteroid injections for greater trochanteric pain syndrome: multicentre randomised controlled trial. <em>BMJ</em> 2009;338:b1088."]}, "12.3": {"title": "Hip pain, piriformis syndrome", "vignette": "A 34-year-old female physiotherapist and keen runner presents with a 6-month history of deep right buttock pain. It started after she increased her hill training for a half-marathon and slipped on a wet step, landing on her right buttock. The pain is worst when she sits for more than 20 minutes, especially on a hard seat or with the right leg crossed, and when she climbs stairs. It sometimes shoots down the back of the thigh to the knee, with tingling, but never into the foot. Her back does not hurt, coughing does not change the pain, and she has no weakness. A lumbar MRI arranged by her family doctor showed \"minor disc bulges, no nerve compression\". Stretching, naproxen and massage have helped a little.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> buttock fall and overuse (running, hills), deep buttock pain, worse sitting and crossing the leg (hip flexion, adduction, internal rotation stretches the piriformis), radiation to the posterior thigh not below the knee, no back pain, no cough impulse, normal neurology, MRI without root compression. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> sciatica-like radiation (lumbar radiculopathy), the MRI disc bulges (incidental), the fall (think SIJ or coccyx injury, ischial bursitis).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> piriformis syndrome, a form of deep gluteal syndrome, after trauma and overuse. It is a diagnosis of exclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar radiculopathy (L5 or S1) or lumbar facet referred pain</li>\n<li>Sacroiliac joint pain (fall onto the buttock)</li>\n<li>Proximal hamstring tendinopathy or ischial bursitis (pain sitting, ischial tuberosity)</li>\n<li>Ischiofemoral impingement (quadratus femoris)</li>\n<li>Gluteus medius or minimus tendinopathy (lateral)</li>\n<li>Hip pathology: labral tear, femoroacetabular impingement</li>\n<li>Pudendal or inferior cluneal neuralgia</li>\n<li>Rarely: sciatic nerve tumour, endometriosis of the sciatic nerve (cyclical pain), vascular anomaly</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (posture, gait, lumbar range, single-leg stance), then supine, then side-lying, then prone.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (knee, ankle, medial hamstring); Neurotip and light touch in L4–S1 and the posterior femoral cutaneous territory; 128 Hz tuning fork; MRC power of hip extensors and abductors, knee flexors, ankle and toes; straight leg raise and slump (often mildly positive in piriformis syndrome, but without a dermatomal pattern).</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpation: tenderness over the piriformis (midway between the PSIS and the greater trochanter), the sciatic notch, the ischial tuberosity and hamstring origin, the SIJ and coccyx.</li>\n<li><strong>Piriformis provocation tests:</strong> FAIR (flexion, adduction, internal rotation in side-lying), Freiberg (forced internal rotation of the extended hip), Pace (resisted abduction and external rotation in sitting), Beatty (side-lying active hip abduction).</li>\n<li><strong>Seated piriformis stretch test.</strong></li>\n<li>SIJ provocation tests (at least 3 of 5 positive suggests SIJ pain); hip range, FADIR, FABER.</li>\n<li>Hamstring tests: Puranen–Orava, bent-knee stretch.</li>\n<li>Lumbar spine: range, extension–rotation.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>name the FAIR test. It is the best-studied test for piriformis syndrome (Fishman 2002), and the examiners expect to hear it.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Imaging to exclude other causes:</strong> lumbar MRI already done; MRI pelvis or MR neurography if needed (piriformis asymmetry or hypertrophy, sciatic nerve signal change, anatomical variant with the nerve dividing through the muscle, masses).</li>\n<li><strong>Electrodiagnostics:</strong> usually normal; a prolonged H-reflex in the FAIR position supports the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> piriformis thickness compared with the left side, the sciatic nerve below it (calibre, echotexture), dynamic movement of the muscle with hip rotation; hamstring origin and ischial bursa.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided piriformis injection</strong> with local anaesthetic: relief supports the diagnosis.</li>\n<li>Bloods only if inflammatory or infective causes are suspected.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Curvilinear 2–6 MHz probe</strong>, abdominal or MSK deep preset, depth 6–8 cm. Patient prone with a pillow under the pelvis.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Iliac crest:</strong> probe transverse over the posterior ilium, medial end at the PSIS (the dimple of Venus): gluteus maximus over bone.</li>\n<li><strong>Greater sciatic notch:</strong> move caudally until the bone gives way to the curved bright line of the ischium. Align the probe along the long axis of the piriformis (from the sacrum towards the greater trochanter) and tilt medially.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> the piriformis lies deep to the gluteus maximus; the sciatic nerve is a flat, bright, honeycomb structure deep to the piriformis (or, in a variant, passing through it). Moving further caudally, the ischium straightens and the sacrospinous ligament appears: that is the ischial spine, below the piriformis.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> piriformis thickness compared with the left; sciatic nerve size.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> the superior gluteal artery between the gluteus maximus and piriformis; the inferior gluteal artery and vein deep to the piriformis near the sciatic nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> with the knee bent to 90°, rotate the hip internally and externally: the piriformis slides and changes shape, which separates it from the gluteus maximus above and helps identify it.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> ilium, posterior superior iliac spine, greater sciatic notch, ischium, gluteus maximus, piriformis, sciatic nerve, superior gluteal artery, inferior gluteal artery and vein, sacrospinous ligament, greater trochanter.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Load and sitting changes:</strong> cushion, avoid crossing the legs, regular breaks from sitting; reduce hill training temporarily.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> piriformis and external rotator stretching, gluteal and core strengthening, neural mobilisation of the sciatic nerve, correction of running biomechanics.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> NSAIDs (short course), muscle relaxants briefly; a neuropathic agent if there are prominent neuropathic features.</li>\n<li><strong>Manual therapy,</strong> dry needling, heat.</li>\n<li>Most patients improve with 6–12 weeks of a structured programme.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided piriformis muscle injection,</strong> with local anaesthetic and steroid (or local anaesthetic alone as a diagnostic test), followed by stretching.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluoroscopic contrast-guided piriformis injection reached the muscle in only about 30% of cadaver injections (most ended in the gluteus maximus); US-guided injection was accurate in about 95% (Finnoff 2008).</li>\n<li>US shows the sciatic nerve and gluteal arteries, so the needle can stay in the muscle.</li>\n<li>Later options: botulinum toxin injection into the piriformis (longer relief in some trials); perisciatic hydrodissection if the nerve is tethered; surgical release of the piriformis tendon (rarely, after failed treatment).</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; explain the possibility of temporary leg numbness or weakness. Check allergies and anticoagulation.</li>\n<li>Prone, pillow under the pelvis; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Pre-scan: piriformis, sciatic nerve, superior and inferior gluteal arteries (colour Doppler); dynamic hip rotation to confirm the muscle.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 90–120 mm (3.5–5 inch), preferably echogenic, in plane from lateral to medial.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the belly of the piriformis, in the lateral part of the muscle, superficial to and away from the sciatic nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> the muscle fibres swell and separate (fluid spreads within the muscle); spread in the plane above means the gluteus maximus, below means near the nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 1–2 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine with methylprednisolone 40 mg (Peng); some use 3–5 mL. For botulinum toxin (later): for example 50–100 units onabotulinumtoxinA, according to local practice.</li>\n<li>Re-test FAIR and seated pain after 15 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Sciatic nerve block</strong> (spread or needle too deep): temporary foot drop, leg numbness, fall risk; nerve injury from direct needle trauma (rare).</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> superior or inferior gluteal artery puncture, haematoma, intravascular injection.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> flare, raised glucose, flushing; muscle atrophy with repeated injections.</li>\n<li><strong>Botulinum toxin:</strong> weakness of neighbouring muscles, flu-like symptoms.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, vasovagal reaction, local anaesthetic toxicity.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> percentage relief when sitting and during FAIR (diagnostic value), leg strength and sensation (sciatic spread); she should not drive if the leg is numb.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation:</strong> start stretching and strengthening while the pain is low; adjust running training.</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> sitting tolerance, pain, return to running, function (for example Oswestry or a hip score).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> repeat injection or botulinum toxin if relief was clear but short; reconsider the diagnosis if the local anaesthetic gave no relief (hamstring origin, SIJ, spine, pudendal).</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Calling it piriformis syndrome without excluding the spine, SIJ and hamstring origin first.</li>\n<li>Not identifying the sciatic nerve and gluteal arteries before injecting.</li>\n<li>Injecting the gluteus maximus by mistake: use dynamic hip rotation to prove which muscle is the piriformis.</li>\n<li>Not mentioning botulinum toxin as the next step.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Chan C-W, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided blocks for pelvic pain. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 17, piriformis syndrome pp. 172–175.", "Bhatia A, Peng P. Pelvic muscles. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 8, piriformis pp. 93–99.", "Peng PWH, Narouze S. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part I: nonaxial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:458–474.", "Finnoff JT, Hurdle MF, Smith J. Accuracy of ultrasound-guided versus fluoroscopically guided contrast-controlled piriformis injections: a cadaveric study. <em>J Ultrasound Med</em> 2008;27:1157–1163.", "Fishman LM, et al. Piriformis syndrome: diagnosis, treatment, and outcome, a 10-year study. <em>Arch Phys Med Rehabil</em> 2002;83:295–301."]}, "14.1": {"title": "Meralgia paresthetica", "vignette": "A 49-year-old male security guard presents with a 5-month history of burning, tingling and numbness over the outer front of his left thigh, in a patch about the size of his hand. It is worse when he stands for a long time or walks, and when he wears his duty belt with radio and baton; it eases when he sits. Even the touch of his trousers is unpleasant. He has gained 15 kg in the past year, and his BMI is now 36. He has type 2 diabetes diagnosed 6 months ago. There is no back pain, no weakness and no change in bladder function. He wonders if he has \"a slipped disc\" like his brother.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> anterolateral thigh, a sharply limited patch, sensory only (burning, numbness, allodynia), worse standing, walking and hip extension, eased by sitting (hip flexion), tight belt, recent weight gain, obesity, diabetes. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> diabetes (diabetic neuropathy or diabetic amyotrophy), his brother's \"slipped disc\" (L2–L3 radiculopathy), the job (a duty belt is the direct compression cause, so management must change it).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> left meralgia paresthetica from compression of the LFCN under or through the inguinal ligament near the ASIS, due to obesity and a tight heavy belt.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper lumbar radiculopathy (L2–L3): would have back pain, motor or reflex changes (knee jerk, hip flexion, quadriceps)</li>\n<li>Lumbar plexopathy; diabetic amyotrophy (pain, then proximal weakness and wasting)</li>\n<li>Diabetic peripheral neuropathy (glove-and-stocking, distal and symmetric)</li>\n<li>Femoral neuropathy (anterior thigh and medial leg numbness, quadriceps weakness, absent knee jerk)</li>\n<li>Greater trochanteric pain syndrome or hip OA (lateral thigh pain without sensory loss)</li>\n<li>Retroperitoneal or pelvic mass compressing the nerve (iliacus haematoma, tumour)</li>\n<li>Iliotibial band syndrome</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (gait, posture, belt position, abdominal girth), then supine with the hips extended.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensory map:</strong> Neurotip and cotton wool across the anterolateral thigh; draw the borders of numbness and allodynia with a skin marker, compare with the right. The patch should be purely in the LFCN territory and stop above the knee.</li>\n<li><strong>Tinel's sign</strong> about 1–2 cm medial and inferior to the ASIS; <strong>pelvic compression test</strong> (side-lying, press down on the pelvis for 45 seconds; relief suggests MP).</li>\n<li><strong>Reverse straight leg raise / femoral nerve stretch</strong> (hip extension reproduces the symptoms).</li>\n<li><strong>Motor and reflexes:</strong> MRC power of hip flexion, knee extension and adduction (should be normal); knee and ankle jerks with a hammer (normal in MP).</li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral neuropathy screen:</strong> 10 g monofilament and 128 Hz tuning fork at the toes (diabetes).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar spine range, extension–rotation; hip range, FADIR, FABER, trochanteric palpation.</li>\n<li>Palpation at the ASIS and inguinal ligament; abdominal examination (mass).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>a clean sensory map that stops above the knee, plus normal power and reflexes, is the core of the diagnosis. Say so.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical diagnosis</strong> in most cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> the LFCN near the ASIS; a swollen nerve (pseudoneuroma) proximal to the inguinal ligament; compare the cross-sectional area with the right side.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided LFCN block:</strong> relief of symptoms with local anaesthetic confirms the diagnosis (a reference standard).</li>\n<li><strong>Nerve conduction studies:</strong> LFCN sensory studies are technically difficult, especially in obesity (often unobtainable on both sides); somatosensory evoked potentials are an option. Useful to exclude lumbar radiculopathy, plexopathy and diabetic neuropathy.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> HbA1c, B12, TSH (for neuropathy).</li>\n<li><strong>Imaging:</strong> lumbar MRI if there are radicular features; CT or MRI pelvis if a mass is suspected (atypical features, weight loss, progression).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>High-frequency linear 6–18 MHz probe</strong>, small-parts or nerve preset, depth 2–3 cm. Patient supine, hip in neutral.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> start with the probe transverse over the ASIS (bright bony landmark), then slide it medially and inferiorly. Identify the sartorius (a triangular muscle), the tensor fasciae latae laterally, and the fascia lata above both.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the LFCN:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The nerve lies in a flat, fat-filled tunnel (the \"fat-filled groove\") between the sartorius and the tensor fasciae latae, deep to the fascia lata, a few centimetres below the ASIS; or superficial to the sartorius closer to the ASIS.</li>\n<li>Trace it proximally towards the inguinal ligament, where it usually passes about 1–4 cm medial to the ASIS (very variable: over the ASIS, through the ligament or through the sartorius in some people), and distally where it divides into anterior and posterior branches.</li>\n<li>It may look hypoechoic, hyperechoic or mixed; scanning up and down (the \"lift\" or \"elevator\" technique) makes it easier to see.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> LFCN cross-sectional area compared with the right side (a larger, swollen nerve proximal to the ligament supports entrapment).</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> the deep circumflex iliac artery (above the ligament) and superficial circumflex iliac vessels; the femoral artery medially (stay away).</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> hip extension and flexion while watching the nerve at the ligament; sonopalpation over the nerve reproduces the tingling (sonographic Tinel's sign).</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> ASIS, inguinal ligament, sartorius, tensor fasciae latae, fascia lata, fascia iliaca, iliacus, LFCN, deep circumflex iliac artery, femoral nerve, artery and vein.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Remove the compression:</strong> swap the duty belt for braces (suspenders) or a load-bearing vest; loosen tight clothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss</strong> and diabetes control.</li>\n<li><strong>Reassurance:</strong> most cases improve within months once the cause is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication for neuropathic symptoms:</strong> topical lidocaine 5% plasters or capsaicin; gabapentin or pregabalin, duloxetine (also helps diabetic neuropathy), or a tricyclic at night.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> stretching of the hip flexors, posture.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided LFCN block</strong> with local anaesthetic and corticosteroid, near the ASIS (below the inguinal ligament), first as a diagnostic block.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Landmark-based LFCN blocks are unreliable because of the variable nerve course: in a cadaver and volunteer study, US identified the nerve in 100% against about 5% with landmarks (Ng 2008).</li>\n<li>US-guided steroid injection reduced pain in all 20 patients in one series at 2 months (Tagliafico 2011); Hurdle 2007 described the technique with good relief.</li>\n<li>Later options: pulsed radiofrequency of the LFCN; cryoneuroablation; peripheral nerve stimulation; surgical neurolysis (decompression) or neurectomy for refractory cases.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies, anticoagulation and blood glucose (diabetes: warn about the steroid glucose rise).</li>\n<li>Supine, hip neutral; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Pre-scan and mark the LFCN between the sartorius and the tensor fasciae latae, and trace it towards the ASIS; colour Doppler for vessels.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25G 38 mm (1.5 inch) out of plane, or 22G 90 mm (3.5 inch) in a very obese patient or in plane from lateral to medial.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> next to the nerve in the fat-filled groove beneath the fascia lata, as proximally as it can be traced (closest to the ligament, where the entrapment is).</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrodissection:</strong> inject a small amount to see the fluid surround the nerve (\"doughnut\").</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 3–5 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine with methylprednisolone 40 mg (Peng uses 3 mL bupivacaine with 40 mg).</li>\n<li>Re-map the numb area after 15–20 minutes: numbness in the whole LFCN territory and relief of burning confirm the block.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Femoral nerve block</strong> if the injection spreads medially or under the fascia iliaca: quadriceps weakness and fall risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular puncture</strong> (circumflex iliac vessels), haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerve injury</strong> or neuritis from intraneural injection.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> raised blood glucose in diabetes (monitor for 2–3 days), flushing, skin depigmentation and fat atrophy (superficial nerve).</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, local anaesthetic toxicity, vasovagal reaction; numbness of the thigh for some hours (expected).</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> relief of burning and allodynia (diagnostic value), the new sensory map, quadriceps power before walking.</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetic glucose monitoring</strong> for 48–72 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain and paraesthesia scores, standing and walking tolerance, the sensory map compared with baseline, medication use.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> continue belt change and weight loss; repeat block if relief was good but temporary; pulsed RF or surgical referral if symptoms persist after 2–3 blocks; if the block did not help, reconsider the spine, plexus or a mass.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not mapping the sensory area, or not checking power and reflexes to exclude L2–L3 radiculopathy and femoral neuropathy.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the belt: the cause has to be removed.</li>\n<li>Using landmarks alone; not tracing the nerve proximally and not knowing its variable course.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the diabetes glucose plan with steroid.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Bhatia A, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided peripheral nerve blockade in chronic pain management. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 25, lateral femoral cutaneous nerve block pp. 243–245.", "Joshi A, Peng P. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 10, pp. 121–128.", "Peng PWH, Narouze S. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part I: nonaxial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:458–474.", "Ng I, Vaghadia H, Choi PT, Helmy N. Ultrasound imaging accurately identifies the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve. <em>Anesth Analg</em> 2008;107:1070–1074.", "Tagliafico A, et al. Ultrasound-guided treatment of meralgia paresthetica (lateral femoral cutaneous neuropathy): technical description and results of treatment in 20 consecutive patients. <em>J Ultrasound Med</em> 2011;30:1341–1346.", "Hurdle MF, et al. Ultrasound-guided blockade of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve: technical description and review of 10 cases. <em>Arch Phys Med Rehabil</em> 2007;88:1362–1364."]}, "15.2": {"title": "Foot pain, ankle osteoarthritis", "vignette": "A 52-year-old construction foreman presents with a 2-year history of right ankle pain. Nine years ago he broke both ankle bones (bimalleolar fracture) in a fall from scaffolding; it was fixed with a plate and screws, later removed. The pain is across the front of the ankle, worst when walking on uneven ground, climbing ladders and squatting, and the ankle feels stiff for 20 minutes in the morning. It swells by the end of the day. He also has an ache below the outer ankle on rough ground, and a patch of burning near the scar on the top of his foot. He smokes 15 cigarettes a day. Paracetamol, ibuprofen and an ankle brace have helped a little. An orthopaedic surgeon has mentioned ankle fusion, but he wants to keep working for another year.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> previous intra-articular fracture, years later (post-traumatic OA), anterior ankle pain, worse with dorsiflexion (ladders, squatting), stiffness, swelling. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> lateral hindfoot ache on rough ground (subtalar joint or sinus tarsi), burning near the scar (superficial peroneal nerve injury or neuroma), smoking (fusion outcome), wish to delay surgery.", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> post-traumatic tibiotalar osteoarthritis with anterior bony impingement (tibial and talar osteophytes).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subtalar joint arthritis or sinus tarsi syndrome (lateral hindfoot pain)</li>\n<li>Superficial peroneal nerve injury or neuroma near the scar (burning)</li>\n<li>Chronic lateral ankle instability; peroneal tendinopathy or tear</li>\n<li>Osteochondral lesion of the talus</li>\n<li>Anterior soft tissue impingement</li>\n<li>Tibialis posterior tendinopathy (medial)</li>\n<li>Inflammatory or crystal arthritis; infection (previous metalwork)</li>\n<li>Lumbar radiculopathy (L4, L5) as a mimic of foot pain</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (alignment, hindfoot varus or valgus from behind, single-leg heel raise), walking (gait, heel and toe walking), then sitting with the legs hanging, then supine.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (knee, ankle); Neurotip and light touch over the superficial and deep peroneal, sural, saphenous and tibial territories; Tinel's sign over the scar; 128 Hz tuning fork and 10 g monofilament (exclude neuropathy in a smoker); MRC power of dorsiflexion, eversion, inversion and plantarflexion.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inspection: scars, swelling, effusion, muscle wasting.</li>\n<li>Range with a goniometer: dorsiflexion and plantarflexion (tibiotalar) compared with the left; inversion and eversion (subtalar) by holding the calcaneus.</li>\n<li>Palpation: anterior joint line, osteophytes, sinus tarsi, peroneal tendons, medial and lateral ligaments.</li>\n<li>Anterior impingement test: forced dorsiflexion reproduces anterior pain.</li>\n<li>Stability: anterior drawer and talar tilt.</li>\n<li>Lumbar screen if the foot pain is not well localised.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>separate tibiotalar movement (dorsiflexion and plantarflexion) from subtalar movement (inversion and eversion). That is how you deal with the lateral hindfoot distractor.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Weight-bearing X-rays:</strong> AP, mortise and lateral of the ankle; foot views for the subtalar joint. Joint space narrowing, osteophytes (anterior tibia and talar neck), malunion, sclerosis, cysts. Classify (for example Kellgren–Lawrence or a Takakura-type grading).</li>\n<li><strong>CT:</strong> to map osteophytes and joint surfaces, and to check the subtalar joint before surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> osteochondral lesion, bone oedema, tendon and ligament problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> effusion and synovitis in the anterior recess, osteophytes, tendons, the superficial peroneal nerve near the scar (neuroma).</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic local anaesthetic injection</strong> of the tibiotalar joint (and separately of the subtalar joint if needed): shows which joint is the pain source, which guides fusion planning.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> only if inflammatory arthritis or infection is suspected (ESR, CRP, uric acid).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 8–15 MHz</strong> probe (hockey stick useful), MSK preset, depth about 3 cm. Patient supine, knee flexed, foot flat on the table (opens the anterior recess); or slight plantarflexion.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> transverse scan above the ankle: tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus tendons, anterior tibial artery and deep peroneal nerve between EHL and EDL; superficial peroneal nerve in the subcutaneous tissue laterally.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the anterior tibiotalar joint:</strong> sagittal scan medial to the tibialis anterior tendon: distal tibia, talar dome with its hypoechoic cartilage, the anterior recess and the anterior fat pad. Look for effusion and synovial thickening, osteophytes on the tibia and talar neck.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth of the anterior recess; up to about 3 mm of fluid can be normal. Compare with the left ankle.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour Doppler to locate the anterior tibial (dorsalis pedis) artery and to look for synovitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> passive dorsiflexion and plantarflexion: watch the recess fill and empty and the osteophytes meet (impingement); eversion and inversion while scanning the sinus tarsi for the subtalar joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Also scan:</strong> sinus tarsi and lateral subtalar joint, peroneal tendons, the superficial peroneal nerve along the scar.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> tibia, talar dome and cartilage, anterior recess, anterior fat pad, tibialis anterior, EHL and EDL tendons, anterior tibial artery, deep peroneal nerve, superficial peroneal nerve, medial malleolus, lateral malleolus.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Activity and work adaptation:</strong> fewer ladders and uneven ground where possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Footwear:</strong> rocker-bottom soles, cushioned heel, stiff-soled boots; ankle brace or a lace-up support; ankle–foot orthosis for severe pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> strengthening, proprioception and balance, maintaining range.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> paracetamol; topical or short oral NSAIDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Weight management; smoking cessation</strong> (also improves fusion outcome).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided intra-articular tibiotalar joint injection of corticosteroid and local anaesthetic, anterior approach medial to the tibialis anterior tendon.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diagnostic and therapeutic. Relief after local anaesthetic confirms the tibiotalar joint as the main pain source.</li>\n<li>US-guided accuracy is about 100% (Soneji and Peng 2016 review; Wisniewski 2010 cadaver study), better than landmark injections.</li>\n<li>Entering medial to tibialis anterior keeps the needle away from the deep peroneal nerve and the anterior tibial (dorsalis pedis) artery.</li>\n<li>Later options: subtalar injection if the hindfoot pain persists; superficial peroneal nerve block or hydrodissection for the scar pain; hyaluronic acid or PRP (limited evidence); arthroscopic debridement of osteophytes; ankle fusion or replacement.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies, anticoagulation, glucose and skin over the old scar.</li>\n<li>Supine, knee flexed, foot flat on the table (or the heel at the edge of the table with gentle plantarflexion); machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Pre-scan: identify the tibialis anterior tendon, EHL, anterior tibial artery and deep peroneal nerve with colour Doppler.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Probe sagittal, just medial to the tibialis anterior tendon, showing the tibia, talar dome and anterior recess.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22–25G 38 mm, in plane from distal to proximal, or out of plane from medial; keep a fairly steep angle so the tip does not scrape the talar cartilage.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the anterior recess under the capsule, between the tibia and the talar dome.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> the recess distends and the anterior fat pad lifts; fluid tracking in the subcutaneous tissue or tendon sheath means extra-articular.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> triamcinolone or methylprednisolone 40 mg with 2–3 mL of 1% lidocaine (or 0.25% bupivacaine); total about 3–4 mL.</li>\n<li>Re-test walking, squatting and dorsiflexion pain after 10–15 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Neurovascular:</strong> injury to the deep peroneal nerve or anterior tibial artery (avoid by staying medial to tibialis anterior); superficial peroneal nerve injury (laterally).</li>\n<li><strong>Joint:</strong> septic arthritis (rare; higher after previous surgery), cartilage injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Tendon:</strong> injection into the tibialis anterior tendon or sheath.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> flare, raised glucose, flushing; skin depigmentation (thin skin over the ankle).</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, allergy; temporary numbness of the dorsum of the foot.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> percentage relief on walking and squatting (diagnostic value for fusion planning); foot sensation and dorsiflexion power; safe walking.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-net:</strong> fever, a hot swollen ankle means urgent review.</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain, walking distance, ability to work (ladders), function score (for example the Foot and Ankle Ability Measure or the AOFAS score).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> repeat no sooner than 3 months; continue bracing, footwear and smoking cessation; refer for surgery when injections no longer give useful relief; avoid steroid within 3 months before fusion or replacement.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Entering lateral to the tibialis anterior, close to the deep peroneal nerve and artery.</li>\n<li>Not separating tibiotalar from subtalar pain.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the scar pain (superficial peroneal nerve).</li>\n<li>Not linking the diagnostic block to surgical planning.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Soneji N, Peng P. Ankle joint and nerves. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 24, tibiotalar joint pp. 310–314.", "Vadada K, Chang RG, Sahler C, Kirschner JS. Foot and ankle. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 7, pp. 69–70.", "Soneji N, Peng PW. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part VI: ankle joint. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2016;41:99–116.", "Wisniewski SJ, et al. Ultrasound-guided versus nonguided tibiotalar joint and sinus tarsi injections: a cadaveric study. <em>PM R</em> 2010;2:277–281.", "Reach JS, et al. Accuracy of ultrasound guided injections in the foot and ankle. <em>Foot Ankle Int</em> 2009;30:239–242."]}, "16.1": {"title": "MSK pain and disability, lateral epicondylosis", "vignette": "A 45-year-old right-handed female carpenter presents with a 9-month history of pain over the outer side of the right elbow. It came on gradually after a large kitchen-fitting job using a screwdriver and hammer all day. It hurts when she grips tools, lifts a kettle or shakes hands, and the pain spreads into the back of the forearm. There is no neck pain. Two months ago she had a \"cortisone shot\" in the elbow from her family doctor that worked very well for 4 weeks, but the pain is now worse than before. She has also noticed that the elbow aches at night. A forearm strap helps a little. She asks for another steroid injection because \"the last one was magic\".", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> age 35–55, repetitive gripping and wrist extension, lateral elbow pain with grip, radiation into the dorsal forearm, chronic course (more than 6 months). <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> the excellent short response then relapse after steroid (classic pattern, and a reason not to repeat it), night ache (think posterior interosseous nerve, radial tunnel, or joint pathology), forearm radiation (radial tunnel syndrome, C6–C7 radiculopathy).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> chronic lateral elbow tendinopathy (epicondylosis) of the common extensor origin, mainly ECRB, made worse after a steroid injection (the rebound after initial relief).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Partial or full-thickness tear of the common extensor tendon (after steroid)</li>\n<li>Radial tunnel syndrome (posterior interosseous nerve compression at the arcade of Frohse): pain 3–4 cm distal to the epicondyle, worse with resisted supination and middle finger extension</li>\n<li>Radiocapitellar joint pathology: osteoarthritis, synovial plica, osteochondral lesion, loose body</li>\n<li>Lateral collateral ligament complex injury or posterolateral rotatory instability</li>\n<li>C6 or C7 radiculopathy</li>\n<li>Referred pain from the shoulder; inflammatory arthritis</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, both arms exposed to the shoulder, forearm supported on a table.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> swelling, skin atrophy or depigmentation at the previous injection site, muscle wasting, carrying angle.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (biceps, brachioradialis, triceps); Neurotip and light touch in C6–C8 and the superficial radial nerve; MRC power of wrist and finger extensors, supinator; cervical range and Spurling test. Grip strength with a <strong>dynamometer</strong>, elbow extended and flexed, compared with the left (pain-free grip strength is a useful outcome measure).</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpation: point tenderness 1 cm distal and anterior to the lateral epicondyle (ECRB origin); radial tunnel (3–4 cm distal, over the supinator); radiocapitellar joint line.</li>\n<li><strong>Provocative tests:</strong> Cozen test (resisted wrist extension with the elbow extended), Mill test (passive wrist flexion with the elbow extended and forearm pronated), Maudsley test (resisted middle finger extension), chair lift test.</li>\n<li><strong>Radial tunnel:</strong> resisted supination, Tinel's sign over the arcade of Frohse.</li>\n<li>Elbow range, stability tests (varus stress, lateral pivot shift).</li>\n<li>Neck and shoulder screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>use a grip dynamometer and say \"pain-free grip strength\". It is both a diagnostic finding and your outcome measure.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical diagnosis</strong> in most cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> common extensor tendon thickening, hypoechoic areas, loss of fibrillar pattern, intrasubstance or partial tears, calcification, cortical irregularity of the epicondyle, Doppler neovascularity; compare with the left. Tears and severity affect outcome and treatment choice.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> to exclude radiocapitellar OA, loose bodies, calcification.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> if symptoms persist, a tear or ligament injury is suspected, or before surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerve conduction studies</strong> for suspected PIN entrapment (often normal in radial tunnel syndrome) or radiculopathy.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 10–15 MHz probe</strong> (hockey stick useful), MSK superficial preset, depth 2–3 cm. Patient seated, elbow flexed 90°, forearm pronated on the table (\"praying\" or \"table-top\" position).</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> anterior elbow (joint recess, biceps tendon), medial (common flexor origin, ulnar nerve), posterior (triceps, olecranon fossa), then lateral.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the lateral elbow:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Long axis (coronal):</strong> proximal end of the probe on the lateral epicondyle: the common extensor tendon as a beak-shaped band, the radial collateral ligament deep to it, the radial head and capitellum (radiocapitellar joint).</li>\n<li><strong>Short axis:</strong> rotate 90° to see the whole width of the tendon.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> tendon thickness at the origin, compared with the left.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> power Doppler (low flow settings, no probe pressure) for neovascularity in the tendon; its presence supports active tendinopathy.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> resisted wrist extension or passive flexion while watching the tendon (tears gap); varus stress for ligament laxity; forearm rotation to scan the radial head and the PIN at the supinator.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> lateral epicondyle, common extensor tendon (ECRB, extensor digitorum, ECU), ECRL, radial collateral ligament, annular ligament, radial head, capitellum, supinator, posterior interosseous nerve, brachioradialis.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and activity modification:</strong> about 80–90% recover within a year; use tools with larger grips, lift with palm up, avoid prolonged gripping.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy (first line):</strong> progressive loading (isometric, then eccentric and heavy slow resistance) of the wrist extensors, plus elbow manipulation. Physiotherapy beat steroid at 1 year in Bisset 2006.</li>\n<li><strong>Counterforce brace</strong> (forearm strap) or wrist splint in extension.</li>\n<li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> topical NSAIDs (good evidence short term), short oral NSAIDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Shockwave therapy:</strong> mixed evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid repeat steroid:</strong> short-term benefit but worse outcomes and more recurrence at 1 year (Coombes 2013).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided percutaneous needle tenotomy (fenestration) of the common extensor origin,</strong> with or without <strong>platelet-rich plasma (PRP)</strong> injection, combined with a loading programme.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corticosteroid gives the fastest short-term relief (up to 6 weeks) but higher recurrence and worse 1-year outcomes than physiotherapy or wait-and-see (Bisset 2006; Coombes 2013). She has already shown this pattern.</li>\n<li>PRP has shown better results than control at 6 months in a large RCT (Mishra 2014); evidence overall is mixed.</li>\n<li>US guidance places the needle in the abnormal tendon areas and shows tears.</li>\n<li>If she insists on steroid, use a small dose around (not into) the tendon, once only, explaining the trade-off.</li>\n<li>Later options: tenotomy devices (percutaneous ultrasonic tenotomy); surgical release or debridement after 6–12 months of failed treatment.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent (including post-procedure pain for several days); stop NSAIDs about 1 week before PRP (they may reduce platelet function); check allergies and anticoagulation.</li>\n<li>Seated, elbow flexed 90°, forearm pronated on the table; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear probe in long axis over the common extensor origin; identify the hypoechoic or torn areas and Doppler vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Local anaesthetic:</strong> 1–2 mL 1% lidocaine to the skin and superficial to the tendon (not into it, if PRP follows).</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22–25G 38 mm, in plane from distal to proximal.</li>\n<li><strong>Fenestration:</strong> with the tip in the abnormal area, pass the needle repeatedly (about 20–40 passes) through the degenerative tendon, fanning across its width, touching the bone of the epicondyle (abrading the footprint) without leaving the skin.</li>\n<li><strong>PRP (if used):</strong> 2–3 mL of leukocyte-rich PRP injected into the fenestrated area.</li>\n<li><strong>If steroid is chosen instead:</strong> 10–20 mg triamcinolone with 1 mL lidocaine, peritendinous (between the tendon and the overlying fascia), not into the tendon.</li>\n<li>Plaster, ice; no NSAIDs after PRP for about 2 weeks.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Common:</strong> post-procedure pain flare for 2–7 days (especially after fenestration and PRP); swelling, bruising.</li>\n<li><strong>Tendon:</strong> weakening or rupture (steroid into the tendon, repeated steroid); post-steroid recurrence.</li>\n<li><strong>Skin:</strong> subcutaneous fat atrophy and depigmentation (steroid; thin lateral elbow skin, already visible in some patients).</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> injury to the posterior interosseous or lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve (rare).</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, vasovagal reaction, lateral collateral ligament injury from aggressive fenestration (instability).</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> pain, wound check; advice: relative rest for 1–2 days, then gentle activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation:</strong> start isometric loading after about 1 week, then progressive eccentric strengthening; return to heavy gripping work in stages over 4–8 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome measures at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months:</strong> pain-free grip strength (dynamometer), PRTEE score (Patient-Rated Tennis Elbow Evaluation), return to work.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound follow-up</strong> if slow progress: tendon healing, tears.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> a second PRP or fenestration at 6–8 weeks if partial response; surgical referral after 6–12 months of failed treatment; avoid repeated steroid.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Offering another steroid injection without discussing the Coombes 2013 and Bisset 2006 evidence.</li>\n<li>Not separating tennis elbow from radial tunnel syndrome (point of tenderness, resisted supination).</li>\n<li>Not using a grip dynamometer.</li>\n<li>Injecting steroid into the tendon instead of around it.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Bodor M, Lesher JM, Colio S. Ultrasound-guided hand, wrist, and elbow injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 27, lateral epicondylitis pp. 273–274.", "Bodor M, Colio S, Khan J, Raj M. Ultrasound-guided injections for elbow pain. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 20, pp. 233–247.", "Lin E, Aligene K, Kirschner JS. Elbow. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 3, pp. 17–28.", "Jacobson JA. Elbow ultrasound. In: <em>Fundamentals of Musculoskeletal Ultrasound</em>, 3rd ed. Elsevier; 2017. Ch. 4.", "Coombes BK, et al. Effect of corticosteroid injection, physiotherapy, or both on clinical outcomes in patients with unilateral lateral epicondylalgia: a randomized controlled trial. <em>JAMA</em> 2013;309:461–469.", "Bisset L, et al. Mobilisation with movement and exercise, corticosteroid injection, or wait and see for tennis elbow: randomised trial. <em>BMJ</em> 2006;333:939.", "Mishra AK, et al. Efficacy of platelet-rich plasma for chronic tennis elbow: a double-blind, prospective, multicenter, randomized controlled trial of 230 patients. <em>Am J Sports Med</em> 2014;42:463–471."]}, "16.2": {"title": "MSK pain and disability, cubital tunnel syndrome", "vignette": "A 52-year-old male long-distance truck driver presents with a 4-month history of tingling and numbness in the left little finger and the little-finger side of the ring finger, with an ache along the inner side of the elbow and forearm. Symptoms are worse when he drives with his left elbow resting on the window ledge, when he talks on the phone for long periods, and at night when he sleeps with his arms bent. He has noticed his left hand is clumsier when doing up buttons and opening jars. He also has some neck stiffness. He has type 2 diabetes and had a left elbow fracture as a child. His family doctor started him on gabapentin 300 mg at night, which helps him sleep but not the tingling.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> ulnar 1½ fingers, medial elbow and forearm ache, worse with prolonged elbow flexion and direct pressure (window ledge, phone, sleep), early motor symptoms (clumsiness), old elbow fracture (cubitus valgus, \"tardy ulnar palsy\"), diabetes. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> neck stiffness (C8 radiculopathy), diabetes (polyneuropathy), clumsiness (Guyon's canal, thoracic outlet; also signals the need for nerve conduction studies and a lower threshold for surgery).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> left ulnar neuropathy at the elbow (cubital tunnel syndrome), from compression and traction with elbow flexion, possibly made worse by a valgus deformity from the childhood fracture and by diabetes.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>C8 (or T1) radiculopathy</li>\n<li>Ulnar neuropathy at the wrist (Guyon's canal): spares the dorsal hand sensation and FCU/FDP to the little finger</li>\n<li>Thoracic outlet syndrome (neurogenic, lower trunk): medial forearm numbness, wasting of thenar muscles</li>\n<li>Lower trunk brachial plexopathy; Pancoast tumour (red flag)</li>\n<li>Diabetic polyneuropathy (symmetric, distal)</li>\n<li>Ulnar nerve subluxation or snapping triceps</li>\n<li>Medial epicondylosis (golfer's elbow) causing medial elbow pain</li>\n<li>Motor neurone disease (weakness without sensory loss)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, both arms exposed, forearms supported; then the elbows flexed and extended.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> carrying angle (cubitus valgus from the old fracture), wasting of the first dorsal interosseous and hypothenar muscles, clawing of the ring and little fingers, scars.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensory:</strong> Neurotip and cotton wool over the little finger, ulnar half of the ring finger, <strong>dorsal ulnar hand</strong> (dorsal cutaneous branch: spared in Guyon's canal lesions, affected at the elbow) and the medial forearm (medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve, C8–T1: affected in C8 or plexus lesions, spared in ulnar neuropathy); two-point discrimination; 10 g monofilament.</li>\n<li><strong>Motor (MRC):</strong> first dorsal interosseous, abductor digiti minimi, FDP to the little finger, FCU; grip and pinch strength with a dynamometer.</li>\n<li><strong>Signs:</strong> Froment's sign (thumb IP flexion on key pinch), Wartenberg's sign (little finger abducted), card test.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflexes</strong> with a hammer: triceps (C7), finger flexor (C8).</li>\n<li>Cervical range and Spurling; Roos (elevated arm stress) test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Provocative tests at the elbow:</strong> Tinel's sign over the cubital tunnel; elbow flexion test (full flexion with wrist extension for 60 seconds); combined elbow flexion and pressure test (most sensitive); scratch collapse test.</li>\n<li>Palpate the nerve in the groove while flexing and extending the elbow (subluxation, snapping).</li>\n<li>Elbow range, valgus stress; medial epicondyle tenderness (golfer's elbow).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>check the dorsal ulnar hand and the medial forearm. Together they localise the lesion to the elbow rather than the wrist or the C8 root.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Nerve conduction studies and EMG (essential with motor symptoms):</strong> slowing of ulnar motor conduction across the elbow (below about 50 m/s, or a drop of more than about 10 m/s compared with the forearm segment), conduction block, reduced sensory amplitudes; EMG of FDI and ADM for denervation. Also screens for diabetic polyneuropathy and C8 radiculopathy.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> ulnar nerve cross-sectional area at the medial epicondyle (above about 10 mm² is abnormal), focal swelling proximal to the cubital tunnel, a ratio against the mid-arm, subluxation on flexion, anconeus epitrochlearis muscle, osteophytes or ganglion.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray elbow:</strong> cubitus valgus, old fracture malunion, osteophytes, loose bodies.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI cervical spine</strong> if radicular features; chest X-ray if lower trunk or Pancoast features.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> HbA1c, B12, TSH.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 10–18 MHz probe</strong> (hockey stick useful), small-parts or nerve preset, depth 2 cm. Patient supine with the arm abducted and externally rotated, elbow slightly flexed (\"hand behind head\" or arm across the chest), or seated with the elbow on a table.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> scan the ulnar nerve in short axis from the mid-arm (medial to the brachial artery, then behind the medial intermuscular septum) down to the forearm between the two heads of the FCU.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the cubital tunnel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short axis between the <strong>medial epicondyle</strong> and the <strong>olecranon</strong>: the nerve is an oval honeycomb structure in the groove, under the cubital tunnel retinaculum (Osborne's ligament), with the FCU aponeurosis distally.</li>\n<li>Long axis: focal swelling just proximal to the tunnel and narrowing within it (notch sign).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> CSA at the medial epicondyle, at the mid-arm and in the forearm; compare with the right side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> intraneural hyperaemia in an inflamed nerve; posterior ulnar recurrent artery near the nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> flex the elbow fully while scanning in short axis: the nerve may sublux over the tip of the medial epicondyle (and a snapping medial triceps may follow it).</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> medial epicondyle, olecranon, ulnar nerve, cubital tunnel retinaculum (Osborne's ligament), two heads of the FCU, medial head of the triceps, ulnar collateral ligament, common flexor origin.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and posture:</strong> avoid leaning on the elbow (padded arm rest in the truck), avoid prolonged elbow flexion, use a headset for phone calls.</li>\n<li><strong>Night splint</strong> holding the elbow at about 30–45° flexion (or a towel wrapped around the elbow) for 3 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Elbow pad</strong> for daytime pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerve gliding exercises.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> continue or adjust a neuropathic agent (gabapentin, pregabalin, duloxetine) for pain and sleep; optimise diabetes control.</li>\n<li>Most mild cases improve over 3–6 months; with motor signs, review early (Cochrane 2016).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided perineural injection and hydrodissection of the ulnar nerve at the cubital tunnel,</strong> releasing the nerve from the retinaculum, with local anaesthetic and either a low-dose steroid or 5% dextrose.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evidence is limited: a placebo-controlled RCT of US-guided steroid injection found no added benefit over placebo at 3 months (van Veen 2015). Hydrodissection with 5% dextrose is being studied. Explain this honestly.</li>\n<li>The value of the injection is as a diagnostic and short-term option, and in patients who cannot or will not have surgery.</li>\n<li>US is essential: the nerve may sublux, and blind injection can be intraneural.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical referral</strong> (simple in situ decompression, or anterior transposition if the nerve subluxes or there is a valgus deformity) for weakness, wasting, denervation on EMG, or failure of 3 months of conservative care. Simple decompression and transposition have similar results (Cochrane 2016). Given his motor symptoms and the old fracture, early surgical opinion is reasonable.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; explain the limited evidence and the temporary numbness; check allergies and anticoagulation; glucose plan if steroid is used.</li>\n<li>Supine, arm abducted and externally rotated with the elbow slightly flexed (avoid full flexion, which can sublux the nerve); machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Pre-scan with dynamic flexion to check for subluxation; colour Doppler.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear probe in short axis over the nerve just proximal to the cubital tunnel (or within it).</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25–27G 38 mm, in plane from lateral (or posterior) to medial in short axis, approaching the nerve from below or beside, away from the medial epicondyle.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the perineural space, between the nerve and the retinaculum; do not enter the nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrodissection:</strong> inject in small aliquots to lift the retinaculum off the nerve and surround it with fluid (\"doughnut\"); stop if there is sharp paraesthesia or high resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 3–5 mL total, for example 1% lidocaine with 10–20 mg triamcinolone, or 5% dextrose for hydrodissection.</li>\n<li>Check sensation and power after 15 minutes; advise no driving while the hand is numb.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Nerve:</strong> intraneural injection, nerve injury, worsening neuropathy or neuritis; transient numbness and weakness of the hand.</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> posterior ulnar recurrent artery puncture, haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> fat atrophy and depigmentation over the superficial medial elbow, raised glucose (diabetes), flushing.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, local anaesthetic toxicity, vasovagal reaction, flare of pain.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> sensation and power (expected local anaesthetic effect), no new sharp neuropathic pain; no driving with a numb hand (he is a professional driver).</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes:</strong> glucose monitoring for 48–72 hours if steroid was used.</li>\n<li><strong>At 6 weeks and 3 months:</strong> symptoms (paraesthesia, night waking), two-point discrimination, FDI and ADM power, grip and pinch strength, Froment's sign, function (QuickDASH); ultrasound CSA and repeat NCS if not improving.</li>\n<li><strong>Escalate:</strong> surgical referral for progressive weakness, wasting, denervation on EMG or lack of improvement after 3 months; continue splinting and pressure avoidance in the meantime.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not localising the lesion (dorsal ulnar hand and medial forearm sensation; FCU and FDP power).</li>\n<li>Not ordering nerve conduction studies when there are motor symptoms.</li>\n<li>Not doing the dynamic flexion test for nerve subluxation before injecting.</li>\n<li>Overstating the evidence for injection; not mentioning surgery for motor deficit.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Bodor M, Colio S, Khan J, Raj M. Ultrasound-guided injections for elbow pain. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 20, medial elbow and ulnar nerve pp. 238–239.", "Deshmukh S, Samet J. Ultrasonography of peripheral nerves. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 30, ulnar nerve at the elbow pp. 290–291.", "Lin E, Aligene K, Kirschner JS. Elbow. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 3, pp. 17–28.", "Peer S, Gruber H. <em>Atlas of Peripheral Nerve Ultrasound</em>. Springer; 2013 (ulnar nerve chapter).", "Caliandro P, et al. Treatment for ulnar neuropathy at the elbow. <em>Cochrane Database Syst Rev</em> 2016;(11):CD006839.", "van Veen KE, et al. Corticosteroid injection in patients with ulnar neuropathy at the elbow: a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial. <em>Muscle Nerve</em> 2015;52:380–385.", "Chang KV, Wu WT, Han DS, Özçakar L. Ulnar nerve cross-sectional area for the diagnosis of cubital tunnel syndrome: a meta-analysis of ultrasonographic measurements. <em>Arch Phys Med Rehabil</em> 2018;99:743–757."]}, "2.1": {"title": "Cervicogenic headache, third occipital nerve (C2–C3)", "vignette": "A 47-year-old male bus driver was rear-ended at traffic lights 2 years ago. Since then he has had a right-sided headache almost every day. It starts in the upper neck and the back of the head and spreads to the right forehead and around the eye. It never changes side. It is brought on by turning his head to check his mirrors and by long shifts, and it eases a little with rest. He has stiffness and reduced turning to the right. Sometimes bright light bothers him during the worst attacks. He also notices tingling over the back of the scalp. He takes paracetamol and ibuprofen on most days of the month. Physiotherapy helped for a short time.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> whiplash, unilateral side-locked headache that starts in the neck and spreads forward, triggered by neck movement and sustained posture, reduced neck rotation. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> photophobia (migraine), occipital scalp tingling (occipital neuralgia, greater occipital nerve), analgesics on most days (medication-overuse headache).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> cervicogenic headache (ICHD-3 11.2.1), with the right C2–C3 zygapophysial joint as the probable source (third occipital headache). The C2–C3 joint is the most common source of headache after whiplash (Lord 1994). Pain from the upper cervical joints is referred to the head through the trigeminocervical nucleus.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Other upper cervical sources: C1–C2 (lateral atlanto-axial) joint, C2–C3 disc, C3 nerve root</li>\n<li>Occipital neuralgia (greater, lesser or third occipital nerve)</li>\n<li>Migraine without aura; hemicrania continua (responds to indomethacin)</li>\n<li>Tension-type headache</li>\n<li>Medication-overuse headache (analgesics on 15 or more days a month)</li>\n<li>Red flags to exclude: vertebral artery dissection after trauma, posterior fossa lesion, Chiari malformation, giant cell arteritis (over 50)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated for neck movements, then prone with the forehead supported for palpation.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cranial nerves, fundoscopy (exclude raised intracranial pressure).</li>\n<li><strong>Sensation over the occiput:</strong> cotton wool and Neurotip in the GON, LON and TON territories.</li>\n<li><strong>Tinel's sign</strong> over the greater occipital nerve (at the superior nuchal line, medial to the occipital artery).</li>\n<li>Upper limb dermatomes, myotomes and reflexes (reflex hammer); Hoffmann's sign; gait.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active range of motion</strong> of the cervical spine; a reduced range is part of the ICHD-3 criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Flexion–rotation test</strong> (reduced rotation of more than 10° in full flexion suggests C1–C2 dysfunction).</li>\n<li><strong>Palpation</strong> of the C2–C3 joint (manual pressure reproduces the usual headache) and the suboccipital muscles; trigger points in trapezius, sternocleidomastoid and splenius capitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Spurling's test</strong> (radiculopathy); Valsalva.</li>\n<li>Craniocervical flexion test (deep neck flexor endurance); posture.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>reproduce the headache by pressure on the C2–C3 joint and show reduced rotation. Mention the ICHD-3 criteria.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Headache diary</strong> (frequency, triggers, analgesic days) to identify medication overuse.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI of the cervical spine</strong> (and brain if there are red flags); CT or MR angiography if dissection is a concern.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray</strong> with flexion and extension views if instability is possible after trauma.</li>\n<li><strong>The diagnostic test is a block:</strong> controlled (comparative) TON blocks with local anaesthetics of different duration. Imaging changes in the facet joints correlate poorly with pain.</li>\n<li>Bloods (ESR, CRP) only if giant cell arteritis is possible.</li>\n<li>Outcome measures: Neck Disability Index, HIT-6, headache days per month.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong>, depth 3–4 cm. Patient lateral (right side up) or prone.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview (posterior, long axis):</strong> begin at the occiput and move caudally in the midline: C1 has no spinous process (posterior tubercle only); <strong>C2 has the first and largest bifid spinous process</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Level identification (lateral, coronal plane):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Probe over the mastoid process, then caudally: <strong>C1 transverse process</strong> and the <strong>vertebral artery</strong> between C1 and C2 (colour Doppler).</li>\n<li>Further caudal: the <strong>C2 articular pillar</strong>, then the <strong>C2–C3 joint</strong> (a gap between two hyperechoic bony lines), then C3 and C4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> the <strong>third occipital nerve</strong> crosses the lateral surface of the C2–C3 joint as a small hyperechoic oval structure (it is the superficial medial branch of C3). The deep medial branch of C3 lies at the C3 waist.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth from skin to the C2–C3 joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> vertebral artery near C1–C2, the deep cervical artery and veins over the pillars.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> gentle rotation shows the joint gap moving; confirm the level by counting from C2 again.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> mastoid process, C1 transverse process, vertebral artery, C2 spinous process (bifid), C2 articular pillar, C2–C3 joint, third occipital nerve, C3 medial branch, splenius capitis, semispinalis capitis, longissimus capitis, levator scapulae.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education</strong> and reassurance; stop analgesic overuse (withdrawal plan).</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> manual therapy (mobilisation of the upper cervical spine) together with specific exercise (deep neck flexors, scapular muscles); this combination has the best evidence (Jull 2002).</li>\n<li>Posture and work adjustments (seat, mirrors, breaks during long shifts).</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> short-term NSAID; amitriptyline at night (also helps sleep); avoid opioids.</li>\n<li>Psychological support: pain coping, sleep, mood after the accident.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided TON block (diagnostic), then RF neurotomy of the third occipital nerve</strong> if two comparative blocks each give at least 80% relief of the usual headache.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RF neurotomy of the TON gave complete relief in most patients with third occipital headache in an uncontrolled series (Govind 2003). A single block has a high false-positive rate, so comparative blocks are needed.</li>\n<li>US identifies the TON over the C2–C3 joint and was validated against fluoroscopy (Eichenberger 2006). The RF itself is usually done under fluoroscopy, with three lesions along the joint.</li>\n<li>Alternatives: GON block (if occipital neuralgia is present), C2–C3 intra-articular steroid injection (short-term).</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; stop analgesics on the day so the response can be judged; record a baseline pain score and the provoking movement.</li>\n<li>Lateral position with the right side up (or prone); machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Count the levels from C1 and C2; place the probe in the coronal plane over the C2–C3 joint; colour Doppler for the vertebral artery and other vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25G 50 mm, in plane from posterior to anterior, to the hyperechoic TON over the joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 0.5 mL of 2% lidocaine for the first block; 0.5 mL of 0.5% bupivacaine on another day for the second (comparative). No steroid in a diagnostic block.</li>\n<li>Low volume, so spread does not reach other nerves.</li>\n<li>Reassess after 15–30 minutes: pain at rest and with the provoking movement (head turning); patient diary for the duration of relief.</li>\n<li><strong>RF neurotomy</strong> (fluoroscopy): three lesions (80°C, 90 s each) along the TON over the C2–C3 joint, after motor stimulation at 2 Hz to exclude root stimulation.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Block:</strong> vertebral artery injection (seizure), spread to the C2 or C3 roots (temporary numbness or weakness), ataxia and dizziness (block of upper cervical proprioception), vasovagal reaction, infection, haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>RF neurotomy of the TON:</strong> numbness of the suboccipital area (expected), dysaesthesia and itching in that area, ataxia and unsteadiness for some weeks, neuritis, rarely injury to the C3 root.</li>\n<li>Intrathecal or epidural injection (rare).</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Was the block positive?</strong> At least 80% relief of the usual headache, with a duration that matches the local anaesthetic used (lidocaine shorter, bupivacaine longer). A concordant response to both blocks supports RF.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety:</strong> dizziness, ataxia and gait before discharge; no driving on the day (he is a bus driver: advise about work).</li>\n<li><strong>After RF:</strong> warn about numbness, itching and unsteadiness; relief often lasts many months, and the procedure can be repeated when the nerve regenerates.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation:</strong> physiotherapy and exercise in the pain-free window; plan for medication-overuse withdrawal.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up:</strong> headache diary, headache days per month, Neck Disability Index, analgesic use.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not counting the levels from C1 and C2 and not identifying the C2–C3 joint.</li>\n<li>Not using colour Doppler for the vertebral artery at C1–C2.</li>\n<li>Not explaining comparative (double) blocks and the 80% relief cut-off before RF.</li>\n<li>Not recognising medication-overuse headache.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Siegenthaler A, Eichenberger U. Ultrasound-guided third occipital nerve and cervical medial branch nerve blocks. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 6, pp. 83–90.", "Etheridge J-PB, Finlayson R. Cervical medial branch and third occipital nerve blocks. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 13, pp. 157–167.", "Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society. The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition. <em>Cephalalgia</em> 2018;38:1–211.", "Lord SM, Barnsley L, Wallis BJ, Bogduk N. Third occipital nerve headache: a prevalence study. <em>J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry</em> 1994;57:1187–1190.", "Govind J, King W, Bailey B, Bogduk N. Radiofrequency neurotomy for the treatment of third occipital headache. <em>J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry</em> 2003;74:88–93.", "Eichenberger U, Greher M, Kapral S, et al. Sonographic visualization and ultrasound-guided block of the third occipital nerve. <em>Anesthesiology</em> 2006;104:303–308.", "Jull G, Trott P, Potter H, et al. A randomized controlled trial of exercise and manipulative therapy for cervicogenic headache. <em>Spine</em> 2002;27:1835–1843."]}, "3.1": {"title": "Cervical radicular pain, C6 radiculopathy", "vignette": "A 47-year-old right-handed female dentist presents with an 8-week history of pain in the right side of the neck that spreads over the shoulder blade, down the outer arm and forearm to the thumb and index finger. The arm pain is much worse than the neck pain and is 8/10 at night. Tilting her head back and to the right, for example when working over a patient, makes it shoot down the arm; resting her right hand on top of her head eases it. She has pins and needles in the thumb and has noticed her grip weakening when holding instruments. She has no problems with walking, balance or fine hand movements on the left, and no bladder change. Naproxen and a week of prednisolone helped briefly; gabapentin made her too sleepy to work. Her MRI shows a right posterolateral C5–C6 disc protrusion narrowing the foramen.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> dermatomal arm pain worse than neck pain, C6 distribution (lateral arm and forearm, thumb, index), Spurling-like provocation (extension and ipsilateral tilt), shoulder abduction relief sign, paraesthesia, mild weakness, MRI concordant at C5–C6 (the C6 root). <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> scapular pain (shoulder pathology), thumb tingling (carpal tunnel syndrome, a \"double crush\"), weak grip (C7 or C8, or median nerve), the occupation (sustained neck extension).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> right C6 radiculopathy from a C5–C6 foraminal disc herniation.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>C5 or C7 radiculopathy (adjacent level, or a different foraminal level)</li>\n<li>Cervical spondylotic myelopathy (red flag: gait, hand clumsiness, hyperreflexia)</li>\n<li>Carpal tunnel syndrome, or a double crush with C6 radiculopathy</li>\n<li>Shoulder pathology: rotator cuff disease, frozen shoulder (referred pain)</li>\n<li>Brachial plexopathy: neuralgic amyotrophy (Parsonage–Turner), thoracic outlet syndrome</li>\n<li>Cervical facet pain with somatic referral (no neurological signs)</li>\n<li>Pancoast tumour, metastasis, infection (red flags)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, neck and both arms exposed; then standing for gait.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reflex hammer:</strong> biceps (C5), brachioradialis (C6), triceps (C7), finger flexors (C8); Hoffmann sign, lower limb reflexes, plantar responses (myelopathy screen).</li>\n<li><strong>Sensory:</strong> Neurotip and cotton wool in C5–T1 dermatomes, compared with the left; two-point discrimination in the thumb.</li>\n<li><strong>MRC power:</strong> shoulder abduction (C5), elbow flexion and wrist extension (C6), elbow extension and wrist flexion (C7), finger flexion (C8), finger abduction (T1); grip with a dynamometer.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural provocation:</strong> Spurling test, upper limb tension test (ULTT1, median bias), shoulder abduction relief sign, neck distraction test. A cluster (Wainner) of positive Spurling, ULTT, distraction and cervical rotation under 60° makes radiculopathy very likely.</li>\n<li><strong>Gait and tandem walking</strong> (myelopathy).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervical range in all planes.</li>\n<li>Shoulder: range, impingement and cuff tests.</li>\n<li>Wrist: Tinel and Phalen (carpal tunnel).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>do the myelopathy screen (Hoffmann, gait, lower limb reflexes) even in a straightforward radiculopathy. It is the red flag that changes management.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>MRI cervical spine</strong> (done): level, side, foraminal versus central stenosis, cord signal change.</li>\n<li><strong>CT</strong> if bony foraminal stenosis (uncovertebral osteophytes) matters for surgical planning.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray</strong> with flexion–extension for instability.</li>\n<li><strong>Nerve conduction studies and EMG:</strong> if the diagnosis is uncertain, to exclude carpal tunnel syndrome or plexopathy, and to show denervation.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods</strong> only with red flags.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic selective nerve root block</strong> with local anaesthetic: confirms the symptomatic level when MRI shows changes at more than one level.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome measures:</strong> arm and neck pain NRS separately, Neck Disability Index.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong> (curvilinear for a short, thick neck), small-parts preset, depth 3–4 cm. Patient lateral decubitus, right side up, or supine with the head turned away.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview and level counting (short axis, transverse):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Start at the <strong>C7 transverse process</strong>: only a posterior tubercle (the anterior tubercle is absent or vestigial), with the vertebral artery anterior to the C7 root.</li>\n<li>Scan cephalad to <strong>C6</strong>: the prominent anterior tubercle (Chassaignac's tubercle), giving a \"two-humped camel\" or \"U\" shape with the posterior tubercle; the C6 root lies in the groove between them.</li>\n<li>Continue to C5 and above (anterior and posterior tubercles become similar in size).</li>\n<li>Alternative: count the roots from the interscalene region.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> the C6 root at the external foraminal opening, between the anterior and posterior tubercles of the C6 transverse process.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> root diameter compared with the left side and with C5 and C7 (a swollen root supports the level).</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler (essential):</strong> colour or power Doppler around the root: the vertebral artery (at C7 and its entry into the C6 foramen in about 90%), and small radicular and segmental arteries (ascending or deep cervical branches) that may enter the foramen; use low probe pressure to see their pulsation.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> ask her to turn her head slightly or swallow to separate structures; sonopalpation over the root reproduces the pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> anterior and posterior tubercles, C5, C6 and C7 nerve roots, vertebral artery and vein, carotid artery, internal jugular vein, longus colli, sternocleidomastoid, anterior and middle scalenes, thyroid, sympathetic trunk.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> most cervical radiculopathy improves within 6–12 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Activity changes:</strong> avoid sustained neck extension at work (ergonomic chair and loupes, operating microscope), regular breaks.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> neural mobilisation, cervical traction, deep neck flexor strengthening, posture.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> NSAIDs short term; a short oral steroid course (already tried); neuropathic agents at a dose she can tolerate (pregabalin at night, or duloxetine, or nortriptyline); avoid long-term opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Soft collar</strong> briefly at night only.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided right C6 selective nerve root block</strong> at the external foraminal opening, posterior to anterior in plane, with <strong>non-particulate dexamethasone</strong> and local anaesthetic.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluoroscopic transforaminal injections have a reported intravascular injection rate of about 18–63%, and rare catastrophic cord or brainstem infarctions have occurred with particulate steroids. US shows the vertebral artery and radicular branches so the needle can avoid them (Narouze 2009; Jee 2013 found similar pain relief with no intravascular injections with US). Fluoroscopy with real-time contrast or digital subtraction remains the standard confirmation.</li>\n<li>Use only non-particulate steroid (dexamethasone) for cervical transforaminal or root injections.</li>\n<li>Alternative: fluoroscopic cervical interlaminar epidural steroid (C7–T1).</li>\n<li>Later options: pulsed RF of the DRG; surgical referral (anterior cervical discectomy and fusion, or posterior foraminotomy) for progressive weakness or persistent disabling pain beyond 6–12 weeks.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent (including the rare risk of spinal cord or brain infarction); check anticoagulation and allergies; IV access is advisable.</li>\n<li>Lateral decubitus, right side up; machine opposite.</li>\n<li>Count the level (C7 then C6) and scan with colour Doppler; mark.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G blunt-tip (or short-bevel) needle, 50 mm, in plane from posterior to anterior.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the posterior aspect of the C6 root at the external foraminal opening, between the tubercles, outside the foramen (do not enter the foramen).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspirate,</strong> then inject a small test volume watching the spread around the root. No visible spread may mean intravascular injection: stop and reposition. With fluoroscopy available, confirm with contrast (and digital subtraction).</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 1–2 mL 0.25% bupivacaine with dexamethasone 4 mg (Peng).</li>\n<li>Monitor for 30 minutes: neurological check of all four limbs, speech and vision.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Catastrophic (rare):</strong> spinal cord, brainstem or cerebellar infarction from intra-arterial injection (vertebral or radicular artery), especially with particulate steroids.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> nerve root injury, temporary arm numbness and weakness; spread to the epidural or intrathecal space (high block); phrenic nerve block (C3–C5) with diaphragm weakness; Horner syndrome; recurrent laryngeal nerve block (hoarseness).</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> vertebral artery puncture, haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic:</strong> local anaesthetic toxicity (seizure with vertebral artery injection of a tiny dose); vasovagal reaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> flushing, insomnia, raised glucose.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, increased pain.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> limb power and sensation in all four limbs, speech, vision, breathing (phrenic), headache; observe for at least 30 minutes; no driving that day.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-net advice:</strong> new weakness, numbness of the legs, severe headache or fever means urgent review.</li>\n<li><strong>At 2–4 weeks:</strong> arm pain NRS (target at least 50% reduction), neck pain, NDI, sleep, ability to work, neurological examination (power, reflexes).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> active rehabilitation; a second injection if partial relief; surgical referral if weakness progresses, myelopathy appears, or pain stays disabling.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not counting the levels from C7 (no anterior tubercle, vertebral artery anterior).</li>\n<li>Not using colour Doppler around the root before every needle pass.</li>\n<li>Using particulate steroid in the neck.</li>\n<li>Missing the myelopathy screen.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Narouze SN. Ultrasound-guided cervical nerve root block. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 8, pp. 95–101.", "Narouze S, Peng P. Ultrasound-guided cervical nerve root block. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 12, pp. 149–156.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Narouze SN, et al. Ultrasound-guided cervical selective nerve root block: a fluoroscopy-controlled feasibility study. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:343–348.", "Jee H, et al. Ultrasound-guided selective nerve root block versus fluoroscopy-guided transforaminal block for the treatment of radicular pain in the lower cervical spine: a randomized, blinded, controlled study. <em>Skeletal Radiol</em> 2013;42:69–78.", "Huntoon MA. Anatomy of the cervical intervertebral foramina: vulnerable arteries and ischemic neurologic injuries after transforaminal epidural injections. <em>Pain</em> 2005;117:104–111."]}, "4.1": {"title": "Cervical facet pain after whiplash", "vignette": "A 39-year-old female bank teller was a front-seat passenger in a rear-end car crash 14 months ago. Neck pain started the next day. She now has constant aching pain on the right side of the lower neck, spreading over the top of the shoulder blade and the back of the shoulder, but not down the arm. It is worst when she looks up, turns her head to the right to reverse the car, or works at her screen for long periods. She has headaches at the back of the head about twice a week. There is no numbness, weakness or arm pain. X-rays at the time were normal. Physiotherapy, paracetamol, naproxen and a course of acupuncture have helped only a little. She has a pending insurance claim and says she is \"fed up and not sleeping\".", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> whiplash mechanism, chronic unilateral axial neck pain, referral to the scapular and posterior shoulder region (C5–C6 facet referral pattern), worse with extension and rotation to the same side, no radicular symptoms, normal X-rays. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> occipital headaches (C2–C3 joint, third occipital nerve; a second level to consider), the insurance claim and low mood (psychosocial factors; not a reason to dismiss her pain), scapular pain (shoulder pathology).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> chronic cervical facet joint pain (C5–C6 on the right) after whiplash. Controlled blocks find facet pain in about half to two-thirds of chronic whiplash patients (Barnsley 1995 and later studies).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>C2–C3 facet pain (third occipital nerve) causing cervicogenic headache</li>\n<li>Discogenic neck pain</li>\n<li>Myofascial pain (trapezius, levator scapulae), cervical muscle strain</li>\n<li>Cervical radiculopathy (no arm symptoms argues against)</li>\n<li>Ligamentous instability (alar or transverse ligament injury) after trauma</li>\n<li>Shoulder pathology (referred to the scapula)</li>\n<li>Fracture missed on initial X-ray; red flags (infection, tumour)</li>\n<li>Psychological contributors: post-traumatic stress, depression, central sensitisation (affect outcome, not the diagnosis)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, neck and shoulders exposed; then prone for palpation.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (biceps, brachioradialis, triceps, Hoffmann sign); Neurotip and cotton wool C4–T1; MRC power of the upper limbs; Spurling test and upper limb tension test (should be negative); lower limb reflexes and gait (myelopathy screen).</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posture (forward head), muscle spasm.</li>\n<li>Cervical range in all planes with an inclinometer; pain on extension and ipsilateral rotation (extension–rotation test).</li>\n<li><strong>Palpation:</strong> unilateral paraspinal tenderness over the C5–C6 articular pillar (about 2–3 cm lateral to the midline), and over C2–C3 below the occiput; trigger points in the trapezius and levator scapulae.</li>\n<li>Cervical flexion–rotation test (C1–C2) for the headache.</li>\n<li>Shoulder range and impingement tests.</li>\n<li>Clinical facet tests have poor diagnostic accuracy; controlled blocks are the reference standard.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>palpate the articular pillar, not the midline, and say that no clinical sign reliably identifies a painful facet joint.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>X-ray</strong> with flexion–extension views: alignment, instability, degenerative changes.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> to exclude disc herniation, ligament injury, cord or root compression; facet changes are often absent after whiplash.</li>\n<li><strong>CT</strong> if a fracture is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic cervical medial branch blocks:</strong> the reference standard. Comparative blocks (lidocaine and bupivacaine on separate occasions) or placebo-controlled blocks reduce false positives; the 2022 multisociety guideline accepts at least 50% relief as the threshold (80% is used for stricter selection).</li>\n<li><strong>Questionnaires:</strong> NDI, sleep, mood (PHQ-9), PTSD screen, pain catastrophising.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong>, small-parts preset, depth 3–4 cm. Patient lateral decubitus, right side up, with a pillow under the head to keep the neck neutral (or prone).</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview and level counting:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Transverse at C7:</strong> C7 transverse process with only a posterior tubercle and the vertebral artery anterior; scan up to C6 (prominent anterior tubercle); count the roots.</li>\n<li><strong>Long axis (coronal) along the articular pillars:</strong> the \"sawtooth\" or \"hills and valleys\" pattern. Start at the mastoid, find C1 (no articular pillar), then the C2–C3 joint (the first joint below C2) and count down: C3–C4, C4–C5, C5–C6.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The C5–C6 joint (a hill).</li>\n<li>The C5 and C6 medial branches cross the waist (the valley, centroid) of the C5 and C6 articular pillars, close to bone, in the plane under the semispinalis capitis.</li>\n<li>The third occipital nerve crosses over the C2–C3 joint (for her headaches).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth from skin to the articular pillar for needle planning; compare joint contours with the left side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> vessels near the articular pillar (deep cervical artery branches) and the vertebral artery anterior to the target.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> gentle neck flexion and extension or rotation to see the joints move and confirm the level; sonopalpation over the C5–C6 joint reproduces her pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> mastoid, C1 transverse process, C2–C3 joint, articular pillars, facet joints, medial branches, third occipital nerve, semispinalis capitis and cervicis, splenius capitis, trapezius, levator scapulae, vertebral artery, transverse processes and tubercles.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and reassurance;</strong> encourage activity and return to work.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise therapy:</strong> deep neck flexor and scapular stabilisation training, graded activity; workstation ergonomics (screen height).</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> paracetamol, short NSAID courses; amitriptyline or nortriptyline at night (sleep and pain); avoid long-term opioids and benzodiazepines.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological care:</strong> CBT, sleep hygiene, treat PTSD or depression if present; multidisciplinary pain programme.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided diagnostic cervical medial branch blocks</strong> of the right C5 and C6 medial branches (for the C5–C6 joint), with small volumes of local anaesthetic only, done twice with different local anaesthetics (comparative blocks); if positive, <strong>radiofrequency neurotomy</strong> of the same nerves.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RF neurotomy gave a median of about 9–10 months of complete relief in a double-blind, sham-controlled trial in whiplash patients selected by placebo-controlled blocks (Lord 1996). It can be repeated.</li>\n<li>US-guided cervical medial branch blocks are feasible and accurate (Finlayson 2012), and US-guided third occipital nerve blocks matched fluoroscopy with fewer needle passes and less time (Finlayson 2013). US avoids radiation and shows vessels.</li>\n<li>Consider a third occipital nerve block on another day for the occipital headaches (C2–C3 joint).</li>\n<li>Intra-articular facet steroid injection has limited evidence.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; explain the pain diary; check anticoagulation and allergies.</li>\n<li>Lateral decubitus, right side up, neck neutral; machine in front of you.</li>\n<li>Long-axis scan to count from C2–C3 down to C5–C6; mark the C5 and C6 articular pillar waists.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Rotate to a transverse (short axis) view at each target: the centroid of the articular pillar is a flat bright line.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22–25G 50 mm, in plane from posterior to anterior, until the tip touches the periosteum at the centroid of the pillar, beneath the semispinalis capitis.</li>\n<li>Confirm in the long-axis view that the tip is at the waist (valley) of the correct pillar.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 0.3–0.5 mL local anaesthetic per nerve (2% lidocaine for the first block; 0.5% bupivacaine for the comparative block). No steroid; small volumes keep the block specific.</li>\n<li>Aspirate before injecting; inject slowly while watching the spread.</li>\n<li>Ask her to do her usual provoking movements (looking up, turning right) and record pain before and after.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> vertebral artery puncture or intravascular injection (seizure, transient neurological symptoms); haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> spread to the ventral ramus or the root (arm numbness or weakness); spread anteriorly to the sympathetic chain (Horner syndrome) or phrenic nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Third occipital nerve block</strong> (if done): ataxia and unsteadiness for a few hours (loss of upper cervical proprioception); numbness of the occipital scalp.</li>\n<li><strong>Spinal:</strong> dural puncture, intrathecal injection (very rare with a correct lateral approach).</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, infection, increased neck pain.</li>\n<li><strong>After RF neurotomy:</strong> numbness or dysaesthesia, neuritis, transient increase in pain, posterior neck muscle weakness.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Before discharge:</strong> neurological check, balance and gait (especially after a TON block); no driving that day.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain diary</strong> for 6–8 hours with provoking activities; relief must fit the duration of the local anaesthetic used (short with lidocaine, longer with bupivacaine).</li>\n<li><strong>Interpretation:</strong> at least 50% (or 80%) relief with concordant duration on both blocks = positive; go to RF neurotomy.</li>\n<li><strong>After RF:</strong> review at 6 weeks and 3 months: pain, range, NDI, sleep, return to work; repeat RF when pain returns.</li>\n<li><strong>Continue</strong> the exercise programme and psychological support.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Counting the levels in transverse view only. Use the long-axis \"sawtooth\" view from C2–C3.</li>\n<li>Targeting the joint (hill) instead of the medial branch at the waist (valley) of the pillar.</li>\n<li>Adding steroid or using large volumes in a diagnostic block.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the headaches (C2–C3 joint, third occipital nerve).</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Siegenthaler A, Eichenberger U. Ultrasound-guided third occipital nerve and cervical medial branch nerve blocks. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 6, pp. 83–90.", "Etheridge J-PB, Finlayson R. Cervical medial branch and third occipital nerve blocks. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 13, pp. 157–167.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Lord SM, Barnsley L, Wallis BJ, McDonald GJ, Bogduk N. Percutaneous radio-frequency neurotomy for chronic cervical zygapophyseal-joint pain. <em>N Engl J Med</em> 1996;335:1721–1726.", "Finlayson RJ, Gupta G, Alhujairi M, Dugani S, Tran DQ. Cervical medial branch block: a novel technique using ultrasound guidance. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2012;37:219–223.", "Finlayson RJ, Etheridge JP, Vieira L, Gupta G, Tran DQ. A randomized comparison between ultrasound- and fluoroscopy-guided third occipital nerve block. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2013;38:212–217.", "Hurley RW, et al. Consensus practice guidelines on interventions for cervical spine (facet) joint pain from a multispecialty international working group. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2022;47:3–59."]}, "5.1": {"title": "Occipital neuralgia, greater occipital nerve", "vignette": "A 39-year-old female accountant has had attacks of pain in the left side of the back of her head for 5 months. The pain is shooting and stabbing, like an electric shock, and lasts a few seconds, many times a day. It runs from the base of the skull up over the top of the head towards the left eye. Between attacks there is a dull ache, and brushing her hair or resting her head on the pillow on that side hurts. She sits at a computer for long hours and her neck feels stiff. She had migraine with aura as a teenager, with nausea, but says this pain is different. Ibuprofen does not help, and she is worried about a brain tumour.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> paroxysmal shooting or stabbing pain lasting seconds, distribution of the greater occipital nerve (occiput to vertex), dysaesthesia and allodynia of the scalp (brushing hair, pillow), unilateral. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> history of migraine with aura (migraine can coexist, and occipital pain can trigger migraine), neck stiffness from desk work (cervicogenic headache), fear of a brain tumour (red flags must be asked about and excluded).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> left greater occipital neuralgia (ICHD-3 13.4): paroxysmal stabbing pain lasting seconds to minutes in the GON distribution, with tenderness over the nerve, dysaesthesia or allodynia, and temporary relief from a local anaesthetic block of the nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesser or third occipital neuralgia</li>\n<li>Cervicogenic headache (C2–C3 facet, C1–C2 joint)</li>\n<li>Migraine (with occipital pain); tension-type headache</li>\n<li>Hemicrania continua; cluster headache (if eye symptoms)</li>\n<li>Secondary causes: C2 root compression, Chiari malformation, posterior fossa or upper cervical tumour, vertebral artery dissection, multiple sclerosis, herpes zoster (C2), giant cell arteritis</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, then prone with the forehead supported, or seated leaning forward.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensation</strong> in the GON, LON and TON territories with cotton wool (allodynia) and a Neurotip (hypo- or hyperalgesia), compared with the right side.</li>\n<li><strong>Tinel's sign:</strong> tap over the GON at the superior nuchal line (about one third of the distance from the occipital protuberance to the mastoid, medial to the occipital artery) and over the LON.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure on the nerve</strong> reproduces the pain and its radiation.</li>\n<li>Cranial nerves including the trigeminal nerve and corneal reflex; fundoscopy; cerebellar signs; upper limb reflexes; Hoffmann's sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervical range of motion; flexion–rotation test (C1–C2).</li>\n<li>Palpation of the C2–C3 joint and the suboccipital muscles; trigger points in trapezius, splenius capitis, semispinalis capitis.</li>\n<li>Spurling's test; posture.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>show the point of maximum tenderness at the GON exit and allodynia in its territory; compare with the other side; name the red flags.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Occipital neuralgia is a clinical diagnosis</strong>, confirmed by the response to a GON block.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI brain and upper cervical spine</strong> (with contrast if indicated) to exclude secondary causes (Chiari, tumour, demyelination, C2 root), especially with atypical features or neurological signs. It also reassures her.</li>\n<li>MR angiography if dissection is a concern.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided GON block</strong> with local anaesthetic.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US:</strong> the GON can be swollen or compressed by scar or vessels (sonopathology).</li>\n<li>Headache diary and HIT-6.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong> (12–18 MHz for the distal approach), depth 2–3 cm. Patient prone with a pillow under the chest and the neck flexed, or seated leaning forward.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> probe transverse in the midline at the <strong>external occipital protuberance</strong>, then move caudally to the <strong>C2 spinous process</strong> (the first large bifid spinous process).</p>\n<p><strong>Level identification:</strong> from the C2 spinous process, move laterally and rotate the probe so the lateral end points up towards the C1 transverse process, along the length of the <strong>obliquus capitis inferior</strong> (OCI), which runs from the C2 spinous process to the C1 transverse process.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus (proximal approach):</strong> the <strong>GON</strong> is seen as an oval hyperechoic structure in the fascial plane <strong>between the OCI (deep) and the semispinalis capitis (superficial)</strong>, superficial to the OCI.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> cross-sectional area of the GON, compared with the right side (enlargement supports neuralgia); depth to the nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> small arteries and veins next to the nerve; the <strong>vertebral artery</strong> lies deep and lateral, near the C1 transverse process; the occipital artery at the superior nuchal line.</p>\n<p><strong>Distal approach:</strong> probe at the superior nuchal line: the GON lies <strong>medial to the occipital artery</strong> (colour Doppler).</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> gentle head rotation shows the OCI contracting under the nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> external occipital protuberance, C2 bifid spinous process, C1 transverse process, OCI, semispinalis capitis, splenius capitis, trapezius, GON, occipital artery, vertebral artery.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education</strong> and reassurance (after MRI); avoid triggers (pressure on the scalp, sustained neck flexion).</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> posture, neck and shoulder exercises, ergonomics at the computer, massage and heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> neuropathic agents (gabapentin, pregabalin, carbamazepine, amitriptyline); NSAIDs are often not effective.</li>\n<li>Treat coexisting migraine if present.</li>\n<li>Psychology: worry about a tumour, stress, sleep.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided proximal GON block at the level of C2 (OCI approach)</strong>, with local anaesthetic and steroid; it is both diagnostic and therapeutic.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The proximal approach targets the nerve at a constant point, before it divides, and in a cadaver and volunteer study the proximal US approach at C2 gave a more reliable block than the distal approach at the superior nuchal line (Greher 2010).</li>\n<li>A GON block with local anaesthetic and steroid can give relief for weeks to months; it is also used in migraine and cluster headache (Afridi 2006).</li>\n<li>For recurrence: repeat blocks, <strong>pulsed RF of the GON</strong>, botulinum toxin; for refractory cases, <strong>occipital nerve stimulation</strong>; surgical decompression in selected patients.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; baseline pain score and allodynia map.</li>\n<li>Prone with the neck flexed (or seated leaning forward); machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Identify the C2 spinous process and the OCI; find the GON between the OCI and semispinalis capitis; colour Doppler for vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25G 38–50 mm, in plane from medial to lateral (away from the vertebral artery and the spinal canal), or out of plane.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the fascial plane next to the GON, superficial to the OCI (do not pass deep to the OCI).</li>\n<li>Aspirate; inject <strong>2–3 mL</strong> of local anaesthetic (for example 0.5% bupivacaine) with steroid (for example 40 mg methylprednisolone or 4–8 mg dexamethasone) and watch the spread around the nerve.</li>\n<li>Reassess at 15–20 minutes: numbness in the GON territory, relief of pain and allodynia.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li>Vasovagal reaction (common in the seated position); dizziness and unsteadiness.</li>\n<li>Intravascular injection (occipital artery or vertebral artery): local anaesthetic toxicity, seizure.</li>\n<li>Spread to the vertebral artery region or the C1–C2 space (deep to the OCI): risk of injection near the spinal canal, rarely high spinal block.</li>\n<li>Haematoma, infection, nerve injury, worsening of pain for a few days.</li>\n<li>Steroid: alopecia and skin atrophy at the injection site, facial flushing, raised blood glucose.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Block success:</strong> numbness in the GON area and relief of the paroxysms and allodynia; a positive diagnostic block supports the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety:</strong> observe for 20–30 minutes (vasovagal reaction, dizziness); no driving if unsteady.</li>\n<li><strong>Duration:</strong> pain diary (attacks per day, intensity, duration of relief).</li>\n<li><strong>Next steps:</strong> repeat the block if relief lasts weeks; pulsed RF or occipital nerve stimulation if relief is short; continue neuropathic medication and physiotherapy.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-evaluate</strong> the diagnosis if the block fails (C2–C3 joint, migraine, secondary cause).</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not identifying the C2 spinous process and the OCI before looking for the nerve.</li>\n<li>Not naming the plane (between the OCI and semispinalis capitis).</li>\n<li>Not using colour Doppler, and not knowing where the vertebral artery lies.</li>\n<li>Not excluding secondary causes with MRI.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Moriggl B, Greher M. Ultrasound-guided greater occipital nerve block. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 23, pp. 231–236.", "Narouze SN. Occipital neuralgia: sonoanatomy and sonopathology of the occipital nerves. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 31, pp. 297–306.", "Hoydonckx Y, Peng P. Greater and lesser occipital nerve. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 2, pp. 33–42.", "Greher M, Moriggl B, Curatolo M, Kirchmair L, Eichenberger U. Sonographic visualization and ultrasound-guided blockade of the greater occipital nerve: a comparison of two selective techniques confirmed by anatomical dissection. <em>Br J Anaesth</em> 2010;104:637–642.", "Afridi SK, Shields KG, Bhola R, Goadsby PJ. Greater occipital nerve injection in primary headache syndromes: prolonged effects from a single injection. <em>Pain</em> 2006;122:126–129.", "Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society. The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition. <em>Cephalalgia</em> 2018;38:1–211."]}, "6.1": {"title": "Shoulder pain, AC joint", "vignette": "A 41-year-old man who does competitive powerlifting presents with an 8-month history of right shoulder pain. There was no single injury. The pain is on the top of the shoulder, and he can point to it with one finger. It is worst during bench press and dips, and when he reaches across his body to fasten the car seatbelt. He wakes when he lies on the right side. He also notices a dull ache at the front of the shoulder after heavy sessions. He has cut down his training weights, used ibuprofen gel, and had four physiotherapy sessions, with little change. He is otherwise well and wants to compete again in 3 months. There is no neck pain and no numbness in the arm.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> heavy pressing sports, one-finger point to the top of the shoulder, pain with cross-body reach, night pain lying on that side, no trauma. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> anterior shoulder ache (think biceps or subacromial), the time pressure of competing in 3 months (tempts you to skip diagnosis and go straight to steroid).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> AC joint pain from distal clavicular osteolysis, a stress-related resorption of the distal clavicle seen in weightlifters, or early AC joint osteoarthritis.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>AC joint osteoarthritis</li>\n<li>Low-grade AC joint sprain (Rockwood type I–II) from repetitive load</li>\n<li>Subacromial impingement and bursitis (often coexists, as inferior AC osteophytes narrow the subacromial space)</li>\n<li>Long head of biceps tendinopathy or SLAP lesion (bench press, O'Brien test)</li>\n<li>Pectoralis major tendinopathy at the humeral insertion</li>\n<li>Glenohumeral instability (posterior, from pressing)</li>\n<li>C4 or C5 radiculopathy; suprascapular neuropathy</li>\n<li>Less common: inflammatory arthritis, septic arthritis, tumour of the distal clavicle</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing for inspection from front and behind, then sitting with both shoulders exposed.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> step or swelling over the AC joint compared with the left, muscle bulk, scapular position.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (biceps C5, brachioradialis C6, triceps C7); Neurotip over C4 (top of the shoulder, the cape area) and C5; light touch; MRC power of deltoid, cuff and biceps. Cervical range of motion and Spurling test.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpation: point tenderness over the AC joint (the most sensitive single sign), compared with the other side.</li>\n<li>Active and passive range of motion; pain at the end of elevation (above 160°) is typical of AC pain, while a mid-range arc points to subacromial pain.</li>\n<li>AC tests: cross-body adduction (Scarf) test, O'Brien test (pain on top of the shoulder, not deep inside), AC resisted extension test, Paxinos sign (thumb pressure under the acromion pushes the clavicle up).</li>\n<li>Exclude the neighbours: Neer and Hawkins (subacromial), Speed and Yergason (biceps), apprehension and relocation, cuff strength tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>say where the O'Brien pain is. Pain on top means AC; pain deep inside means labrum.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Plain X-ray:</strong> AP, axillary and a Zanca view (10–15° cephalic tilt, about half the usual exposure). Look for distal clavicle resorption, loss of subchondral bone line, cysts, joint narrowing, osteophytes and joint widening.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> joint capsule bulging, effusion, osteophytes, cortical irregularity of the distal clavicle, and Doppler signal (synovitis). Compare with the other side. Also checks the cuff and the subacromial bursa.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> the most sensitive test for osteolysis (bone marrow oedema in the distal clavicle) and to assess the cuff before surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided AC joint injection</strong> of local anaesthetic: the reference standard for AC joint pain. Relief of at least 50% with a negative cross-body test afterwards confirms it.</li>\n<li>Bloods only if inflammatory or septic arthritis is suspected (ESR, CRP, white count).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. Linear high-frequency probe (10–18 MHz), MSK superficial preset, depth about 2 cm, focus at the joint. Patient seated with the arm hanging by the side, which opens the joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> a short shoulder sweep first (biceps in the groove, subscapularis, supraspinatus and the SASD bursa), so the AC joint is not looked at in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the AC joint:</strong> probe in the coronal plane over the top of the shoulder. Follow the clavicle laterally until the joint gap appears as a V between two bright bone surfaces. Look at:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the capsule and superior AC ligament (bulging above the bone line suggests effusion or synovitis);</li>\n<li>the bone surfaces (irregular, eroded distal clavicle in osteolysis; osteophytes in OA);</li>\n<li>joint width, measured and compared with the left side.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour or power Doppler over the capsule for synovitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> watch the joint during cross-body adduction (painful compression, capsular bulging) and with downward traction on the arm (joint widening suggests instability).</p>\n<p><strong>Also look for:</strong> a \"geyser sign\", a cyst above the AC joint that may signal a full-thickness cuff tear beneath.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> acromion, distal clavicle, AC joint space, AC capsule and superior AC ligament, fibrocartilage disc (in younger patients), deltoid, trapezius, supraspinatus, SASD bursa, coracoacromial ligament.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Load modification:</strong> narrow-grip bench press, stop dips and deep flyes, avoid lowering the bar below chest level; keep training legs and lower body.</li>\n<li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> topical NSAIDs, short courses of oral NSAIDs; ice after training.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> scapular stabilisation, posterior capsule stretching, rotator cuff strengthening.</li>\n<li><strong>Taping</strong> to offload the joint during training.</li>\n<li>Most cases of osteolysis settle with 2–3 months of activity change, but symptoms often return when full loading resumes.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided AC joint injection: local anaesthetic first as a diagnostic block, with corticosteroid for treatment.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Accuracy of US-guided AC injection is about 95–100%, against about 40–50% for palpation-guided injection (Peck 2010; Sabeti-Aschraf 2011). The joint is small and its line is often not where it is palpated.</li>\n<li>Later options: PRP or prolotherapy (limited evidence); if the injection gives clear but short relief, arthroscopic distal clavicle excision.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies and bleeding risk; explain the diagnostic purpose.</li>\n<li>Patient seated, arm hanging by the side (gentle downward traction opens the joint); machine opposite.</li>\n<li>Sterile skin prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear 10–18 MHz probe in the coronal plane, with the joint V centred on the screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25G (or 27G) 38 mm.</li>\n<li><strong>Approach:</strong> out-of-plane from the anterior (or posterior) side at the middle of the probe, aiming to see the tip as a bright dot entering the gap; or in-plane from lateral to medial with a gel stand-off, because the joint is so superficial.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> just beneath the AC capsule, in the joint. The joint lies less than 1 cm deep; a deeper tip will deposit drug in the subacromial bursa or supraspinatus.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> total about 1–1.5 mL, as the joint holds very little. For example 0.5 mL triamcinolone 40 mg/mL (20 mg) with 0.5–1 mL 1% lidocaine. Watch the capsule lift.</li>\n<li>Avoid depositing steroid in the subcutaneous tissue: the skin here is thin.</li>\n<li>Repeat the cross-body adduction test and point tenderness after 5–10 minutes and record the result.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Common:</strong> post-injection flare; transient raised glucose; facial flushing.</li>\n<li><strong>Local:</strong> subcutaneous fat atrophy and skin depigmentation (thin skin over the joint); misplaced injection into the subacromial bursa or supraspinatus, which gives a false-positive diagnostic result.</li>\n<li><strong>Rare:</strong> septic arthritis, haematoma, vasovagal reaction, allergy.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated steroid:</strong> cartilage and ligament weakening.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> percentage pain relief, point tenderness and the cross-body test after the local anaesthetic. At least 50% relief supports the AC joint as the pain generator; no relief means rethink the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>At 2–6 weeks:</strong> pain during pressing, night pain, range of motion, a function score (QuickDASH or SPADI).</li>\n<li><strong>Return to sport:</strong> graded return with modified technique; avoid maximal pressing for about 2 weeks after steroid.</li>\n<li><strong>Escalate:</strong> if relief is clear but short-lived, refer for distal clavicle excision; limit steroid injections to about 3 per year.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not checking whether the O'Brien test hurts on top or deep inside.</li>\n<li>Calling the injection only \"therapeutic\": it is the diagnostic reference standard, so re-examine afterwards.</li>\n<li>Injecting too deep, or too much volume for a joint that holds about 1 mL.</li>\n<li>Missing the Zanca view and MRI for osteolysis.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Schaefer MP, Fox K. Ultrasound-guided shoulder joint and bursa injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 26, AC joint pp. 258–260.", "McDonald JK, Peng P. Shoulder. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 19, AC joint pp. 223–224 and 229–230.", "Baxi N, Spinner DA. Shoulder. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 2, pp. 9–11.", "Peng PWH, Cheng P. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part III: shoulder. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2011;36:592–605.", "Peck E, Lai JK, Pawlina W, Smith J. Accuracy of ultrasound-guided versus palpation-guided acromioclavicular joint injections: a cadaveric study. <em>PM R</em> 2010;2:817–821.", "Sabeti-Aschraf M, et al. Ultrasound guidance improves the accuracy of the acromioclavicular joint infiltration: a prospective randomized study. <em>Knee Surg Sports Traumatol Arthrosc</em> 2011;19:292–295."]}, "6.3": {"title": "Shoulder pain, long head of biceps tendinopathy", "vignette": "A 35-year-old right-handed female tennis coach presents with a 4-month history of pain at the front of the right shoulder. The pain began after a week-long coaching camp with many hours of serving demonstrations and carrying ball baskets. She points to a spot just below the front of the shoulder, and the pain sometimes spreads down the front of the upper arm. It is worse when she lifts objects with the palm up, carries shopping with her elbow bent and serves overhead. She has felt a click at the front of the shoulder when throwing. Night pain is mild. She has had ibuprofen and 6 sessions of physiotherapy with only partial improvement. She has no neck pain and no numbness. Her mother had a \"popped biceps\" after a steroid injection, and she is worried about that.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> overhead and lifting activity, anterior shoulder pain localised to the bicipital groove, radiation down the anterior arm, pain with resisted elbow flexion and forearm supination (palm-up lifting), clicking. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> clicking with throwing (SLAP lesion or biceps subluxation from a pulley lesion), the mother's tendon rupture (steroid injection into a tendon), overhead serving (subacromial impingement).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> primary LHB tendinopathy with tenosynovitis in the bicipital groove.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>SLAP lesion (superior labrum anterior to posterior), often coexisting in overhead athletes</li>\n<li>Biceps instability: subluxation or dislocation from a biceps pulley (rotator interval) lesion or subscapularis tear</li>\n<li>Subacromial impingement with SASD bursitis; supraspinatus tendinopathy</li>\n<li>Subscapularis tendinopathy or tear</li>\n<li>AC joint pathology</li>\n<li>Partial tear or early rupture of the LHB</li>\n<li>C5 or C6 radiculopathy</li>\n<li>Pectoralis major tendinopathy; coracoid impingement</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing for inspection (both shoulders and arms exposed, look for a \"Popeye\" deformity), then seated.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (biceps C5, brachioradialis C6, triceps C7); Neurotip and light touch in C5–C7 dermatomes and over the lateral forearm (musculocutaneous nerve); MRC power of elbow flexion and supination; cervical range and Spurling test.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpation of the bicipital groove with the arm in 10° internal rotation, then external rotation: the tender spot should move with the groove (it is the tendon, not the deltoid).</li>\n<li>Range of shoulder and elbow movement; strength of the rotator cuff.</li>\n<li><strong>Biceps tests:</strong> Speed test (resisted forward flexion with elbow extended and palm up), Yergason test (resisted supination with elbow flexed 90°), upper-cut test.</li>\n<li><strong>SLAP tests:</strong> O'Brien active compression test (pain deep inside), biceps load test II, crank test.</li>\n<li><strong>Instability of the tendon:</strong> palpate for a snap during passive rotation with the arm abducted.</li>\n<li><strong>Subscapularis:</strong> lift-off, belly press, bear hug. <strong>Impingement:</strong> Neer, Hawkins–Kennedy.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>show that the tender spot moves with rotation of the arm. This separates LHB tenderness from deltoid tenderness, and it is quick to do.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound (first line):</strong> tendon thickening, hypoechogenicity, loss of the fibrillar pattern, fluid in the sheath (more than about 2 mm, or surrounding the tendon), partial tear, Doppler hyperaemia; subluxation on dynamic external rotation; subscapularis and pulley; compare with the left.</li>\n<li><strong>Note:</strong> fluid in the biceps sheath can come from the glenohumeral joint (the sheath communicates with the joint), so look for a joint effusion too.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI or MR arthrogram:</strong> for a SLAP lesion, pulley lesion or partial tear when the diagnosis is uncertain or surgery is planned.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> usually normal; excludes calcific deposits and AC or GH arthritis.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic US-guided local anaesthetic injection into the sheath:</strong> relief supports the LHB as the pain source.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 10–15 MHz</strong> probe, MSK superficial preset, depth about 2–3 cm. Patient seated, elbow flexed 90°, forearm supinated and resting on her thigh.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> a systematic shoulder scan (biceps, subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, posterior joint, AC joint), starting with the biceps.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the LHB:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Short axis</strong> in the bicipital groove between the greater and lesser tuberosities, under the transverse humeral ligament: the tendon is an oval bright structure. Follow it from the rotator interval to the musculotendinous junction (pectoralis major insertion).</li>\n<li><strong>Long axis:</strong> rotate the probe 90° to see the fibrillar pattern and any fluid along the tendon. Tilt the probe to avoid anisotropy (an artefact that makes the tendon look dark).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> tendon thickness and fluid depth in the sheath, compared with the left.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> power Doppler for tendon hyperaemia; colour Doppler for the ascending branch of the anterior circumflex humeral artery lateral to the tendon (avoid it when injecting).</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> passive external and internal rotation of the arm while scanning the groove in short axis: the tendon subluxing over the lesser tuberosity indicates instability.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> long head of biceps tendon, bicipital groove, greater tuberosity, lesser tuberosity, transverse humeral ligament, subscapularis, deltoid, rotator interval and coracohumeral ligament, anterior circumflex humeral artery branch, pectoralis major tendon.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Relative rest and load management:</strong> limit serving demonstrations, carry baskets with both arms close to the body, modify technique.</li>\n<li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> topical NSAIDs; short course of oral NSAIDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> scapular stabilisation, rotator cuff strengthening, posterior capsule stretching, progressive eccentric loading; address thoracic posture.</li>\n<li><strong>Ice</strong> after activity.</li>\n<li>Return to full overhead activity gradually.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided injection of the LHB tendon sheath</strong> (peritendinous, not into the tendon) with corticosteroid and local anaesthetic.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>US-guided sheath injections were accurate in about 87% of cases, against about 27% for palpation-guided injections (Hashiuchi 2011). Many \"blind\" injections end up in the deltoid or the tendon.</li>\n<li>Steroid injected directly into the tendon can lead to rupture, which is her mother's experience and her worry. US lets you see the needle tip outside the tendon.</li>\n<li>Later options: PRP injection for tendinopathy; arthroscopic biceps tenotomy or tenodesis for refractory pain, tears or instability (and repair of an associated SLAP or pulley lesion).</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent, including the small risk of tendon rupture; check allergies and anticoagulation.</li>\n<li>Seated, elbow flexed 90°, forearm supinated on her thigh; arm slightly externally rotated to bring the groove forward; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Linear probe in short axis over the bicipital groove; identify the tendon and, with colour Doppler, the anterior circumflex humeral artery branch on the lateral side.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 25G 38 mm (Spinner: 25G 1.5 inch), in plane from lateral to medial in short axis (or out of plane at the probe midpoint).</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> inside the sheath next to the tendon, preferably on its medial side, between the tendon and the lesser tuberosity, after passing the transverse humeral ligament. Do not inject against or into the tendon.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> fluid should spread around the tendon (a \"halo\" or \"doughnut\"); swelling of the tendon itself or high resistance means withdraw.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> about 1 mL corticosteroid (for example methylprednisolone 40 mg or triamcinolone 20–40 mg) with 1–2 mL of 1% lidocaine; total about 2–3 mL (larger volumes flow up into the glenohumeral joint).</li>\n<li>Re-test Speed and Yergason after 10 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Tendon:</strong> intratendinous injection and later rupture of the LHB (\"Popeye\" deformity), more likely with repeated injections.</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> puncture of the anterior circumflex humeral artery branch, haematoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Common steroid effects:</strong> post-injection flare, skin depigmentation and fat atrophy (the area is superficial), flushing, raised glucose.</li>\n<li><strong>Rare:</strong> infection (septic tenosynovitis or arthritis, as the sheath communicates with the joint), vasovagal reaction, allergy.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> relief of groove tenderness, Speed and Yergason tests (diagnostic value); no new weakness.</li>\n<li><strong>Advice:</strong> avoid heavy lifting with the elbow flexed and the palm up, and vigorous serving, for about 2 weeks after steroid.</li>\n<li><strong>At 4–6 weeks:</strong> pain during coaching activities, function score (QuickDASH or a sports module), strength, any clicking.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up for warning signs:</strong> a sudden pop and bulge in the arm (rupture).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> continue a progressive loading programme; limit steroid injections to about 2–3 a year; refer for MRI arthrogram and surgical opinion if pain persists, clicking or instability, or a tear.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Injecting into the tendon instead of the sheath; not using Doppler for the anterior circumflex humeral artery branch.</li>\n<li>Not doing the dynamic external rotation test for subluxation.</li>\n<li>Forgetting SLAP and subscapularis tests in an overhead athlete.</li>\n<li>Large injection volumes that flow into the joint and muddle the diagnosis.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Schaefer MP, Fox K. Ultrasound-guided shoulder joint and bursa injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 26, biceps tendon sheath pp. 257–259.", "McDonald JK, Peng P. Shoulder. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 19, pp. 213–231 (LHB scan p. 220).", "Baxi N, Spinner DA. Shoulder. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 2, pp. 7–9.", "Peng PWH, Cheng P. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part III: shoulder. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2011;36:592–605.", "Hashiuchi T, et al. Accuracy of the biceps tendon sheath injection: ultrasound-guided or unguided injection? A randomized controlled trial. <em>J Shoulder Elbow Surg</em> 2011;20:1069–1073.", "Aly AR, Rajasekaran S, Ashworth N. Ultrasound-guided shoulder girdle injections are more accurate and more effective than landmark-guided injections: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Br J Sports Med</em> 2015;49:1042–1049."]}, "6.4": {"title": "Shoulder pain, frozen shoulder", "vignette": "A 54-year-old female schoolteacher presents with a 4-month history of left shoulder pain. Two months before it started, she broke her left wrist and wore a sling for 5 weeks. The pain came on gradually, is felt deep in the shoulder and spreads down the outer arm to the elbow. It is severe at night, and she cannot lie on either side. Over the last 6 weeks she has found it increasingly hard to fasten her bra, reach her back pocket or write on the top of the whiteboard. She has type 2 diabetes and takes metformin. Her family doctor's X-ray of the shoulder was reported as normal. Paracetamol and ibuprofen help little. A physiotherapist tried stretching, which made the pain much worse.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> age 40–60, female, diabetes, period of immobilisation, severe night pain, progressive loss of movement (behind the back, then overhead), normal X-ray, aggressive stretching made it worse. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> the recent wrist fracture (think CRPS), pain radiating to the elbow (think C5 radiculopathy or a cuff tear).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> adhesive capsulitis, secondary to immobilisation, with diabetes as a risk factor; currently in the painful freezing phase (stage 1–2).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glenohumeral osteoarthritis (normal X-ray argues against)</li>\n<li>Locked posterior dislocation (always rule out with an axillary view)</li>\n<li>Calcific tendinitis, or a large rotator cuff tear with secondary stiffness</li>\n<li>Subacromial impingement with pain-limited movement (passive range would be preserved)</li>\n<li>CRPS type I after the wrist fracture (shoulder–hand syndrome)</li>\n<li>Inflammatory arthritis, septic arthritis</li>\n<li>C5 radiculopathy</li>\n<li>Tumour: proximal humerus metastasis, apical lung tumour (night pain is a red flag)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing for inspection and active movement, then supine for passive range (the scapula is fixed, so glenohumeral motion is measured accurately).</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> muscle wasting, scapular position, swelling; look at the left hand for CRPS signs (colour, sweating, swelling, skin and nail changes).</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (biceps, brachioradialis, triceps); Neurotip and light touch in C5–T1 dermatomes and the axillary nerve patch; MRC power (often limited by pain, so note it); cervical range and Spurling test.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active and passive range in all planes with a goniometer, compared with the right side.</li>\n<li><strong>Capsular pattern:</strong> passive external rotation lost most, then abduction, then internal rotation. Loss of passive external rotation of 50% or more with the arm by the side is the key sign.</li>\n<li>Hard, painful end-feel; scapula moving early (shrug sign).</li>\n<li>Cuff strength tests within the available range (normal strength argues against a large tear).</li>\n<li>Impingement tests are positive but not specific in frozen shoulder.</li>\n<li>Hand: temperature and swelling compared with the right; allodynia with cotton wool (CRPS).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>measure external rotation with the elbow at the side and say \"capsular pattern\". Examine the hand to deal with the CRPS distractor.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> AP and <strong>axillary view</strong>, to exclude glenohumeral OA, calcific deposits and a locked posterior dislocation. In frozen shoulder it is normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> coracohumeral ligament (CHL) thickened compared with the other side; soft tissue thickening and Doppler hyperaemia in the rotator interval; limited sliding of the supraspinatus under the acromion during abduction; excludes a cuff tear and calcific tendinitis.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI or MR arthrogram:</strong> only if the picture is unclear. Signs: CHL thickening, obliterated fat in the rotator interval (subcoracoid triangle), thickened axillary recess capsule (above about 4 mm), small joint volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> HbA1c (control matters for steroid and for prognosis); TSH; lipids; ESR and CRP if inflammatory arthritis or infection is possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Staging:</strong> freezing (painful), frozen (stiff), thawing (recovery). Natural history 1–3 years; a minority keep some stiffness.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine opposite, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. Linear 6–15 MHz probe; a curvilinear 2–6 MHz probe may be needed for the posterior joint in a large shoulder. Patient seated on a stool.</p>\n<p><strong>Systematic overview:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Long head of biceps in the groove (fluid in the sheath reflects joint effusion).</li>\n<li><strong>Rotator interval:</strong> probe oblique between the coracoid and the greater tuberosity. Measure CHL thickness and compare with the right; look for hypoechoic soft tissue around the biceps.</li>\n<li>Subscapularis in long axis with passive external rotation.</li>\n<li>Supraspinatus in the modified Crass position (she may not reach it; use neutral if not).</li>\n<li>Posterior joint: infraspinatus, posterior labrum and capsule, effusion.</li>\n<li>Axillary recess (probe in the axilla with the arm slightly abducted): capsule thickness.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> power Doppler in the rotator interval; hyperaemia supports early, inflammatory frozen shoulder.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> probe in the coronal plane at the acromion during passive abduction; restricted sliding of the supraspinatus under the acromion. Passive external rotation while watching the subscapularis shows the limited glide.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> coracoid, coracohumeral ligament, rotator interval, long head of biceps, subscapularis, supraspinatus, humeral head and cartilage, deltoid, infraspinatus, posterior labrum, glenoid, axillary recess.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education:</strong> self-limiting but slow (often 1–3 years); reassure, and set expectations.</li>\n<li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> paracetamol, NSAIDs if tolerated, short-term night analgesia; a short course of oral prednisolone gives short-term relief but less than an injection, and raises glucose.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> gentle range-of-motion exercises within pain limits in the freezing phase; more forceful stretching only once pain settles (frozen phase). Heat before exercise.</li>\n<li><strong>Treat risk factors:</strong> glucose control.</li>\n<li><strong>TENS</strong> for pain relief (weak evidence).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided glenohumeral joint corticosteroid injection, posterior approach, with or without hydrodilatation (capsular distension).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intra-articular steroid gives the best early pain relief of the available treatments, especially in the first months (Challoumas 2020).</li>\n<li>US guidance gives about 92–99% accuracy, against 27–72% blind (Spinner 2014 review); Rutten 2009 reported 94% first-attempt success with US.</li>\n<li>Hydrodilatation adds short-term benefit in some studies; no approach (posterior, rotator interval or subacromial) has been shown to be clearly better.</li>\n<li>Adjunct: suprascapular nerve block for pain, so she can do physiotherapy.</li>\n<li>Later: manipulation under anaesthesia or arthroscopic capsular release if stiffness persists beyond 6–12 months of good treatment.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check allergies, anticoagulation and blood glucose (HbA1c).</li>\n<li>Patient seated (or lateral decubitus, affected side up), hand on the opposite shoulder to open the posterior joint; machine opposite.</li>\n<li>Sterile skin prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Probe in the transverse plane just below and parallel to the scapular spine. Identify the humeral head, the triangular hyperechoic posterior labrum, the glenoid and the infraspinatus over them.</li>\n<li>Local anaesthetic skin wheal.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 90 mm spinal needle (or 22G 50 mm in a slim shoulder), in plane from lateral to medial.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> between the humeral head cartilage and the posterior labrum, deep to the capsule. Avoid the cartilage and the labrum.</li>\n<li><strong>Test injection:</strong> fluid should spread medially and lift the posterior capsule. If there is resistance, the tip may be in cartilage: withdraw slightly or rotate the bevel.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> triamcinolone 40 mg or methylprednisolone 40 mg with 4–5 mL of 1% lidocaine or 0.5% ropivacaine.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrodilatation (optional):</strong> continue with saline in 5 mL steps up to about 20–30 mL, watching the capsule distend; stop if the patient cannot tolerate it, or when the capsule ruptures (a sudden loss of resistance).</li>\n<li>Do not go too far medially, towards the spinoglenoid notch, where the suprascapular nerve and vessels run.</li>\n<li>Afterwards: gentle range of motion straight away; supervised physiotherapy from about 1 week.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Common:</strong> post-injection pain flare; raised blood glucose for 2–5 days (advise extra monitoring); facial flushing.</li>\n<li><strong>Joint:</strong> septic arthritis (rare), cartilage injury from the needle, chondrotoxicity of high-dose local anaesthetic (use the lowest effective concentration).</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrodilatation:</strong> pain during distension, capsular rupture with fluid in the muscles (usually harmless).</li>\n<li><strong>Neurovascular:</strong> injury to the suprascapular nerve or vessels if the needle goes too medial; vasovagal reaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> allergy, local anaesthetic toxicity, skin atrophy if injected superficially.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> pain relief and passive range (external rotation) after the local anaesthetic; neurological check; blood glucose plan.</li>\n<li><strong>At 2–6 weeks:</strong> night pain, pain scores, passive external rotation and abduction measured with a goniometer, function score (SPADI or Oxford Shoulder Score).</li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation:</strong> progress stretching as pain allows; home programme.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeat:</strong> up to 3 injections, at least 6 weeks apart, if each helped.</li>\n<li><strong>Escalate:</strong> refer for capsular release or manipulation if stiffness remains after 6–12 months; re-image if the course is unusual (red flags, progressive weakness).</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not measuring passive external rotation, or not naming the capsular pattern.</li>\n<li>Not asking for an axillary view to exclude a locked posterior dislocation.</li>\n<li>Missing the diabetes link: glucose before and after steroid.</li>\n<li>No mention of hydrodilatation or the suprascapular nerve block as options.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Schaefer MP, Fox K. Ultrasound-guided shoulder joint and bursa injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 26, glenohumeral joint pp. 259–262.", "McDonald JK, Peng P. Shoulder. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 19, rotator interval approach pp. 225–226, posterior approach p. 227.", "Baxi N, Spinner DA. Shoulder. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 2, pp. 11–13.", "Peng PWH, Cheng P. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part III: shoulder. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2011;36:592–605.", "Challoumas D, et al. Comparison of treatments for frozen shoulder: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>JAMA Netw Open</em> 2020;3:e2029581.", "Rutten MJCM, et al. Glenohumeral joint injection: a comparative study of ultrasound and fluoroscopically guided techniques before MR arthrography. <em>Eur Radiol</em> 2009;19:722–730.", "Buchbinder R, Green S, Youd JM, Johnston RV. Arthrographic distension for adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder). <em>Cochrane Database Syst Rev</em> 2008;(1):CD007005."]}, "6.5": {"title": "Shoulder pain, suprascapular nerve block", "vignette": "An 81-year-old retired seamstress presents with 3 years of worsening right shoulder pain. The pain is deep in the shoulder and the outer arm, constant, and much worse at night; she sleeps sitting in a chair. She cannot lift her arm above shoulder height to brush her hair, and she struggles to dress. She has heart failure with an ejection fraction of 30%, chronic kidney disease stage 4 and takes apixaban for atrial fibrillation. Her orthopaedic surgeon has said she is not fit for a reverse shoulder replacement. Two glenohumeral steroid injections helped for about 6 weeks each. Paracetamol helps a little; tramadol made her confused and she had a fall. Her daughter asks if there is anything else that can help her sleep.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> elderly, chronic shoulder pain with night pain and loss of function, structural disease (glenohumeral arthritis and massive cuff tear), not fit for surgery, short-lived response to joint injections, medication intolerance (opioid confusion and falls), CKD (no NSAIDs), heart failure. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> apixaban (bleeding risk decision), CKD and heart failure (drug and steroid choices), the daughter's question (realistic goals: sleep and function, not cure).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> cuff tear arthropathy (glenohumeral osteoarthritis secondary to a massive chronic rotator cuff tear) of the right shoulder.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary glenohumeral osteoarthritis</li>\n<li>Massive rotator cuff tear without arthritis; subacromial impingement</li>\n<li>AC joint osteoarthritis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory arthritis (rheumatoid arthritis, polymyalgia rheumatica)</li>\n<li>Crystal arthropathy (Milwaukee shoulder: calcium hydroxyapatite)</li>\n<li>Avascular necrosis of the humeral head</li>\n<li>Septic arthritis (in the elderly, may be subtle)</li>\n<li>Cervical radiculopathy (C5); referred pain (cardiac, lung apex tumour)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated (she cannot lie flat easily); assess both shoulders from the front and behind.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> wasting of supraspinatus and infraspinatus, deltoid bulk, swelling or effusion (a fluid-filled \"fluid sign\" over the shoulder in Milwaukee shoulder), scars, posture.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer (biceps, brachioradialis, triceps); Neurotip and light touch over C4–C6 and the axillary nerve patch; MRC power of deltoid and cuff; cervical range (gently) and Spurling test.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active and passive range with a goniometer, compared with the left: pseudoparalysis (active elevation much less than passive) suggests a massive tear.</li>\n<li>Crepitus on movement; joint line tenderness posteriorly.</li>\n<li>Cuff strength: external rotation lag sign, hornblower sign (teres minor), drop arm, Jobe, lift-off or belly press.</li>\n<li>AC joint: cross-body adduction.</li>\n<li>Functional tasks: hand to mouth, hand to back of the head, hand to back.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>do the lag signs and say \"pseudoparalysis\". They show the cuff is irreparable, which is why a nerve block, not cuff repair, is the plan.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> true AP (Grashey), axillary and scapular Y views: superior migration of the humeral head (acromiohumeral distance under 7 mm), acetabularisation of the acromion, glenohumeral joint narrowing, osteophytes (Hamada classification of cuff tear arthropathy).</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> absent or retracted supraspinatus and infraspinatus, effusion, synovitis, bursal fluid; guides injections.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> only if surgery is reconsidered; shows fatty infiltration of the cuff muscles (Goutallier grading).</li>\n<li><strong>Joint aspiration</strong> if there is a large effusion: crystals (hydroxyapatite or CPPD), cell count, culture.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> renal function (CKD 4), FBC, ESR and CRP if inflammatory arthritis or infection is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic suprascapular nerve block:</strong> relief predicts benefit from pulsed RF.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong> (curvilinear in a large patient), MSK preset, depth 4–5 cm. Patient seated, leaning slightly forward, hand on the opposite shoulder.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> a systematic shoulder scan (biceps, subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, posterior glenohumeral joint, AC joint): cuff defects, effusion, joint surfaces.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the suprascapular nerve (posterior approach):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Probe in the coronal plane over the scapular spine, then moved cephalad and parallel to it, onto the supraspinous fossa.</li>\n<li>Identify the trapezius (superficial), the supraspinatus muscle (deep) and the bony floor of the fossa.</li>\n<li>Tilt the probe until the floor shows a dip between the suprascapular notch and the spinoglenoid notch: the suprascapular nerve and artery lie on the floor under the fascia of the supraspinatus.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Alternative (anterior approach, proximal):</strong> the nerve can be found in the supraclavicular fossa as it leaves the superior trunk of the brachial plexus, beneath the omohyoid.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth from skin to the floor of the fossa for needle planning; compare cuff muscle bulk with the left side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour Doppler shows the suprascapular artery running with the nerve on the floor of the fossa (the nerve lies next to it); at the notch the artery runs above the transverse scapular ligament, the nerve below.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> passive external rotation while scanning the infraspinatus and posterior joint; abduction while scanning the subacromial space (humeral head riding up against the acromion).</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> trapezius, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, scapular spine, supraspinous fossa, suprascapular notch, spinoglenoid notch, suprascapular nerve, suprascapular artery, transverse scapular ligament, acromion, humeral head.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Goals:</strong> sleep, comfort and independence in daily activities, agreed with her and her daughter.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> regular paracetamol (adjusted for weight and frailty); topical NSAIDs on the shoulder with caution (small systemic absorption) or topical capsaicin; avoid oral NSAIDs (CKD 4, heart failure); avoid tramadol (confusion, falls, renal accumulation); low-dose buprenorphine patch only if essential and monitored.</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> gentle range of motion, anterior deltoid strengthening programme, pendular exercises; occupational therapy aids (long-handled brush, dressing aids, pillow support at night).</li>\n<li><strong>Heat or ice;</strong> TENS.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided suprascapular nerve block in the supraspinous fossa (posterior approach)</strong> with local anaesthetic and steroid; if it gives good but short-lived relief, <strong>pulsed radiofrequency of the suprascapular nerve.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The suprascapular nerve supplies about 70% of the sensory innervation of the shoulder joint (glenohumeral and AC joints, subacromial bursa), plus the supraspinatus and infraspinatus.</li>\n<li>Suprascapular nerve blocks improve pain and function in chronic shoulder pain from degenerative disease and arthritis (Shanahan 2003; Chan and Peng 2011 review), and can work longer than a joint injection.</li>\n<li>The posterior fossa approach works even in people without a suprascapular notch (about 8%), keeps the drug in the fossa compartment, and uses the bone of the fossa floor as a backstop, which lowers the risk of pneumothorax.</li>\n<li>Other options: articular branch block of the axillary nerve with the SSN block for better relief; peripheral nerve stimulation of the SSN; glenohumeral injections (short-lived).</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; discuss apixaban. A suprascapular block is a low-to-intermediate bleeding risk superficial block in a compressible area in many guidelines; agree the plan with her prescriber (balance with stroke risk). Check allergies; plan a smaller steroid dose given heart failure (fluid retention) and the glucose rise.</li>\n<li>Seated, leaning forward on a pillow, hand on the opposite shoulder; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Probe in the coronal oblique plane over the supraspinous fossa; identify the trapezius, supraspinatus, fossa floor, suprascapular artery (colour Doppler) and nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 80 mm, in plane from medial to lateral (the acromion makes a lateral entry awkward); keep the needle tip in view.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the floor of the supraspinous fossa between the suprascapular notch and the spinoglenoid notch, next to the artery, under the supraspinatus fascia. Touch bone gently.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspirate</strong> (the artery is close), then inject in small aliquots watching the spread along the floor of the fossa under the muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 5–8 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine (or 0.5% ropivacaine) with 20–40 mg methylprednisolone or triamcinolone (consider the lower dose for heart failure).</li>\n<li><strong>For pulsed RF (later):</strong> a 22G RF cannula to the same point; sensory stimulation at 50 Hz reproduces paraesthesia in the shoulder; motor stimulation at 2 Hz shows supraspinatus and infraspinatus twitches; pulsed RF at 42°C for 2 × 120 seconds.</li>\n<li>Re-test her pain and range of motion after 20 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> if the needle passes through the notch or over the superior border of the scapula (rare with the fossa approach, where the needle stays on bone).</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> suprascapular artery puncture, intravascular injection, haematoma (higher on apixaban).</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> nerve injury; temporary weakness of shoulder abduction and external rotation (supraspinatus and infraspinatus) for the duration of the local anaesthetic; brachial plexus spread with the anterior approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Phrenic nerve block</strong> with the anterior (supraclavicular) approach, which matters in heart failure; another reason to prefer the posterior approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid:</strong> raised glucose, fluid retention (heart failure), flushing.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, vasovagal reaction, local anaesthetic toxicity.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> pain at rest and on movement; range of motion; observe for breathlessness or chest pain (pneumothorax); check the puncture site for a haematoma (apixaban).</li>\n<li><strong>Safety:</strong> an expected temporary weakness of the arm; use a sling if needed, fall prevention at home, help with dressing that evening.</li>\n<li><strong>At 2–6 weeks:</strong> night pain and sleep (her main goal), pain score, function (SPADI or Oxford Shoulder Score), daily activities, analgesic use.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> if the block gave good but short relief, pulsed RF (relief often 3–6 months, repeatable); if little relief, reconsider the diagnosis or add an axillary nerve articular branch block; restart apixaban as agreed.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Choosing the notch approach and forgetting pneumothorax risk; not using the artery as the landmark.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that the nerve supplies about 70% of the shoulder joint sensation, and not mentioning pulsed RF as the next step.</li>\n<li>Ignoring her comorbidities: no NSAIDs (CKD), care with steroid (heart failure), apixaban plan, opioid side effects.</li>\n<li>Using the anterior approach in a patient with heart failure (phrenic nerve risk).</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Bhatia A, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided peripheral nerve blockade in chronic pain management. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 25, suprascapular nerve block pp. 245–247.", "Shah JM, Pellis Z, Provenzano DA. Suprascapular nerve. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 4, pp. 53–59.", "Peng PWH, Cheng P. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part III: shoulder. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2011;36:592–605.", "Chan CW, Peng PWH. Suprascapular nerve block: a narrative review. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2011;36:358–373.", "Harmon D, Hearty C. Ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve block technique. <em>Pain Physician</em> 2007;10:743–746.", "Shanahan EM, et al. Suprascapular nerve block (using bupivacaine and methylprednisolone acetate) in chronic shoulder pain. <em>Ann Rheum Dis</em> 2003;62:400–406."]}, "7.1": {"title": "Thoracic pain, thoracic facet joint pain", "vignette": "A 58-year-old male accountant presents with an 18-month history of aching pain in the middle of his upper back, slightly more on the right, between the shoulder blades. It is worse after long hours at his desk, when he twists to look behind while reversing, and when he arches backwards; it is eased by lying flat and by heat. It does not go around the chest and is not linked to eating, breathing or exertion. He has hypertension and was found to have mild osteopenia two years ago. He has lost no weight and has no fever. A cardiologist did an exercise test, which was normal. Paracetamol, physiotherapy and massage help for a day or two. A thoracic X-ray reported \"mild degenerative changes in the mid-thoracic spine\".", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> chronic axial mid-back pain, paramedian, worse with sustained posture, rotation and extension, eased by lying down, no band-like radiation, no visceral pattern, normal cardiac test, degenerative X-ray. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> age and hypertension (cardiac or aortic pain), osteopenia (vertebral compression fracture), no radiation (makes thoracic radiculopathy and intercostal neuralgia less likely but not impossible).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> thoracic facet joint pain (spondylosis), T6–T8, right side more than left. Thoracic facet pain accounts for about a third to almost half of chronic thoracic spinal pain in controlled-block studies (Manchikanti 2004).</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Red flags first:</strong> osteoporotic vertebral compression fracture, metastasis or myeloma, infection (discitis), aortic aneurysm or dissection, cardiac pain, pancreatic or gastric disease, pleural disease</li>\n<li>Costovertebral or costotransverse joint pain</li>\n<li>Thoracic disc herniation (rare; may cause radiculopathy or myelopathy)</li>\n<li>Thoracic radiculopathy, intercostal neuralgia, post-herpetic neuralgia</li>\n<li>Myofascial pain (rhomboids, trapezius, erector spinae)</li>\n<li>Scheuermann's disease, DISH, ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Referred cervical facet pain (C7–T1)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (posture, kyphosis, scoliosis, range), seated (rotation with the pelvis fixed), prone (palpation), supine (abdomen).</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong> reflex hammer: upper and lower limb reflexes, abdominal reflexes, plantar responses and clonus (cord compression from a thoracic disc or tumour); Neurotip and cotton wool for a sensory level and thoracic dermatomes; MRC power of the lower limbs; gait.</p>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thoracic range: flexion, extension, rotation, side flexion with an inclinometer; pain on extension–rotation.</li>\n<li>Palpation: paramedian tenderness over the facet joints (about 2 cm lateral to the spinous processes), costotransverse joints, spinous process percussion (fracture, infection), rib springing.</li>\n<li>Myofascial trigger points; chest expansion (ankylosing spondylitis).</li>\n<li>Cervical spine screen; shoulder and scapula.</li>\n<li>General: abdominal examination, peripheral pulses, blood pressure in both arms.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>state the red flags and examine for them, including a sensory level and abdominal reflexes. Thoracic pain is the region where serious causes are most often missed.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> FBC, ESR, CRP, calcium, alkaline phosphatase, protein electrophoresis (myeloma), PSA if appropriate.</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray</strong> (done): alignment, wedge fractures, degenerative change.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI thoracic spine:</strong> to exclude fracture (bone oedema), malignancy, infection, disc herniation and cord compression; facet effusion or oedema supports facet pain.</li>\n<li><strong>CT</strong> for bony detail (facet arthropathy, DISH).</li>\n<li><strong>DEXA</strong> (osteopenia, risk of fracture).</li>\n<li><strong>Exclude non-spinal causes</strong> as clinically indicated (ECG and cardiac review done; chest X-ray; abdominal ultrasound or CT for aorta and pancreas).</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic thoracic medial branch blocks</strong> (comparative) or intra-articular facet blocks: the reference standard.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Curvilinear 2–6 MHz probe</strong> (linear in a slim patient), depth 5–7 cm. Patient prone with a pillow under the chest.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview and level counting:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Count ribs from T12 upwards (the 12th rib is short and floating) in a paramedian sagittal view lateral to the transverse processes, or count down from C7 and T1 (the T1 transverse process is wide and square, with the first rib).</li>\n<li><strong>Paramedian sagittal views,</strong> moving from lateral to medial: ribs (round shadows with pleura between them), then the transverse processes (square shadows, no pleura visible between), then the laminae and facet joints (a continuous \"sawtooth\" line of laminae and articular processes).</li>\n<li><strong>Transverse view:</strong> spinous process, lamina, facet joint, transverse process and costotransverse joint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> the T6–T7 and T7–T8 facet joints in the paramedian sagittal view (a gap between the inferior and superior articular processes), and the superolateral corner of the transverse processes (medial branch target at upper and lower thoracic levels).</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth from skin to the facet joint and transverse process; compare joint appearance with the left side.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> intercostal and dorsal branch vessels; look for the pleura (sliding) lateral to the transverse processes to stay away from it.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> pleural sliding with breathing lateral to the transverse processes (marks the danger zone); gentle thoracic rotation to watch the facet joint move.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> spinous process, lamina, inferior and superior articular processes, facet joint, transverse process, costotransverse joint, rib, pleura, erector spinae, multifidus, trapezius, rhomboids.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Exclude and treat red flags;</strong> bone health (calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate if indicated after DEXA).</li>\n<li><strong>Posture and ergonomics:</strong> workstation, regular breaks, mirrors to reduce twisting while driving.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise therapy:</strong> thoracic extension and rotation mobility, scapular and core strengthening.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> paracetamol, topical NSAIDs, short courses of oral NSAIDs (blood pressure caution).</li>\n<li><strong>Heat, TENS, manual therapy</strong> (short-term).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided diagnostic thoracic medial branch blocks</strong> (for the painful joints, the medial branches at the level and the level above), or <strong>thoracic facet joint injection</strong>; if comparative blocks are positive, <strong>radiofrequency neurotomy</strong> (usually under fluoroscopy).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anatomy matters:</strong> thoracic medial branches cross the superolateral corner of the transverse process at T1–T4 and T9–T10, but at T5–T8 they may run suspended in the intertransverse space above the transverse process (Chua and Bogduk 1995). Target the superolateral corner of the transverse process, accepting that mid-thoracic blocks are less reliable.</li>\n<li>US-guided thoracic facet joint injection is feasible: in a description of the technique, the needle reached the joint in most cases (Stulc 2011, fluoroscopy confirmed). US avoids radiation and shows the pleura.</li>\n<li>Evidence for thoracic interventions is more limited than for the lumbar and cervical spine.</li>\n<li>Alternatives: erector spinae plane block (short-term relief); treat costotransverse joint pain separately if found.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check anticoagulation and allergies; explain the pain diary.</li>\n<li>Prone with a pillow under the chest; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Count and mark the levels (ribs from T12 or from T1); confirm with a second method.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li><strong>Facet joint injection:</strong> paramedian sagittal view over the facet column; 22G 80–90 mm needle in plane from caudal to cranial into the joint gap between the inferior articular process (above) and the superior articular process (below); 0.5–1 mL local anaesthetic (and 10–20 mg triamcinolone if therapeutic).</li>\n<li><strong>Medial branch block:</strong> transverse or paramedian view of the transverse process; needle in plane from lateral to medial to the superolateral corner of the transverse process, on bone; 0.5 mL local anaesthetic per nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay on bone</strong> and medial to the costotransverse joint; never go lateral to the transverse process (pleura).</li>\n<li>Aspirate before injecting; small volumes.</li>\n<li>Record pain with provoking movements (rotation, extension) before and after.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> (needle lateral to the transverse process or between ribs): chest pain, breathlessness.</li>\n<li><strong>Spinal:</strong> dural puncture, intrathecal or epidural spread, spinal cord injury (if the needle passes medially through the interlaminar space).</li>\n<li><strong>Vascular:</strong> intercostal or segmental artery puncture, intravascular injection.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> spread to the intercostal nerve (chest wall numbness), paravertebral spread.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> infection, vasovagal reaction, increased pain; after RF: neuritis, dysaesthesia.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately:</strong> breathing, chest pain, oxygen saturation (pneumothorax); lower limb power and sensation; observe for 30 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-net:</strong> new breathlessness or chest pain means urgent review and a chest X-ray.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain diary</strong> for 6–8 hours; at least 50% (or 80%) relief with concordant duration on comparative blocks = positive.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> positive blocks: RF neurotomy; negative: reconsider costotransverse, myofascial, discogenic or visceral causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up:</strong> pain, function (ODI or NDI-style questionnaire), posture and exercise programme; bone health.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not listing red flags and visceral causes (aorta, heart, pancreas, fracture, tumour).</li>\n<li>Not counting levels reliably (ribs from T12, or from T1).</li>\n<li>Going lateral to the transverse process (pleura).</li>\n<li>Not knowing that mid-thoracic medial branches may not lie on bone (Chua and Bogduk).</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Moriggl B. Spine sonoanatomy for pain physicians. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 5, thoracic spine pp. 61–65.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Karmakar MK. <em>Atlas of Sonoanatomy for Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine</em> (thoracic paravertebral sonoanatomy chapter).", "Stulc SM, Hurdle MF, Pingree MJ, Brault JS, Porter CA. Ultrasound-guided thoracic facet injections: description of a technique. <em>J Ultrasound Med</em> 2011;30:357–362.", "Chua WH, Bogduk N. The surgical anatomy of thoracic facet denervation. <em>Acta Neurochir (Wien)</em> 1995;136:140–144.", "Manchikanti L, Boswell MV, Singh V, Pampati V. Prevalence of facet joint pain in chronic spinal pain of cervical, thoracic, and lumbar regions. <em>BMC Musculoskelet Disord</em> 2004;5:15."]}, "7.2": {"title": "Post-herpetic neuralgia, thoracic (T6)", "vignette": "A 76-year-old retired male teacher had shingles on the right side of his chest 5 months ago. The rash has healed, but he has a constant burning pain in a band from his spine around to the front of the right chest, below the nipple, with sudden stabbing pains. His shirt touching the skin is unbearable and he cannot lie on that side, so he sleeps poorly. The area also itches. He has type 2 diabetes, atrial fibrillation treated with apixaban, and mild kidney impairment. Last week he felt tightness across the chest when climbing stairs. Pregabalin made him dizzy and he fell once. His daughter says he has become low and stays at home.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> shingles with pain persisting more than 3 months after the rash, dermatomal band (T6), burning and stabbing pain, allodynia to clothing, itch, older age. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> chest tightness on exertion (angina must be excluded, not attributed to PHN), apixaban (anticoagulant plan before any block), pregabalin side effects with a fall and kidney impairment (dose and choice of drugs), low mood and social withdrawal (depression).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> post-herpetic neuralgia of the right T6 dermatome: neuropathic pain persisting at least 3 months after the herpes zoster rash. Risk factors here: older age, severe acute pain, diabetes.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential and coexisting diagnoses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Angina or other cardiac cause of chest pain (must be excluded)</li>\n<li>Thoracic radiculopathy (disc, spinal metastasis, vertebral fracture)</li>\n<li>Diabetic truncal radiculoneuropathy</li>\n<li>Intercostal neuralgia from other causes; rib fracture</li>\n<li>Visceral pain referred to the chest wall (gallbladder, lung, pleura)</li>\n<li>Recurrent or persistent zoster (zoster sine herpete)</li>\n<li>Depression and sleep disorder (coexisting)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> seated, chest and back exposed, the healthy left side examined first for comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection:</strong> scars, pigment changes and skin loss in the T6 band; rash (recurrence).</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools (map the area):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Allodynia:</strong> brush or cotton wool (dynamic mechanical allodynia); mark the border with a pen.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoaesthesia or hyperalgesia:</strong> Neurotip.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermal:</strong> cold and warm test tubes.</li>\n<li><strong>Vibration:</strong> 128 Hz tuning fork.</li>\n<li>Dermatomes above and below (T4 to T8) to see how many levels are involved.</li>\n<li>Lower limb power, reflexes and plantar responses (exclude thoracic myelopathy); peripheral neuropathy screen in the feet (diabetes).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpation of the thoracic spine and ribs (fracture, metastasis); costovertebral joints.</li>\n<li>Thoracic range of motion; rib springing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>General:</strong> heart and lungs; pulse (AF); a brief mood screen.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>draw the map of allodynia and sensory loss, compare with the other side, and count the levels involved.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>PHN is a clinical diagnosis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Exclude cardiac causes:</strong> ECG and referral for cardiac assessment (exertional chest tightness).</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods:</strong> renal function (for drug dosing), HbA1c, FBC, clotting status before an intervention.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI of the thoracic spine</strong> if there are atypical features (no rash history, motor signs, red flags).</li>\n<li>Questionnaires: DN4 or LANSS, pain NRS, sleep score, PHQ-9 and GAD-7.</li>\n<li>Anticoagulation plan with his cardiologist or GP.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz probe</strong>, depth 3–4 cm. Patient prone (or lateral, right side up).</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> paramedian sagittal scan from the 12th rib upwards; ribs are seen as rounded hyperechoic lines with acoustic shadows, with the pleura deep between them.</p>\n<p><strong>Level identification:</strong> <strong>count the ribs from the 12th rib upwards</strong> (or from the first rib downwards) to reach the 6th rib; confirm with a second count.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> at the <strong>angle of the rib</strong> (about 6–8 cm lateral to the spinous process), place the probe in the long axis between two ribs (or rotate slightly to show the space in an oblique plane): the three intercostal muscles (external, internal, innermost), the <strong>intercostal vessels and nerve</strong> in the plane between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles, below the lower border of the rib, and the <strong>pleura</strong> deep.</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> depth from skin to the pleura at the target level.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> the intercostal artery and vein below the rib (the vein is most cranial, then the artery, then the nerve: VAN).</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> <strong>pleural sliding</strong> with breathing (the \"gliding sign\"); this marks the pleura and later shows there is no pneumothorax.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> ribs (cortex and acoustic shadow), external, internal and innermost intercostal muscles, intercostal artery, vein and nerve, pleura, lung (A-lines), erector spinae muscle, transverse process.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Topical first (elderly, side effects):</strong> lidocaine 5% plaster (up to 12 hours a day) over the painful area; capsaicin 8% patch (single application, repeat after 3 months if helpful).</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic:</strong> amitriptyline or nortriptyline at low dose (care with AF, falls and anticholinergic effects); gabapentin or pregabalin at a low dose, adjusted for kidney function (he fell on pregabalin); duloxetine (helps mood). Tramadol as second line; avoid strong opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep and mood:</strong> treat depression, psychology input, activity plan.</li>\n<li>Skin care for itch; loose clothing; TENS.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention:</strong> recombinant zoster vaccine (Shingrix) in older adults to prevent zoster and PHN.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided intercostal nerve blocks at the angle of the rib at T5, T6 and T7</strong> (one level above and one below, for collateral innervation), with local anaesthetic and steroid, after an agreed plan for the apixaban.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evidence for interventions in established PHN is limited; nerve blocks give short- to medium-term relief and allow better sleep and rehabilitation (systematic review: Lin 2019).</li>\n<li>The block is placed at the angle of the rib, proximal to the origin of the lateral cutaneous branch (mid-axillary line), so the whole dermatome is covered.</li>\n<li>Intercostal blocks are low to intermediate bleeding risk procedures in the ASRA guidelines (Narouze 2018): discuss holding apixaban with the cardiologist, balancing stroke risk in AF.</li>\n<li>Alternatives: thoracic paravertebral or erector spinae plane block (several levels with one injection), pulsed RF of the dorsal root ganglion or intercostal nerve, spinal cord stimulation for refractory cases.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; anticoagulation plan agreed (apixaban held for the agreed time if needed); blood glucose (steroid); IV access, monitoring.</li>\n<li>Prone with a pillow under the abdomen, arms hanging to move the scapulae laterally; machine on the opposite side.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep, sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Count the ribs from the 12th rib; identify T5, T6 and T7 at the angle of the rib; colour Doppler; check pleural sliding.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22–25G 50 mm, in plane from caudal to cranial, aiming for the lower edge of the rib above.</li>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> the plane between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles, just below the rib; watch the pleura move away (displaced downwards) as the injectate spreads.</li>\n<li>Aspirate; inject <strong>3–5 mL per level</strong> of local anaesthetic (for example 0.25% bupivacaine) with steroid (for example 40 mg methylprednisolone split between levels). Watch the total dose for local anaesthetic toxicity (absorption is high from the intercostal space).</li>\n<li>Check pleural sliding after the block at each level; reassess pain and allodynia at 20–30 minutes.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> (the main risk; lower with US).</li>\n<li><strong>Local anaesthetic toxicity:</strong> the intercostal space has the fastest absorption of all blocks.</li>\n<li>Intravascular injection (intercostal vessels); haematoma (anticoagulant).</li>\n<li>Spread to the paravertebral or epidural space (hypotension, bilateral block); rarely intrathecal spread through a dural cuff (medial approach).</li>\n<li>Nerve injury, infection, increase in pain for a few days.</li>\n<li>Steroid: raised blood glucose (diabetes), flushing.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Safety:</strong> breathing, oxygen saturation, chest pain; <strong>pleural sliding on US</strong> or chest X-ray if pneumothorax is suspected; observe for local anaesthetic toxicity; blood pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Glucose:</strong> check after steroid (diabetes).</li>\n<li><strong>Anticoagulant:</strong> restart apixaban at the agreed time.</li>\n<li><strong>Response:</strong> pain score, allodynia map, sleep; if relief is good but short, consider repeating the block, or pulsed RF.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up:</strong> medication review (falls, kidney function), mood and activity, zoster vaccination, refer for cardiac review of the chest tightness.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not counting the ribs from the 12th rib, and not showing the pleura and pleural sliding.</li>\n<li>Not blocking the levels above and below.</li>\n<li>Not addressing the anticoagulant before the procedure.</li>\n<li>Missing the exertional chest tightness (angina must be excluded).</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Bhatia A, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided peripheral nerve blockade in chronic pain management (intercostal nerve block). In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 25, pp. 243–254 (intercostal nerve, pp. 248–250).", "Chiu YM, Gulati A. Intercostal nerve block. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 5, pp. 61–74.", "Johnson RW, Rice ASC. Postherpetic neuralgia. <em>N Engl J Med</em> 2014;371:1526–1533.", "Finnerup NB, Attal N, Haroutounian S, et al. Pharmacotherapy for neuropathic pain in adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Lancet Neurol</em> 2015;14:162–173.", "Lin CS, Lin YC, Lao HC, Chen CC. Interventional treatments for postherpetic neuralgia: a systematic review. <em>Pain Physician</em> 2019;22:209–228.", "Narouze S, Benzon HT, Provenzano D, et al. Interventional spine and pain procedures in patients on antiplatelet and anticoagulant medications (second edition). <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2018;43:225–262."]}, "8.1": {"title": "Lumbosacral radicular pain, caudal epidural", "vignette": "A 44-year-old female nurse presents with a 10-week history of right leg pain that started after lifting a patient. The pain runs from the buttock down the back of the thigh and calf to the outer border of the foot and the little toe; she rates it 8/10 and says the leg pain is worse than the back pain. Coughing and sitting make it worse. She has pins and needles along the outer foot. She has had no bladder, bowel or saddle symptoms. She has taken naproxen, a 2-week course of pregabalin that made her dizzy, and 6 weeks of physiotherapy, with only slight improvement. She has missed 8 weeks of work. She had a lumbar epidural for childbirth 10 years ago with an accidental dural puncture.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> acute lifting injury, dermatomal leg pain worse than back pain, S1 distribution (posterior calf, lateral foot, little toe), worse with cough and sitting, paraesthesia, 10 weeks of failed conservative care, no red flags. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> previous dural puncture (affects your choice of route), outer-foot paraesthesia (L5 or S1?), the sick leave (yellow flags).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> right S1 radiculopathy from a posterolateral L5–S1 disc herniation.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>L5 radiculopathy (L4–L5 disc or foraminal L5–S1 disc); dorsum of foot and great toe</li>\n<li>Lateral recess or foraminal stenosis</li>\n<li>Sacroiliac joint pain or facet pain with somatic referral (not below the knee, no neurological signs)</li>\n<li>Piriformis syndrome or deep gluteal syndrome</li>\n<li>Peripheral causes: peroneal neuropathy, lumbosacral plexopathy (diabetic amyotrophy)</li>\n<li>Red flags to exclude: cauda equina syndrome, tumour, infection (discitis, epidural abscess), fracture</li>\n<li>Hip pathology (if pain were in groin or thigh)</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (posture, gait, heel and toe walking, single-leg calf raises ×10 for S1 power), then supine, then prone.</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflex hammer: knee (L3–L4), ankle (S1), medial hamstring (L5); plantar responses and clonus.</li>\n<li>Neurotip and light touch in L4, L5 and S1 dermatomes; vibration with a 128 Hz tuning fork; saddle sensation if any doubt.</li>\n<li>MRC power: ankle dorsiflexion (L4), great toe extension and hip abduction (L5), plantarflexion and hip extension (S1).</li>\n<li>Neural tension: straight leg raise (positive between 30° and 70°), crossed SLR (specific for disc herniation), Bragard's sign, slump test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar range: flexion often limited and reproduces leg pain; list (sciatic scoliosis).</li>\n<li>Palpation; SIJ provocation tests and hip examination (FABER, FADIR, internal rotation) to exclude mimics.</li>\n<li>Piriformis tests (FAIR, Freiberg) if the buttock is tender.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>do single-leg calf raises. A tired S1 is often missed on the couch, and it is a two-second test.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>MRI lumbar spine:</strong> indicated because of 10 weeks of disabling pain despite conservative care and because an injection is planned. Look for the level, the side, root compression, and lateral recess or foraminal stenosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Urgent MRI</strong> if red flags appear (cauda equina symptoms, progressive weakness).</li>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> limited value; flexion–extension only if instability is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>Electrodiagnostics (NCS and EMG):</strong> if the diagnosis is uncertain or to exclude peripheral neuropathy; H-reflex for S1.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods</strong> only with red flags (FBC, ESR, CRP).</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome measures:</strong> NRS for leg and back pain separately, Oswestry Disability Index; screen yellow flags (STarT Back, fear avoidance).</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side of the table, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Linear 6–13 MHz</strong> probe for the sacral hiatus (curvilinear for a large patient and for the lumbar overview), depth 3–4 cm. Patient prone with a pillow under the pelvis.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview:</strong> paramedian sagittal scan of the lumbosacral junction (sacrum, L5–S1 gap, laminae), then transverse scans of the sacrum moving caudally.</p>\n<p><strong>Focus on the sacral hiatus:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Transverse view:</strong> two hyperechoic sacral cornua (an inverted \"U\" on each side, the \"frog eyes\"), the sacrococcygeal ligament as a bright band between them, and the dorsal surface of the sacral base below as a second bright line.</li>\n<li><strong>Longitudinal view:</strong> rotate 90° over the hiatus: the sacrococcygeal ligament sloping between the dorsal sacrum above and the base of the sacrum below, with the hypoechoic caudal canal between.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> hiatus width (between the cornua) and depth (ligament to the base of the canal); a very shallow or closed hiatus, part of the up to 10% anatomical variation of the sacrum, predicts a difficult or failed injection.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour Doppler of the canal and the ligament for vessels before needling; during injection, a single uniform colour flow cranially supports epidural spread.</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> inject a small volume of saline and watch turbulence move up the canal; or ask her to bear down gently (Valsalva) while watching the canal.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> sacral cornua, sacral hiatus, sacrococcygeal ligament, dorsal surface of the sacrum, base of the sacral canal (ventral sacrum), median sacral crest, coccyx, posterior superior iliac spines.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> most disc-related sciatica improves within 6–12 weeks; large herniations can resorb.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay active,</strong> avoid bed rest; graded return-to-work plan with occupational health (lifting restrictions).</li>\n<li><strong>Physiotherapy:</strong> directional preference exercises (McKenzie), neural mobilisation, core strengthening.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> NSAIDs (short course, with gastric protection if needed); neuropathic agents have weak evidence in sciatica (pregabalin was no better than placebo in a 2017 RCT); short opioid course only if essential; avoid long-term opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological support</strong> for yellow flags.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided caudal epidural steroid injection.</strong> For a single-level S1 radiculopathy, a transforaminal S1 injection under fluoroscopy is the most targeted alternative; the caudal route is a good choice for S1 because the L5–S1 level is close to the hiatus, and it avoids the dura (important with her previous accidental dural puncture).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blind caudal injections miss the epidural space in about 26% of cases even in experienced hands (Stitz 1999), and intravascular injection occurs in about 2.5–9%.</li>\n<li>US-guided needle placement succeeded in 100% of cases (Chen 2004) and gave similar short-term relief to fluoroscopy in an RCT (Park 2013). US cannot follow the needle inside the bony canal or reliably detect intravascular spread, so contrast fluoroscopy remains the gold standard when available.</li>\n<li>Epidural steroids give short-term (weeks to 3 months) relief of radicular pain; they help rehabilitation and return to work but do not reduce the rate of surgery in the long term.</li>\n<li>Later options: transforaminal epidural, pulsed RF of the dorsal root ganglion; surgical referral (microdiscectomy) for persistent disabling pain beyond 6–12 weeks or for progressive deficit.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; check anticoagulants, allergies (contrast, local anaesthetic) and glucose.</li>\n<li>Prone with a pillow under the pelvis; legs slightly internally rotated (toes in) to relax the gluteal muscles; machine opposite.</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine; protect the natal cleft), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li>Transverse view: sacral cornua and hiatus centred; rotate to the longitudinal view.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 50–90 mm spinal needle (Spinner uses 22–25G 90 mm), in plane from caudal to cranial in the longitudinal view, at a shallow angle.</li>\n<li>Pass through the sacrococcygeal ligament (a \"pop\") into the canal. Advance no more than about 1 cm beyond the ligament: the dural sac usually ends at S2 but can be lower, and the needle cannot be seen once inside the canal.</li>\n<li>Aspirate for blood or CSF (a negative aspiration does not exclude intravascular placement).</li>\n<li><strong>Test dose</strong> of 2–3 mL saline or local anaesthetic, watching turbulence spread cranially in the canal (and on colour Doppler); no swelling over the sacrum (subcutaneous injection).</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> for example triamcinolone 40–80 mg or methylprednisolone 80 mg (or dexamethasone 8–10 mg, non-particulate) diluted with saline and local anaesthetic to 10–20 mL. A 20 mL volume reaches S1 in about 100% and L5 in about 89%.</li>\n<li>If fluoroscopy is available: confirm with contrast (epidural \"Christmas tree\" spread, no vascular runoff).</li>\n<li>Monitor for 30 minutes: blood pressure, leg power, sensation.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Needle position:</strong> subcutaneous injection (failed block); intravascular injection; intraosseous injection; dural puncture with post-dural puncture headache or subarachnoid block (if the needle is advanced too far).</li>\n<li><strong>Neurological:</strong> transient leg numbness or weakness; increased radicular pain; very rarely nerve injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Infection:</strong> local, epidural abscess, meningitis (rare).</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding:</strong> epidural haematoma (rare; follow anticoagulation guidance).</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid-related:</strong> flushing, insomnia, raised glucose, transient hypertension, menstrual irregularity; HPA axis suppression for weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, raised epidural pressure with large fast volumes (headache, visual disturbance), allergy.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Before discharge:</strong> leg strength and sensation, ability to walk safely, no headache; no driving if the legs are numb.</li>\n<li><strong>Safety-net advice:</strong> new severe back pain, fever, progressive weakness or bladder or bowel change means urgent review (abscess, haematoma, cauda equina).</li>\n<li><strong>At 2–4 weeks:</strong> leg pain NRS (target at least 50% reduction), back pain, ODI, sleep, return to work, straight leg raise, neurological examination.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan:</strong> use the window for active rehabilitation; a second injection if there was partial benefit; refer for surgery if pain remains disabling or deficits progress.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Not separating leg pain from back pain in history and outcome measures.</li>\n<li>Not asking about cauda equina symptoms and not checking saddle sensation when indicated.</li>\n<li>Advancing the needle far into the canal: the dural sac may be close.</li>\n<li>Not admitting that US cannot exclude intravascular injection.</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Vydyanathan A, Narouze SN. Ultrasound-guided caudal epidural injections. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 13, pp. 145–149.", "Spinner DA. Spine. In: Spinner DA, Kirschner JS, Herrera JE, eds. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound Guided Musculoskeletal Injections</em>. Springer; 2014. Ch. 10, caudal epidural pp. 132–133.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Stitz MY, Sommer HM. Accuracy of blind versus fluoroscopically guided caudal epidural injection. <em>Spine</em> 1999;24:1371–1376.", "Chen CPC, et al. Ultrasound guidance in caudal epidural needle placement. <em>Anesthesiology</em> 2004;101:181–184.", "Yoon JS, et al. The feasibility of color Doppler ultrasonography for caudal epidural steroid injection. <em>Pain</em> 2005;118:210–214.", "Park Y, et al. Ultrasound-guided vs. fluoroscopy-guided caudal epidural steroid injection for the treatment of unilateral lower lumbar radicular pain: a prospective, randomized, single-blind clinical study. <em>Am J Phys Med Rehabil</em> 2013;92:575–586.", "Mathieson S, et al. Trial of pregabalin for acute and chronic sciatica. <em>N Engl J Med</em> 2017;376:1111–1120."]}, "9.1": {"title": "Lumbar facet pain", "vignette": "A 63-year-old retired bus driver presents with an 18-month history of low back pain across both sides of the lower back, worse on the right. The pain is worst after standing for a long time, walking downhill, and getting up from a chair; it eases when he sits or lies down. He is stiff for about 20 minutes in the morning. The pain spreads into both buttocks and sometimes to the back of the right thigh, but never below the knee. There is no numbness or weakness, and no bladder or bowel change. He had a coronary stent 2 years ago and takes aspirin. His BMI is 28. Twelve weeks of physiotherapy, paracetamol and a course of naproxen gave little benefit, and he stopped the naproxen because of indigestion.", "clues": "<strong>Clues to underline:</strong> age over 60, axial pain, worse with standing, extension and getting up, eased by sitting, morning stiffness under 30 minutes, referral to buttock and thigh but not below the knee, no neurological symptoms. <strong>Planted distractors:</strong> thigh referral (think radiculopathy), walking-related pain at his age (think neurogenic claudication), aspirin (do you stop it?).", "pe": "STD", "answers": ["<p><strong>Most likely:</strong> lumbar facet joint pain from facet arthropathy at the lower lumbar levels; referral to the buttock and thigh above the knee is typical somatic referred pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discogenic pain (worse sitting and bending forward)</li>\n<li>Sacroiliac joint pain (pain below L5, over the sulcus)</li>\n<li>Lumbar spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication</li>\n<li>Degenerative spondylolisthesis with instability</li>\n<li>Myofascial pain (quadratus lumborum, gluteal muscles)</li>\n<li>Hip osteoarthritis referring to the buttock and thigh</li>\n<li>Osteoporotic vertebral fracture</li>\n<li>Red flags at his age: malignancy (metastasis, myeloma), infection, abdominal aortic aneurysm</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> standing (posture, gait, heel and toe walking, range of motion), then supine (neurological and hip examination), then prone (palpation, SIJ, facet loading).</p>\n<p><strong>Neurological examination with tools:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflex hammer: knee (L3–L4) and ankle (S1) reflexes, plantar responses.</li>\n<li>Neurotip and light touch in L2–S1 dermatomes; monofilament and 128 Hz tuning fork at the feet (to exclude neuropathy).</li>\n<li>MRC power: hip flexion (L2), knee extension (L3), ankle dorsiflexion (L4), great toe extension (L5), plantarflexion (S1).</li>\n<li>Neural tension: straight leg raise, slump test, femoral stretch test (all should be negative).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Musculoskeletal examination</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar range: flexion (Schober test) and extension; pain on extension and on returning from flexion.</li>\n<li>Extension–rotation (Kemp) test; paraspinal tenderness over the facets.</li>\n<li>No single test is diagnostic. Revel's criteria (older age, relief lying down, no worse with cough, flexion, rising from flexion, hyperextension or extension–rotation) have limited predictive value.</li>\n<li>Sacroiliac provocation tests (distraction, compression, thigh thrust, Gaenslen, sacral thrust): 3 or more positive suggests SIJ pain.</li>\n<li>Hip: range (internal rotation), FABER and FADIR, to exclude hip OA.</li>\n<li>Abdomen: palpate for an aortic aneurysm; peripheral pulses (vascular claudication).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Examiner tip:</b>say that there is no reliable clinical test for facet pain. The diagnosis is made with controlled blocks.</div>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>X-ray:</strong> AP and lateral, with flexion–extension views if instability is suspected: facet arthropathy, disc height, spondylolisthesis, fractures.</li>\n<li><strong>MRI:</strong> to exclude other causes and red flags, and to assess stenosis; facet effusion and bone marrow oedema around the joint support active facet pain. Imaging changes are common in people without pain, so they cannot confirm the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>SPECT-CT:</strong> increased uptake in a facet joint may predict a better response to blocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Bloods</strong> only with red flags: FBC, ESR, CRP, PSA, protein electrophoresis.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic medial branch blocks:</strong> the reference standard. The 2020 multisociety consensus suggests at least 50% relief as the threshold; dual comparative blocks (short- and long-acting local anaesthetic) reduce false positives, which can occur in about 25–40% of single blocks.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>Set-up:</strong> machine on the opposite side of the table, screen in line of sight; patient and operator details entered; transducer cleaned; gloves on. <strong>Curvilinear 2–6 MHz probe</strong>, abdominal or spine preset, depth 6–8 cm, focus at the transverse process. Patient prone with a pillow under the abdomen to reduce the lordosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional overview and level counting:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Paramedian sagittal scan from the sacrum:</strong> the flat bright sacrum, the L5–S1 gap, then the laminae as a \"sawtooth\" upwards. Mark each level on the skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Paramedian sagittal transverse process view:</strong> the \"trident\" of transverse process shadows with psoas between them; count from the sacrum.</li>\n<li><strong>Transverse interlaminar view at each level:</strong> spinous process, lamina, facet joint, superior articular process (SAP), transverse process (TP), the posterior dura and anterior complex in the interlaminar window.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Focus:</strong> the junction of the SAP and the cephalad edge of the TP at L3, L4 and L5 (the medial branch targets), and the junction of the S1 SAP with the sacral ala (L5 dorsal ramus).</p>\n<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> skin-to-target depth for needle planning; compare both sides' facets (joint gap, effusion, hypertrophy).</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler:</strong> colour Doppler near the target and the intervertebral foramen (lumbar segmental arteries).</p>\n<p><strong>Dynamic test:</strong> gentle flexion of the trunk (or hips over the pillow) opens the interlaminar and facet windows; scan while the patient breathes to separate muscle from bone.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures to be able to name:</strong> spinous process, lamina, facet joint, SAP, inferior articular process, TP, intertransverse ligament, psoas, erector spinae and multifidus, ligamentum flavum–posterior dura complex, intrathecal space, anterior complex, sacrum, sacral ala, iliac crest.</p>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Education and reassurance;</strong> stay active.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise therapy:</strong> core stabilisation, flexion-biased programme, aerobic conditioning; weight management.</li>\n<li><strong>Medication:</strong> paracetamol (limited benefit); topical NSAIDs; oral NSAIDs only with gastroprotection and cardiac caution (he has a stent); duloxetine for chronic low back pain. Avoid long-term opioids.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological input:</strong> CBT within a multidisciplinary programme if pain is affecting function and mood.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjuncts with weak evidence:</strong> TENS, manual therapy, acupuncture.</li>\n</ul>", "<p><strong>US-guided diagnostic lumbar medial branch blocks</strong> at L3 and L4 medial branches and the L5 dorsal ramus (to cover the L4–L5 and L5–S1 joints), right side first or both sides; <strong>then radiofrequency ablation</strong> of the same nerves if the blocks are positive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each facet joint is supplied by the medial branch at its own level and the level above, so two nerves per joint are blocked.</li>\n<li>Medial branch blocks predict RF outcome better than intra-articular facet injections; intra-articular steroid has limited long-term benefit.</li>\n<li>US accuracy: Greher 2004 found 89% of needles at the target on CT; Shim 2006 found about 95% correct on fluoroscopy. Accuracy falls in obesity (Rauch 2009). US cannot detect intravascular spread, so fluoroscopy with contrast remains the reference standard, and RF ablation is normally done under fluoroscopy.</li>\n</ul>", "<ol>\n<li>Consent; explain the pain diary. Aspirin can continue for medial branch blocks (low bleeding risk, ASRA guidance).</li>\n<li>Prone with a pillow under the abdomen; machine opposite.</li>\n<li>Count and mark the levels with the sagittal scans (see Q4).</li>\n<li>Sterile prep (chlorhexidine), sterile probe cover and gel.</li>\n<li><strong>L3 and L4 medial branches:</strong> transverse view at the level; the target is the junction of the SAP with the cephalad edge of the TP. Use the cephalad edge: the bright line between transverse processes is the intertransverse ligament, and a needle that passes it heads for the foramen.</li>\n<li><strong>Needle:</strong> 22G 80–100 mm echogenic needle, in plane from lateral to medial, until the tip touches bone at the SAP–TP junction.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirm</strong> in the paramedian sagittal view that the tip sits on the cephalad edge of the TP, not between transverse processes.</li>\n<li><strong>L5 dorsal ramus:</strong> transverse view at L5–S1; out of plane, caudal to cephalad, to the junction of the S1 SAP and the sacral ala; confirm the tip has not passed into the L5–S1 foramen.</li>\n<li><strong>Injectate:</strong> 0.5 mL local anaesthetic per nerve (for example 2% lidocaine for the first block, 0.5% bupivacaine for the comparative block). No steroid for a diagnostic block. Use small volumes, which keeps the block specific.</li>\n<li>Watch for a small hypoechoic spread at the tip; no visible spread may mean intravascular injection.</li>\n<li>Record baseline pain, then pain during his usual provoking activities.</li>\n</ol>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>False results:</strong> intravascular uptake (false negative); spread to the foramen or a large volume (false positive); placebo response.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural:</strong> spread to the ventral ramus with temporary leg numbness or weakness (warn about walking and driving); nerve root injury if the needle enters the foramen.</li>\n<li><strong>Other:</strong> vasovagal reaction, bleeding or haematoma, infection, dural puncture with post-dural puncture headache (rare), local anaesthetic toxicity (rare with these volumes).</li>\n<li><strong>After RF ablation:</strong> neuritis and dysaesthesia, multifidus denervation, rarely burns.</li>\n</ul>", "<ul>\n<li><strong>Pain diary:</strong> pain scores every 30–60 minutes for 6–8 hours, while he does activities that normally hurt (standing, walking downhill, getting up). Relief should match the expected duration of the local anaesthetic.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold:</strong> at least 50% relief of his index pain (some use 80% with dual comparative blocks) counts as positive.</li>\n<li><strong>Neurological check</strong> before discharge; no driving if the leg is numb.</li>\n<li><strong>If positive:</strong> repeat a comparative block, or go to RF ablation (expected relief 6–12 months, can be repeated). <strong>If negative:</strong> reconsider SIJ or discogenic pain.</li>\n<li>Continue the exercise programme alongside.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"tip\"><b>Where marks are lost</b><ul>\n<li>Blocking only one nerve per joint. Each joint needs two: L4–L5 joint needs L3 and L4 medial branches; L5–S1 joint needs L4 medial branch and L5 dorsal ramus.</li>\n<li>Not counting levels systematically from the sacrum.</li>\n<li>Using steroid or large volumes in a diagnostic block.</li>\n<li>Not admitting the limit of US: it cannot exclude intravascular injection.</li>\n<li>Forgetting red flags in a 63-year-old (aneurysm, malignancy).</li>\n</ul></div>"], "refs": ["Irwin DM, Gofeld M. Ultrasound-guided lumbar facet nerve block and intra-articular injection. In: Narouze SN, ed. <em>Atlas of Ultrasound-Guided Procedures in Interventional Pain Management</em>, 2nd ed. Springer; 2018. Ch. 10, pp. 117–123.", "Greher M, Peng P. Lumbar medial branches and L5 dorsal ramus. In: Peng P, et al., eds. <em>Ultrasound for Interventional Pain Management</em>. Springer; 2020. Ch. 14, pp. 169–180.", "Narouze S, Peng PWH. Ultrasound-guided interventional procedures in pain medicine: a review of anatomy, sonoanatomy, and procedures. Part II: axial structures. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2010;35:386–396.", "Greher M, et al. Ultrasound-guided lumbar facet nerve block: accuracy of a new technique confirmed by computed tomography. <em>Anesthesiology</em> 2004;101:1195–1200.", "Shim JK, et al. Ultrasound-guided lumbar medial-branch block: a clinical study with fluoroscopy control. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2006;31:451–454.", "Rauch S, et al. Ultrasound-guided lumbar medial branch block in obese patients: a fluoroscopically confirmed clinical feasibility study. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2009;34:340–342.", "Cohen SP, et al. Consensus practice guidelines on interventions for lumbar facet joint pain from a multispecialty, international working group. <em>Reg Anesth Pain Med</em> 2020;45:424–467."]}};
